(window.COURSES=window.COURSES||{})["fp"]=JSON.parse("{\"id\":\"fp\",\"name\":\"File Processing\",\"short\":\"File Processing\",\"code\":\"CS308\",\"by\":\"Dr. M. AbdelFattah\",\"lectures\":[{\"n\":1,\"title\":\"Intro to File Organization and File I/O in C/C++\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Data structure vs file structure\",\"pts\":[\"Both involve <b>representation of data</b> + <b>operations for accessing data</b>.\",\"Difference: data structures deal with data in <b>main memory</b>; file structures deal with data in <b>secondary storage</b> (files).\"]},{\"h\":\"Course references and lecture outline\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Course references</b> (slide 3):<br>• <i>File Structures: An Object-Oriented Approach with C++</i> by Folk, Zoellick and Riccardi: the <b>main reference</b>.<br>• <i>File Organization and Processing</i> (Alan L. Tharp).<br>• <i>Database Management Systems</i> (Ramakrishnan, Gehrke).<br>• <i>Operating System Concepts</i> (Silberschatz, Galvin, Gagne).<br>• Lecture notes by Prof Lucia Moura.\",\"The lecture has two parts (slides 5, 6, 18):<br>• <b>Part (1)</b> Introduction to file management and organization:<br>(1) data processing<br>(2) file and computer storage<br>(3) file structures<br>(4) file organization<br>(5) file management<br>(6) file managers<br>• <b>Part (2)</b> Basic file processing operations, handling files in C/C++: physical vs logical file, opening and closing files, reading and writing, a sample program, full methods and a quiz.\"]},{\"h\":\"(1) Data processing\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Data processing</b> (slide 7):<br>• <b>Storage of data</b>: data is saved within files, and the files are saved in storage devices.<br>• <b>Organization of data</b>: how we organize files on primary and secondary storage.<br>• <b>Access of data</b>: sequential or direct access.<br>• <b>Processing of data</b>: the processing of data after storage, organization and access.\",\"Data is saved in files, and files are saved on primary (RAM) or secondary (hard disk) storage. Organization is either <b>sequential</b> or <b>direct</b> access.\"]},{\"h\":\"(2) Files and computer storage\",\"pts\":[\"<b>File</b> = a collection of data placed on <b>secondary, non-volatile</b> storage (hard disk, magnetic tape, optical media).\",\"Computer storage can be secondary storage or main memory.<br>File storage is slow because it has <b>many moving parts</b>.\",\"Figure (slide 9, Computer Storage):<br>• <b>Main memory (RAM)</b>: data is manipulated here; fast, small, expensive, volatile.<br>• <b>Secondary storage</b> (disk holding files such as A.mp3, first.java, mo.flv): data is stored here; slow, large, cheap, stable. Examples: disk, tape, CD-ROM.<br>• Data is transferred between the two. Callouts: \\\"Files will store here\\\" (secondary storage); \\\"Why is file storage so slow?\\\"; \\\"File storage systems have many moving parts\\\".\",\"Why files?<br>• They are the only suitable way to store large amounts of information (pictures, music, video).<br>• Operating systems are too large to fit fully in memory.<br>• Databases are everywhere.<br>• Backup and archiving.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-storage\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-storage\":2},\"figH\":{\"fp-storage\":\"Computer storage: main memory vs secondary storage\"}},{\"h\":\"(3) File structures\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Logical structure</b>: how programmers see the file (text, image, data file…).<br><b>Physical structure</b>: how it is stored on secondary storage.\"]},{\"h\":\"(4) File organization\",\"pts\":[\"Figure (slide 12, File Organization): a messy pile of books labelled <b>Unorganized</b> next to books neatly arranged on shelves labelled <b>Organized</b>.\",\"• <b>Logical organization methods</b>: heap, indexing, hashing.<br>• <b>Physical organization methods</b>: organizing tracks by <b>sectors</b> or by <b>blocks</b>.\",\"File organization methods must provide <b>fast access time</b> and <b>good space utilization</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-file-org\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-file-org\":\"File organization: from an unorganized pile to organized shelves\"}},{\"h\":\"(5) File management and (6) file managers\",\"pts\":[\"<b>File management</b> covers:<br>• preparing/formatting storage media<br>• allocating storage space and addressing<br>• managing free space.\",\"<b>File managers</b> (system software): the <b>OS</b> and the <b>DBMS</b>.\",\"Course objectives:<br>• Get information with <b>as few disk accesses as possible</b>.<br>• Group related information so a request needs <b>only one trip to the disk</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Physical file vs logical file\",\"table\":[[\"Physical file\",\"Logical file\"],[\"A collection of bytes stored on a disk\",\"A channel connecting the program to the physical file\"],[\"Has a physical name, e.g. A.txt\",\"Has a logical name: a variable in the program, e.g. infile, fp\"],[\"Used once, when opening the file\",\"Used as many times as needed: open, read, write, append, close\"]],\"pts\":[\"Figure (slide 20, Physical File vs. Logical File): a hard disk with 6 physical files (A.txt, b.txt, aa.txt, aa.mp3, a.doc, z.mp3). One C/C++ program deals with the physical file b.txt through the logical name <b>infile</b>; another program deals with aa.txt through its own logical name <b>infile</b>.\",\"Declaring the logical name (slide 22):<br>• In C: <code>FILE * outfile;</code><br>• In C++ the logical name is an object of the class <code>fstream</code>: <code>fstream outfile;</code><br>In both languages the logical name outfile is associated with the physical file myfile.txt at the time of <b>opening</b> the file.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-log-phys\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-log-phys\":1},\"figH\":{\"fp-log-phys\":\"Physical files on the disk and the logical names programs use\"}},{\"h\":\"Opening files\",\"pts\":[\"Opening makes a file ready for use: either open an existing file or create a new one. After opening you are positioned at the <b>beginning</b> of the file.\",\"Figure (slide 23, Opening Files in C): <code>FILE *outFile; outFile = fopen(\\\"A.txt\\\", \\\"w\\\");</code><br>Labels:<br>• <b>outFile</b> = logical name<br>• <b>fopen</b> = method to open file<br>• \\\"A.txt\\\" = physical file<br>• \\\"w\\\" = the mode<br>A bubble lists the modes: r (reading), w (writing), a (append), r+ w+ a+, rb wb ab, rb+ wb+ ab+.\",\"C++: <code>fstream outfile; outfile.open(\\\"myfile.txt\\\", ios::out);</code><br>The second argument is an <b>integer</b> indicating the mode; its value is set as a bitwise OR (operator <code>|</code>) of constants defined in the class <code>ios</code>.\",\"C modes:<br>• <b>\\\"r\\\"</b>: open existing for reading.<br>• <b>\\\"w\\\"</b>: create new or truncate existing for output.<br>• <b>\\\"a\\\"</b>: create new or append for output.<br>• <b>\\\"r+\\\"</b>: existing for input and output.<br>• <b>\\\"w+\\\"</b>: create/truncate for input and output.<br>• <b>\\\"a+\\\"</b>: create/append for input and output.<br>• Adding <b>b</b> (rb, wb, ab, r+b…) means <b>binary mode</b>.\",\"C++ flags:<br>• <code>ios::in</code> input<br>• <code>ios::out</code> output<br>• <code>ios::app</code> seek to end before each write<br>• <code>ios::ate</code> start positioned at end<br>• <code>ios::trunc</code> always create new (truncate)<br>• <code>ios::binary</code> binary mode.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-fopen-parts\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-fopen-parts\":1},\"figH\":{\"fp-fopen-parts\":\"The parts of an fopen call\"}},{\"h\":\"C mode ↔ C++ flags\",\"table\":[[\"C\",\"C++\"],[\"r\",\"in\"],[\"w\",\"out | trunc  (or out)\"],[\"a\",\"out | app\"],[\"r+\",\"out | in\"],[\"w+\",\"out | in | trunc\"],[\"a+\",\"out | in | app\"],[\"…b\",\"… | binary\"]],\"fig\":[\"fp-modes\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-modes\":\"Try each opening mode on a file, with its C++ flags\"}},{\"h\":\"Closing files\",\"pts\":[\"Closing frees the logical name for another physical file, \\\"like hanging up the telephone after a call\\\".\",\"Bytes are not sent one by one. They are collected in a <b>buffer</b> and sent as a <b>block</b>.\",\"If you do not close, the OS closes the file at the end of the program. If the program <b>terminates abnormally, data may be lost</b>.\",\"Closing in code (slide 28):<br>• In C: <code>fclose(outfile);</code><br>• In C++: <code>outfile.close();</code>\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-buffer\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-buffer\":\"The buffer: what closing a file does, and what a crash loses\"}},{\"h\":\"Reading and writing\",\"pts\":[\"Generic operations (slides 29, 33):<br>• <code>Read(Source_file, Destination_addr, Size)</code>: Source_file = the logical file name the program reads from; Destination_addr = the first address of the memory block where we want to read the data; Size = how much information is brought in from the file (byte count).<br>• <code>Write(Destination_file, Source_addr, Size)</code>: Destination_file = the logical file name where the data will be written; Source_addr = the first address of the memory block where the data to be written is stored; Size = the number of bytes to be written.\",\"• <code>fgetc(fp)</code> reads one character.<br>• <code>fgets(str, n, fp)</code> reads a string (‡ at most n-1 characters).<br>• <code>fscanf(fp, \\\"fmt\\\", vars)</code> reads formatted text.<br>• <code>fread(&amp;blk, size, count, fp)</code> reads a record/block.\",\"fread/fwrite arguments:<br>(1) address of the variable<br>(2) element size in bytes<br>(3) number of elements<br>(4) logical file name.\",\"Reading in C (slide 31, \\\"or in C++ using C streams\\\"): <code>char c; char a[100]; FILE * infile; infile = fopen(\\\"myfile.txt\\\",\\\"r\\\"); fread(&amp;c,1,1,infile); /* reads one character */ fread(a,1,10,infile); /* reads 10 characters */</code>. The slide comments c as \\\"a character\\\" and a as \\\"an array with 100 characters\\\". A <b>char</b> occupies 1 byte, so the element size is 1.\",\"Writing in C (slide 34): <code>char c; char a[100]; FILE * outfile; outfile = fopen(\\\"mynew.txt\\\",\\\"w\\\"); /* omitted initialization of c and a */ fwrite(&amp;c,1,1,outfile); fwrite(a,1,10,outfile);</code>. The slide lists the same four arguments as fread, calling the 1st one \\\"destination address (address of variable c)\\\". ‡ For fwrite the 1st argument is really the <b>source</b> address: the memory the data is written from.\",\"Reading in C++ (slide 32): <code>char c; char a[100]; fstream infile; infile.open(\\\"myfile.txt\\\",ios::in); infile &gt;&gt; c;</code> (reads one character), <code>infile.read(&amp;c,1);</code> (alternative way of reading one character), <code>infile.read(a,10);</code> (reads 10 bytes).<br>Writing in C++ (slide 35): <code>char c; char a[100]; fstream outfile; outfile.open(\\\"mynew.txt\\\",ios::out); /* omitted initialization of c and a */ outfile &lt;&lt; c; outfile.write(&amp;c,1); outfile.write(a,10);</code>.\",\"In C++ (slides 32, 35) the operator <code>&gt;&gt;</code> communicates the same information at a higher level: since c is a char variable, it is implicit that only 1 byte is transferred. C++ fstream also provides the <b>read</b> method, corresponding to fread in C (and <b>write</b>, corresponding to fwrite). Opening for writing: <code>outfile.open(\\\"mynew.txt\\\",ios::out);</code>.\",\"Detecting end of file (slide 36):<br>• In C, check whether <code>fread</code> returned <b>0</b>: <code>int i; i = fread(&amp;c,1,1,infile);</code> (attempted to read), then <code>if (i==0)</code> (true if file has ended) …<br>• In C++, check whether <code>infile.fail()</code> returns true: <code>infile &gt;&gt; c;</code> (attempted to read), then <code>if (infile.fail())</code> (true if file has ended) …<br>• Alternatively, check whether <code>infile.eof()</code> returns true.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-rw-args\",\"fp-read-sim\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-rw-args\":2},\"figH\":{\"fp-rw-args\":\"Generic Read and Write, and the arguments of fread and fwrite\",\"fp-read-sim\":\"Reading a file in C and C++, until the end of the file\"}},{\"h\":\"Methods used to read from files (C)\",\"pts\":[\"Slide 30 gives four methods to read from files, each with its shortcut name, usage, syntax and an example (table above).\"],\"table\":[[\"Method\",\"Shortcut\",\"Usage\",\"Syntax\",\"Example\"],[\"fgetc\",\"File Get Character\",\"Read one character from file\",\"<code>fgetc(file_pointer);</code>\",\"<code>ch = fgetc(fp);</code>\"],[\"fgets\",\"File Get String\",\"Read string from file\",\"<code>fgets(string_var, No_Of_Character, file_pointer);</code>\",\"<code>fgets(string, 80, fp);</code>\"],[\"fscanf\",\"File Scan Format\",\"Read formatted text from file\",\"<code>fscanf(file_pointer, \\\"conversion specifiers\\\", var(s)_name);</code>\",\"<code>result = fscanf(my_file, \\\"%c %d %x\\\", ch, num, hex);</code>\"],[\"fread\",\"File Read\",\"Read record from file\",\"<code>fread(&amp;block_var, block_size, No_of_block, file_pointer);</code>\",\"<code>fread(&amp;emp, sizeof(emp), 1, my_file);</code>\"]],\"fig\":[\"fp-read-methods\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-read-methods\":\"The four ways to read from a file in C\"}},{\"h\":\"Standard I/O logical names\",\"table\":[[\"Purpose\",\"Default meaning\",\"C\",\"C++\"],[\"Standard output\",\"Console/Screen\",\"stdout\",\"cout\"],[\"Standard input\",\"Keyboard\",\"stdin\",\"cin\"],[\"Standard error\",\"Console/Screen\",\"stderr\",\"cerr\"]],\"pts\":[\"These streams do not need to be opened or closed in the program (slide 37).\"]},{\"h\":\"Sample: read employee records (C)\",\"code\":\"struct employee { char name[20]; int sal; };\\nFILE *my_file; struct employee emp;\\nmy_file = fopen(\\\"c:\\\\\\\\emp.txt\\\", \\\"rb+\\\");\\nif (my_file == NULL) { printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); }\\nwhile (!feof(my_file)) {\\n    fread(&emp, sizeof(emp), 1, my_file);\\n    printf(\\\"The Emp Name: %s \\\\t\\\", emp.name);\\n    printf(\\\"The Emp Sal : %d\\\\n\\\", emp.sal);\\n}\\nfclose(my_file);\",\"pts\":[\"Task (slide 38): write a C program that displays the contents of a file on the screen. Algorithm: open the file for input → while there are characters/records, read one and write it to the screen → close the file.\",\"Slide callouts (slides 39-40):<br>• <code>struct employee</code>: create employee struct.<br>• <code>fopen(...)</code>: open the emp.txt file.<br>• <code>if(my_file == NULL)</code>: test if the file opened or not.<br>• <code>while(!feof(my_file))</code>: use a while loop to read from the file until the end of file.<br>• Loop body: read from the file using fread, print the output to the screen using printf, end the while loop with }.<br>• <code>fclose(my_file);</code>: close the logical file.\",\"‡ Tip: <code>while(!feof())</code> only becomes true <i>after</i> a read fails, so the last record may print twice. Checking <code>fread(...) == 1</code> is safer.<br>The slide's code (slides 39-40) also:<br>• prints <code>%f</code> for the int salary (use <code>%d</code>, as in the code above)<br>• has a stray <code>\\\"</code> after emp.sal<br>• writes <code>==</code> as <code>= =</code><br>• uses <code>void main()</code> and ends with <code>getch();</code>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-sample-run\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-sample-run\":\"Running the sample program: why while(!feof) prints the last record twice\"}},{\"h\":\"Full methods (slides 41-54)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Method (1) writeC()</b> (slides 41-42): ask the user to enter one character from the keyboard, then write it to the physical file alphabet.txt (<code>getche()</code>, then <code>fputc(ch,fp)</code>).\",\"<b>Method (2) readC()</b> (slides 43-44): read one character from alphabet.txt and print it to the screen (<code>ch = fgetc(fp); putchar(ch);</code>).\",\"<b>Method (3) writeS()</b> (slides 45-46): ask the user to enter strings from the keyboard until Enter is pressed on an empty line, writing each string to std.txt (<code>gets(string)</code>, stop when <code>strlen(string)==0</code>, else <code>fputs(string,my_file)</code>).\",\"<b>Method (4) readS()</b> (slides 47-48, named <code>read()</code> on the slide): read strings from std.txt and print them (<code>fgets(string,80,my_file)</code>, stop when <code>feof</code> is non-zero, else <code>puts(string)</code>).\",\"<b>Method (5) writeASCII()</b> (slides 49-50): write part of the ASCII table (characters 32 to 127) to ascii.txt using <code>fprintf</code>: a header line \\\"Letter Decimal Hexadecimal\\\", then each character as %c, %d and %x.\",\"<b>Method (6) readASCII()</b> (slides 51-53, named <code>read()</code> on the slide): read ascii.txt back: the header with <code>fgets</code> and <code>puts</code>, then each line with <code>fscanf(my_file,\\\"%c %d %x\\\",...)</code> until it returns <code>EOF</code>, printing each with printf.\",\"<b>Activity (1) / Quiz (1)</b> (slide 54): re-implement all the previous C program samples, from slide 38 to slide 53, as C++ programs.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-ascii\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-ascii\":5},\"figH\":{\"fp-ascii\":\"The ASCII table written by writeASCII and read by readASCII\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"File (definition)\",\"A collection of data placed on secondary (non-volatile) storage.\"],[\"Data structure vs file structure\",\"DS: data in main memory. FS: data in secondary storage.\"],[\"Logical vs physical structure\",\"Logical: how programmers see the file. Physical: how it is stored on disk.\"],[\"Logical organization methods\",\"Heap, indexing, hashing.\"],[\"Physical organization methods\",\"Organizing tracks by sectors or by blocks.\"],[\"File organization must provide…\",\"Fast access time and good space utilization.\"],[\"File managers\",\"OS and DBMS.\"],[\"Physical file\",\"Bytes on disk with a physical name (A.txt); used once, at open.\"],[\"Logical file\",\"A program variable (fp, infile) channel to the physical file; used many times.\"],[\"\\\"w+\\\" mode\",\"Create a new file or truncate an existing one, for input and output.\"],[\"\\\"a\\\" mode\",\"Create a new file or append to an existing one, for output.\"],[\"ios::app vs ios::ate\",\"app: seek to the end before EACH write. ate: initially positioned at the end.\"],[\"EOF detection in C / C++\",\"C: fread returns 0. C++: infile.fail() or infile.eof() returns true.\"],[\"Why close files?\",\"Frees the logical name, flushes the buffer; abnormal termination may lose data.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Mention the main differences between a physical file and a logical file.\",\"Physical file: a collection of bytes on disk with a physical name (A.txt), used once when opening. Logical file: a channel connecting the program to the physical file, with a logical name that is a program variable (infile, fp), used many times for open, read, write, append and close.\"],[\"Why do we need files?\",\"They are the only suitable way to store large amounts of information such as pictures, music and video; operating systems are too large to load fully into memory; databases are everywhere; and for backup and archiving.\"],[\"What does file management involve?\",\"Preparing/formatting storage media, allocating storage space and addressing, and managing free space.\"],[\"What happens if you do not close a file?\",\"The OS closes it at the end of execution, but if the program terminates abnormally, data in the buffer may be lost.\"],[\"Write a method writeC() that asks the user to enter one character from the keyboard, then writes it to the physical file alphabet.txt. (slides 41-42)\",\"void writeC(){ FILE *fp; fp = fopen(\\\"c:\\\\\\\\alphabet.txt\\\",\\\"w\\\"); if(fp==NULL){ printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); } char ch; ch = getche(); fputc(ch,fp); fclose(fp); } ‡ The slide writes Printf and Exit with capitals; C is case-sensitive, so they must be printf and exit.\"],[\"Write a method readS() which reads strings from the physical file std.txt and prints them to the screen. (slides 47-48)\",\"void read(){ FILE *my_file; int result; my_file=fopen(\\\"c:\\\\\\\\std.txt\\\",\\\"r\\\"); if(my_file==NULL){ printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); } char string[80]; while(1){ fgets(string,80,my_file); result = feof(my_file); if(result !=0) break; puts(string); } fclose(my_file); } (The slide names the method read() rather than readS().)\"],[\"Write a method readC() which reads one character from the physical file alphabet.txt and prints it to the screen. (slides 43-44)\",\"void readC(){ FILE *fp; fp = fopen(\\\"c:\\\\\\\\alphabet.txt\\\",\\\"r\\\"); if(fp==NULL){ printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); } char ch; ch = fgetc(fp); putchar(ch); fclose(fp); }\"],[\"Write a method writeS() that asks the user to enter strings from the keyboard until Enter is pressed (on an empty line), then writes them to the physical file std.txt. (slides 45-46)\",\"void writeS(){ FILE *my_file; my_file=fopen(\\\"c:\\\\\\\\std.txt\\\",\\\"w\\\"); if(my_file==NULL){ printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); } char string[80]; while(1){ gets(string); if(strlen(string)==0) break; fputs(string,my_file); } fclose(my_file); } ‡ gets() was removed from C11 because it cannot limit the input length; fgets(string,80,stdin) is the safe choice.\"],[\"Write a method writeASCII() which writes part of the ASCII table to the physical file ascii.txt using fprintf. (slides 49-50)\",\"void writeASCII(){ FILE *my_file; char ch; my_file=fopen(\\\"ascii.txt\\\",\\\"w\\\"); if(my_file==NULL){ printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); } fprintf(my_file,\\\"Letter\\\\t Decimal \\\\t Hexadecimal \\\\n\\\"); for(ch = 32; ch&lt;=127; ch++) fprintf(my_file, \\\"%c \\\\t%d \\\\t %x \\\\n\\\", ch, ch, ch); fclose(my_file); } ‡ The slide is missing the opening \\\" of the second format string. Also, if char is signed, ch&lt;=127 is always true and the loop never ends; use an int counter.\"],[\"Write a method readASCII() which reads the data from the physical file ascii.txt and prints it to the screen. (slides 51-53)\",\"void readASCII(){ FILE *my_file; char string[80]; int ch, num, hex; int result; my_file=fopen(\\\"ascii.txt\\\",\\\"r\\\"); if(my_file==NULL){ printf(\\\"Error in opening file\\\"); exit(1); } fgets(string,80,my_file); puts(string); while(1){ result = fscanf(my_file,\\\"%c %d %x\\\",ch,num,hex); if(result==EOF) break; printf(\\\"%c \\\\t%d \\\\t %x \\\\n\\\", ch, num, hex); } fclose(my_file); } ‡ The slide names it read() and passes ch, num, hex to fscanf; fscanf needs their addresses (&amp;ch, &amp;num, &amp;hex), and %c needs a char variable.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Files are managed by system software:\",\"o\":[[\"OS\",\"Only half the answer: the OS is one file manager, but the DBMS also manages files, so the full answer is \\\"Both a and b\\\" (L1, File managers).\"],[\"DBMS\",\"Only half the answer: the DBMS is one file manager, but the OS also manages files, so the full answer is \\\"Both a and b\\\" (L1, File managers).\"],[\"MIS\",\"MIS is an information system, not a file manager.\"],[\"Both a and b\",\"Correct. Files are managed by the OS and the DBMS.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"File organization methods must provide:\",\"o\":[[\"Fast access time and good space utilization\",\"Correct. The lecture states that file organization methods must provide fast access time and good space utilization, i.e. few disk accesses and little wasted space (L1, File organization).\"],[\"Preparing/formatting storage media\",\"Preparing/formatting storage media is listed under file MANAGEMENT, not under what an organization method must provide (fast access, good space use) (L1, File management).\"],[\"Allocating storage space and addressing\",\"Allocating storage space and addressing is a file MANAGEMENT task; organization methods must provide fast access time and good space utilization (L1).\"],[\"Managing free spaces\",\"Managing free space is the third file MANAGEMENT task; organization methods are judged by fast access time and good space utilization (L1).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm Model B\"},{\"q\":\"A logical file is:\",\"o\":[[\"A collection of bytes stored on a disk\",\"A collection of bytes stored on a disk is the definition of the PHYSICAL file; the logical file is the program's channel to it (L1, Physical vs logical file).\"],[\"A channel connecting the program to the physical file\",\"Correct. It is named by a program variable such as fp or infile.\"],[\"The file's name on disk, e.g. A.txt\",\"A name on disk like A.txt is the physical name of the physical file; the logical name is a program variable such as fp or infile (L1).\"],[\"A backup of the physical file\",\"The course never defines a logical file as a backup; it is the channel (a program variable) that connects the program to the physical file (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is the correct syntax for opening a file in C?\",\"o\":[[\"FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *mode)\",\"Correct. fopen takes the file name and the mode and returns FILE*.\"],[\"FILE *fopen(const char *filename)\",\"fopen needs two arguments: the file name AND the mode string (\\\"r\\\", \\\"w\\\", \\\"a\\\"…); without the mode the file cannot be opened (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"FILE *open(const char *filename, const char *mode)\",\"The C library function is fopen, not open; the course writes outFile = fopen(\\\"A.txt\\\", \\\"w\\\"), which returns a FILE* (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"FILE open(const char *filename)\",\"Wrong on every part: the function is fopen, it returns a pointer FILE*, and it takes both a file name and a mode (L1, Opening files).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"What does the mode \\\"w+\\\" do?\",\"o\":[[\"Creates a text file for writing, discards previous contents if any\",\"That is mode \\\"w\\\": create/truncate for OUTPUT only. The + adds input, so \\\"w+\\\" allows both reading and writing (L1, C modes).\"],[\"Creates a text file for update (read and write), discards previous contents if any\",\"Correct. w+ = create or truncate, for input and output.\"],[\"Creates a text file for writing, keeps previous contents\",\"Keeping previous contents while writing is \\\"a\\\" (append). \\\"w+\\\" truncates an existing file, discarding its contents (L1, C modes).\"],[\"Creates a text file for update, keeps previous contents\",\"Update while keeping contents is \\\"a+\\\" (create/append for input and output). \\\"w+\\\" truncates, so old contents are lost (L1, C modes).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"If a mode includes \\\"b\\\" after the initial letter (e.g. \\\"rb\\\"), it indicates:\",\"o\":[[\"Text file\",\"Text mode is the default when there is NO b; adding b (rb, wb, ab, r+b) switches to binary mode (L1, C modes).\"],[\"Big text file\",\"There is no \\\"big text\\\" mode; the only meaning of the letter b in a mode string is binary (L1, C modes).\"],[\"Binary file\",\"Correct. Adding b after the mode letter (rb, wb, ab, r+b…) opens the file in binary mode; the C++ equivalent is | ios::binary (L1, C modes).\"],[\"Blueprint text\",\"\\\"Blueprint\\\" is not a file mode; b in a mode string such as \\\"rb\\\" means binary mode (L1, C modes).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which C++ class creates a stream for BOTH input and output?\",\"o\":[[\"ofstream\",\"ofstream is an OUTPUT-only file stream (default ios::out), so it cannot be used for both input and output (L1, C++ file streams).\"],[\"ifstream\",\"ifstream is an INPUT-only file stream (default ios::in), so it cannot write; fstream handles both (L1, C++ file streams).\"],[\"iostream\",\"iostream is the header/class for console I/O (cin, cout); file streams for both input and output come from fstream (L1, C++ file streams).\"],[\"fstream\",\"Correct. fstream can be opened for both input and output, e.g. open(\\\"f.txt\\\", ios::in | ios::out); ifstream is input only and ofstream output only (L1, Opening files).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"The default opening mode of an ifstream is:\",\"o\":[[\"ios::in\",\"Correct. ifstream is the input file stream, so when no mode is given it opens with ios::in (read) (L1, C++ file streams).\"],[\"ios::out\",\"ios::out is the default of ofstream (output stream); an ifstream is for input, so its default is ios::in (L1).\"],[\"ios::app\",\"ios::app (seek to end before each write) is an output flag you must request explicitly; it is no stream's default, and ifstream defaults to ios::in (L1).\"],[\"ios::trunc\",\"ios::trunc (always create a new, empty file) is an output flag; it makes no sense for reading, and ifstream's default is ios::in (L1).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT used to seek the file pointer?\",\"o\":[[\"ios::set\",\"Correct. The origins are ios::beg, ios::cur and ios::end. ios::set does not exist.\"],[\"ios::end\",\"ios::end is a valid seek origin: the offset is counted from the end of the file. The non-existent one is ios::set (L1, Summary MCQ).\"],[\"ios::cur\",\"ios::cur is a valid seek origin: the offset is counted from the current position. The invalid one is ios::set (L1, Summary MCQ).\"],[\"ios::beg\",\"ios::beg is a valid seek origin: the offset is counted from the start of the file. ios::set does not exist (L1, Summary MCQ).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"How do you detect end of file in C++ after an attempted read?\",\"o\":[[\"fread returns 0\",\"fread returning 0 is how end of file is detected in C, not C++; in C++ infile.fail() or infile.eof() returns true (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"infile.fail() returns true (or infile.eof())\",\"Correct. The lecture says that in C++ end of file is detected when infile.fail() returns true, or infile.eof() returns true; in C, fread returns 0 (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"infile.close() returns false\",\"close() just releases the logical name and flushes the buffer; it does not report end of file. Use infile.fail() or infile.eof() (L1).\"],[\"cout prints EOF\",\"cout only writes to the screen; it does not detect anything. EOF in C++ is detected with infile.fail() or infile.eof() (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The ___ function reads at most one less than the number of characters specified by size.\",\"o\":[[\"fget()\",\"fget() is not a C library function; the string-reading function is fgets(str, n, fp), and fgetc reads one character (L1, Reading).\"],[\"fgets()\",\"Correct. fgets(str, n, fp) reads up to n-1 characters and adds '\\\\0'.\"],[\"fput()\",\"fput() is not a standard function, and anything named put writes rather than reads; the reader is fgets() (L1).\"],[\"fputs()\",\"fputs() WRITES a string to a file; the question asks about reading at most n−1 characters, which is fgets() (L1).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Summary MCQ\"},{\"q\":\"In C, the logical name for the standard error device is:\",\"o\":[[\"stdout\",\"stdout is the C logical name for standard output (the screen), not standard error (L1, Standard I/O names).\"],[\"stdin\",\"stdin is the C logical name for standard input (the keyboard), not standard error (L1, Standard I/O names).\"],[\"stderr\",\"Correct. In the standard I/O table, standard error (console/screen) is stderr in C and cerr in C++ (L1, Standard I/O names).\"],[\"cerr\",\"cerr is standard error, but it is the C++ logical name; the question asks for C, which is stderr (L1, Standard I/O names).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"EOF is an integer type defined in stdio. hand has a value ____________\",\"o\":[[\"NULL\",\"NULL is a null POINTER: what fopen returns when the file cannot be opened (if (my_file == NULL)), not the integer EOF (L1).\"],[\"0\",\"0 is what fread returns at end of file (zero elements read); it is a count, not the EOF constant (L1).\"],[\"– 1\",\"Correct. EOF is an integer constant defined in stdio.h with the value −1. The L1 readASCII() loop does result = fscanf(...) and stops when result == EOF; fgetc and fscanf return EOF (−1) at end of file or on error. (The lectures use EOF in code but do not print its value; −1 is the C standard value.)\"],[\"1\",\"1 is a normal successful count (e.g. fread read one record); it does not mean end of file (L1).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The Structures of file are .....\",\"o\":[[\"Physical structure\",\"Physical structure alone is only half: the logical structure is the other one (L1, File structures).\"],[\"Both A and B\",\"Correct. L1 \\\"(3) File structures\\\": a file has a LOGICAL structure (how programmers see it: text, image, data file…) and a PHYSICAL structure (how it is stored on secondary storage). \\\"Both A and B\\\" is mislabelled on the quiz: it means physical (a) and logical (d).\"],[\"Stream structure\",\"Stream is one of the L3 ways of organizing data inside a file (stream, field, record), not one of the file's two structures (L3).\"],[\"Logical structures\",\"Logical structure alone is only half: the physical structure is the other one (L1, File structures).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"What is the function of fputs()?\",\"o\":[[\"write a line to a file\",\"Correct. L1 C file functions: fputs(str, fp) writes a string (a line) to a file: f = file, put = write, s = string.\"],[\"read a character from a file\",\"Reading one character is fgetc(fp) (L1).\"],[\"read a line from a file\",\"Reading a line is fgets(str, n, fp), the reading partner of fputs (L1).\"],[\"write a character to a file\",\"Writing one character is fputc(ch, fp) (L1).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which function is used to reposition the file pointer?\",\"o\":[[\"changep()\",\"changep() is not a C or C++ file function (L1).\"],[\"moveg()\",\"moveg() does not exist; it only imitates the \\\"g\\\" of seekg (L1).\"],[\"go_p()\",\"go_p() does not exist (L1).\"],[\"seekg()\",\"Correct. L1 C++ file I/O: seekg() (\\\"seek get\\\") moves the read file pointer, e.g. infile.seekg(0, infile.end) jumps to the end and tellg() then gives the size. Its partner for the write pointer is seekp(); the C equivalent is fseek().\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which one of the following is correct syntax for opening a file.\",\"o\":[[\"FILE *fopen(const *filename, const char *mode)\",\"Correct. L1: in C a file is opened with fopen, which takes the file name and the mode (\\\"r\\\", \\\"w\\\", \\\"a\\\", \\\"rb\\\", \\\"wb\\\"…) and returns a FILE * pointer (NULL on failure), as in my_file = fopen(\\\"ascii.txt\\\",\\\"r\\\"). The real prototype is FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *mode); the quiz drops \\\"char\\\" from the first argument, but (a) is the only option with the right name, both arguments and the right return type.\"],[\"FILE *fopen(const *filename)\",\"No MODE: fopen always needs one so it knows whether to read, write or append (L1).\"],[\"FILE *open(const *filename, const char *mode)\",\"Wrong name: the C library function is fopen; open is the C++ stream method (infile.open(...)) or a low-level system call, neither returning FILE * (L1).\"],[\"FILE open(const*filename)\",\"Wrong name, no mode, and it returns FILE by value instead of a FILE * pointer (L1).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is not used as a file opening mode?\",\"o\":[[\"ios::in\",\"ios::in opens the file for input (reading): an opening mode (L1).\"],[\"ios::trunc\",\"Correct. Strictly all four are flags you can pass when opening a C++ stream (L1 lists ios::in, out, app, ate, trunc, binary). The question comes from a well-known C++ MCQ bank whose answer is ios::trunc: it does not decide HOW you access the file (read, write, binary, position); it only truncates an existing file to zero length on opening (\\\"always create new\\\").\"],[\"ios::binary\",\"ios::binary opens the file in binary instead of text mode: an opening mode (L1).\"],[\"ios::ate\",\"ios::ate opens the file positioned at the end: an opening mode (L1).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"<code>log.txt</code> already exists and holds 500 bytes. A program runs <code>fp = fopen(\\\"log.txt\\\", \\\"a\\\");</code> and writes 20 bytes. What does the file contain afterwards?\",\"o\":[[\"The original 500 bytes followed by the new 20 bytes (520 bytes)\",\"Correct. \\\"a\\\" opens an existing file (or creates a new one) for output and appends at the end.\"],[\"Only the new 20 bytes\",\"That is what \\\"w\\\" does: it truncates the existing file before writing.\"],[\"The first 20 bytes are overwritten; the file stays 500 bytes\",\"That would be \\\"r+\\\" with writing at the start. \\\"a\\\" never overwrites existing data.\"],[\"Nothing changes, because \\\"a\\\" opens the file for reading only\",\"\\\"a\\\" is an output mode (create or append), so the 20 bytes are written at the end. Read-only access is mode \\\"r\\\" (L1, C modes).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which C++ flag combination is equivalent to the C mode <code>\\\"r+\\\"</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"ios::in\",\"ios::in alone is input only, the equivalent of \\\"r\\\". \\\"r+\\\" needs both input and output: ios::out | ios::in (L1, C mode ↔ C++ flags).\"],[\"ios::out | ios::in\",\"Correct. \\\"r+\\\" opens an existing file for input and output.\"],[\"ios::out | ios::in | ios::trunc\",\"Adding trunc gives \\\"w+\\\", which destroys the existing contents.\"],[\"ios::out | ios::app\",\"ios::out | ios::app is the C++ form of \\\"a\\\" (output only, appending), with no input; \\\"r+\\\" is ios::out | ios::in (L1, C mode ↔ C++ flags).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"You must open an EXISTING binary file of employee records so you can both read records and overwrite some of them, without destroying the current contents. Which <code>fopen</code> mode fits?\",\"o\":[[\"\\\"wb+\\\"\",\"w+ creates or truncates the file, so all existing records would be lost.\"],[\"\\\"ab\\\"\",\"\\\"a\\\" is output only, and every write goes to the end, so you cannot read or overwrite records.\"],[\"\\\"rb+\\\"\",\"Correct. r+ opens an existing file for input and output, and b makes it binary. The slide sample uses this mode.\"],[\"\\\"rb\\\"\",\"\\\"r\\\" is input only, so you could not overwrite records.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the call <code>fread(&amp;blk, sizeof(blk), 5, fp);</code> what does the argument <code>5</code> mean?\",\"o\":[[\"The size of each element in bytes\",\"The element size is the 2nd argument, sizeof(blk).\"],[\"The byte offset to start reading from\",\"fread has no offset argument. Positioning is done with fseek.\"],[\"The logical file name\",\"The logical file name is the 4th argument, fp.\"],[\"The number of elements to read\",\"Correct. The arguments are: address of the variable, element size, number of elements, logical file name.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In C++, the flag <code>ios::ate</code> moves the file pointer to the end of the file before EACH write operation.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That describes ios::app. ios::ate only positions you at the end once, when the file is opened.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. ios::ate = initially positioned at the end; ios::app = seek to the end before each write.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Given <code>fstream outfile; outfile.open(\\\"myfile.txt\\\", ios::out);</code>, which statement is correct?\",\"o\":[[\"<code>outfile</code> is the logical name and <code>\\\"myfile.txt\\\"</code> is the physical name\",\"Correct. The logical file is the program variable (the channel); the physical file is the name on disk.\"],[\"<code>\\\"myfile.txt\\\"</code> is the logical name and <code>outfile</code> is the physical name\",\"Reversed. The physical name is the name stored on disk.\"],[\"Both names are physical names\",\"outfile is a program variable, so it is a logical name.\"],[\"<code>ios::out</code> is the logical name\",\"ios::out is the opening mode (output), not a name; the logical name is outfile and the physical name is \\\"myfile.txt\\\" (L1, Opening files).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a PHYSICAL file organization method?\",\"o\":[[\"Heap\",\"Heap is a LOGICAL organization method (with indexing and hashing); physical methods organize tracks by sectors or by blocks (L1).\"],[\"Organizing tracks by sectors\",\"Correct. Physical methods organize tracks by sectors or by blocks.\"],[\"Indexing\",\"Indexing is a LOGICAL organization method; physical organization is about tracks organized by sectors or blocks (L1).\"],[\"Hashing\",\"Hashing is a LOGICAL organization method (with heap and indexing); physical methods organize tracks by sectors or blocks (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A file holds exactly 3 employee records. This loop runs over it:<pre>while (!feof(fp)) {\\n    fread(&amp;emp, sizeof(emp), 1, fp);\\n    printf(\\\"%s %d\\\\n\\\", emp.name, emp.sal);\\n}</pre>What problem can happen?\",\"o\":[[\"The first record is skipped\",\"The first fread reads record 1 normally; nothing is skipped.\"],[\"The loop never ends\",\"feof does become true once a read hits the end, so the loop ends.\"],[\"The last record may be printed twice\",\"Correct. feof only becomes true AFTER a read fails, so one extra iteration prints the old contents of emp again. Checking fread(...) == 1 is safer.\"],[\"fread returns 1 at end of file\",\"At end of file fread returns 0 (the number of elements read), not 1. That 0 is the reliable EOF check in C (L1, Reading and writing).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"When a program writes to a file, each byte is sent to the disk individually at the moment it is written.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The slides say bytes are not sent one by one.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Bytes are collected in a buffer and sent as a block, which is why an abnormal termination before closing may lose data.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In C++, which logical name is connected by default to the keyboard?\",\"o\":[[\"cout\",\"cout is C++ standard output, connected to the console/screen, not the keyboard (L1, Standard I/O names).\"],[\"cerr\",\"cerr is C++ standard error, also connected to the console/screen; the keyboard is standard input, cin (L1, Standard I/O names).\"],[\"stdin\",\"stdin is the C name for standard input, not the C++ one.\"],[\"cin\",\"Correct. Standard input (keyboard) is stdin in C and cin in C++.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"……… deal with data stored in secondary storage (files).\",\"o\":[[\"Data structures\",\"Data structures deal with data in main memory, not in secondary storage (L1, Data structure vs file structure).\"],[\"File managers\",\"File managers (the OS and the DBMS) are system software that manage files; the structures for data in secondary storage are file structures (L1).\"],[\"File structures\",\"Correct. File structures deal with data in secondary storage, data structures with data in main memory (L1, Data structure vs file structure).\"],[\"Main memory\",\"Main memory is where data structures keep their data; it is a storage level, not a kind of structure (L1).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Data structures and file structures both involve a representation of data and operations for accessing data.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Both have these two parts; they differ only in where the data lives: main memory vs secondary storage (L1, Data structure vs file structure).\"],[\"False\",\"They share both parts; the only difference is main memory (data structures) vs secondary storage (file structures) (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A file is a collection of data placed on ………\",\"o\":[[\"Main memory (RAM)\",\"RAM is volatile and is where data is manipulated; a file is kept on secondary, non-volatile storage (L1, Files and computer storage).\"],[\"Secondary, non-volatile storage\",\"Correct. A file is a collection of data placed on secondary, non-volatile storage such as a hard disk, tape or optical media (L1, Files and computer storage).\"],[\"A buffer\",\"A buffer is a memory area where bytes are collected before being sent as a block; it is not where a file is kept (L1, Closing files).\"],[\"A logical file\",\"A logical file is a channel from the program to the physical file, not the storage that holds the data (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's Computer Storage figure, secondary storage is described as ………\",\"o\":[[\"Fast, small, expensive, volatile\",\"These are the properties of main memory (RAM), where data is manipulated (L1, Files and computer storage).\"],[\"Fast, large, cheap, volatile\",\"Secondary storage is slow and stable, not fast and volatile (L1).\"],[\"Slow, small, expensive, stable\",\"Secondary storage is large and cheap; small and expensive describe main memory (L1).\"],[\"Slow, large, cheap, stable\",\"Correct. Data is stored on secondary storage, which is slow, large, cheap and stable (L1, Files and computer storage).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"……… is how programmers see the file (text, image, data file…).\",\"o\":[[\"Physical structure\",\"The physical structure is how the file is actually stored on secondary storage (L1, File structures).\"],[\"Logical structure\",\"Correct. The logical structure is the programmer's view of the file; the physical structure is how it is stored (L1, File structures).\"],[\"File organization\",\"File organization is about the methods (heap, indexing, hashing, sectors, blocks) used to arrange files (L1).\"],[\"File management\",\"File management is formatting media, allocating space and managing free space (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Hashing is an example of a ……… method.\",\"o\":[[\"Logical organization\",\"Correct. The logical organization methods are heap, indexing and hashing (L1, File organization).\"],[\"Physical organization\",\"Physical organization methods organize tracks by sectors or by blocks (L1).\"],[\"File management\",\"File management covers formatting media, allocating space and addressing, and managing free space (L1).\"],[\"Data access\",\"Data access in the lecture is either sequential or direct; hashing is listed as an organization method (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT one of the file management tasks?\",\"o\":[[\"Preparing/formatting storage media\",\"This is a file management task (L1, File management).\"],[\"Allocating storage space and addressing\",\"This is a file management task (L1, File management).\"],[\"Managing free space\",\"This is a file management task (L1, File management).\"],[\"Providing fast access time and good space utilization\",\"Correct. This is what file ORGANIZATION methods must provide, not a file management task (L1, File organization).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: The physical name of a file (e.g. A.txt) is used every time the program reads from or writes to the file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The physical name is used only once, when opening the file; reads and writes use the logical name (L1, Physical file vs logical file).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The physical name is used once, at opening; after that the program uses the logical name as many times as needed (L1, Physical file vs logical file).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In both C and C++, the logical name is associated with the physical file at the time of ………\",\"o\":[[\"Reading the file\",\"Reading uses a logical name that is already linked to the physical file (L1).\"],[\"Opening the file\",\"Correct. The logical name (e.g. outfile) is associated with the physical file (myfile.txt) when the file is opened (L1, Physical file vs logical file).\"],[\"Closing the file\",\"Closing does the opposite: it frees the logical name for another physical file (L1, Closing files).\"],[\"Declaring the variable\",\"Declaring (FILE *outfile; or fstream outfile;) only creates the logical name; it is not linked to any file yet (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In C++, the logical name of a file is an object of the class ………\",\"o\":[[\"FILE\",\"FILE is the C type used for the logical name (FILE * outfile;), not the C++ class (L1).\"],[\"ios\",\"ios is the class whose constants (ios::in, ios::out…) make up the open mode (L1).\"],[\"fstream\",\"Correct. In C++ the logical name is an object of the class fstream: fstream outfile; (L1, Physical file vs logical file).\"],[\"stdout\",\"stdout is the C logical name of standard output, not a class (L1, Standard I/O).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the statement <code>fp = fopen(\\\"data.txt\\\", \\\"a\\\");</code> the argument <code>\\\"a\\\"</code> is the ………\",\"o\":[[\"Logical name\",\"The logical name is fp, the variable that receives the result of fopen (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"Physical file\",\"The physical file is \\\"data.txt\\\" (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"Method to open the file\",\"The method is fopen (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"Mode\",\"Correct. The second argument is the mode; \\\"a\\\" creates a new file or appends to an existing one, for output (L1, Opening files).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"<code>report.txt</code> already holds 300 bytes. A program runs <code>fp = fopen(\\\"report.txt\\\", \\\"w\\\");</code>, writes 50 bytes and closes the file. How many bytes does the file hold now?\",\"o\":[[\"350\",\"350 = 300 + 50 would happen with mode \\\"a\\\" (append); \\\"w\\\" truncates the old contents (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"50\",\"Correct. \\\"w\\\" truncates the existing file, so only the 50 new bytes remain (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"300\",\"300 would mean the old size is kept and the first 50 bytes overwritten, as with \\\"r+\\\"; \\\"w\\\" truncates (L1).\"],[\"0\",\"The file is truncated first, but the 50 bytes written afterwards stay in it (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which C++ flag combination is equivalent to the C mode <code>\\\"a+\\\"</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"ios::out | ios::app\",\"out | app is the C mode \\\"a\\\" (append, output only) (L1, C mode ↔ C++ flags).\"],[\"ios::out | ios::in | ios::trunc\",\"out | in | trunc is the C mode \\\"w+\\\" (L1).\"],[\"ios::out | ios::in | ios::app\",\"Correct. \\\"a+\\\" = out | in | app: create/append, for input and output (L1, C mode ↔ C++ flags).\"],[\"ios::in\",\"ios::in alone is the C mode \\\"r\\\" (L1).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The C mode <code>\\\"wb\\\"</code> corresponds in C++ to ………\",\"o\":[[\"ios::out | ios::trunc | ios::binary\",\"Correct. \\\"w\\\" = out | trunc, and adding b adds | binary (L1, C mode ↔ C++ flags).\"],[\"ios::in | ios::binary\",\"in | binary is \\\"rb\\\" (L1).\"],[\"ios::out | ios::app | ios::binary\",\"out | app | binary is \\\"ab\\\" (L1).\"],[\"ios::out | ios::in | ios::binary\",\"out | in | binary is \\\"r+b\\\" (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In C++, the second argument of <code>open()</code> is a string such as <code>\\\"w\\\"</code>.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The second argument in C++ is an integer built with bitwise OR of ios constants; strings like \\\"w\\\" are C's fopen modes (L1, Opening files).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. It is an integer whose value is set as a bitwise OR (|) of constants defined in the class ios, e.g. ios::out (L1, Opening files).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What does closing a file do to its logical name?\",\"o\":[[\"Frees it so it can be used for another physical file\",\"Correct. Closing frees the logical name for another physical file, like hanging up the telephone after a call (L1, Closing files).\"],[\"Deletes the physical file it was linked to\",\"Closing does not delete anything; the physical file stays on disk (L1).\"],[\"Truncates the physical file\",\"Truncation is done by the \\\"w\\\" mode or ios::trunc when opening, not by closing (L1).\"],[\"Links it permanently to the physical file\",\"The link is made at opening; closing removes it (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: If a program does not close its file, the OS closes it at the end of the program, but if the program terminates abnormally, data may be lost.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The bytes wait in a buffer and are sent as a block, so an abnormal termination can lose the buffered data (L1, Closing files).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says exactly this: the OS closes it at the end, but abnormal termination may lose data (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the generic operation <code>Read(Source_file, Destination_addr, Size)</code>, <code>Destination_addr</code> is ………\",\"o\":[[\"The logical file name the program reads from\",\"That is Source_file (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"The number of bytes brought in from the file\",\"That is Size (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"The first address of the memory block where the data will be read into\",\"Correct. Destination_addr is the first address of the memory block where we want to read the data (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"The physical name of the file on disk\",\"Read works with the logical name, and Destination_addr is a memory address, not a file name (L1).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In <code>fread(a, 1, 10, infile);</code> where <code>a</code> is a char array, the argument <code>1</code> is ………\",\"o\":[[\"The address of the variable\",\"The address is the first argument, a (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"The number of elements\",\"The number of elements is the third argument, 10 (L1).\"],[\"The logical file name\",\"The logical file name is the fourth argument, infile (L1).\"],[\"The element size in bytes\",\"Correct. The second argument is the element size; a char occupies 1 byte, so it is 1 (L1, Reading and writing).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the slide-30 table, which C function is used to read a record from a file?\",\"o\":[[\"fread\",\"Correct. fread (File Read) reads a record: fread(&amp;emp, sizeof(emp), 1, my_file); (L1, Methods used to read from files).\"],[\"fgetc\",\"fgetc (File Get Character) reads one character (L1).\"],[\"fgets\",\"fgets (File Get String) reads a string (L1).\"],[\"fscanf\",\"fscanf (File Scan Format) reads formatted text (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the slide-30 table, the shortcut name of <code>fscanf</code> is ………\",\"o\":[[\"File Get String\",\"File Get String is fgets (L1, Methods used to read from files).\"],[\"File Get Character\",\"File Get Character is fgetc (L1).\"],[\"File Scan Format\",\"Correct. fscanf = File Scan Format: it reads formatted text from a file (L1, Methods used to read from files).\"],[\"File Read\",\"File Read is fread (L1).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: In C, you detect the end of file by checking whether <code>fread</code> returned 0.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. int i = fread(&amp;c,1,1,infile); if (i==0) is true when the file has ended (L1, Detecting end of file).\"],[\"False\",\"This is the C method on slide 36; the C++ method is infile.fail() or infile.eof() (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: In C++, with <code>infile &gt;&gt; c;</code> where <code>c</code> is a char, you must state how many bytes to transfer.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The &gt;&gt; operator works at a higher level: since c is a char, it is implicit that only 1 byte is transferred (L1, Reading and writing).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Because c is a char variable, &gt;&gt; implicitly transfers 1 byte; the byte count is given only with read/fread (L1, Reading and writing).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: The standard logical names such as <code>stdout</code> and <code>cin</code> must be opened before the program uses them.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Standard input, output and error do not need to be opened or closed in the program (L1, Standard I/O logical names).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. These streams (stdout/cout, stdin/cin, stderr/cerr) do not need to be opened or closed (L1, Standard I/O logical names).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the sample program that displays <code>emp.txt</code>, what is the purpose of <code>if (my_file == NULL)</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"To test whether the file opened\",\"Correct. fopen returns NULL when the file cannot be opened, so the program prints an error and exits (L1, Sample: read employee records).\"],[\"To test whether the end of file was reached\",\"End of file is tested by while(!feof(my_file)) (L1).\"],[\"To check whether fread read a whole record\",\"That is the safer check fread(...) == 1 from the tip, not the NULL test (L1).\"],[\"To free the logical name\",\"Freeing the logical name is done by fclose(my_file); (L1).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which function does Method (1) <code>writeC()</code> use to write the character to <code>alphabet.txt</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"putchar\",\"putchar prints the character to the screen; it is used in readC() (L1, Full methods).\"],[\"fputc\",\"Correct. writeC reads the character with getche() and writes it with fputc(ch, fp) (L1, Full methods).\"],[\"fputs\",\"fputs writes strings; it is used in writeS() (L1).\"],[\"fprintf\",\"fprintf writes formatted text; it is used in writeASCII() (L1).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Method (6) <code>readASCII()</code> keeps reading lines with <code>fscanf</code> until <code>fscanf</code> returns ………\",\"o\":[[\"0\",\"Returning 0 is how fread signals the end of file in C, not fscanf in this method (L1, Detecting end of file).\"],[\"NULL\",\"NULL is what fopen returns when the file cannot be opened (L1).\"],[\"1\",\"1 is a normal successful read count; the loop stops on EOF (L1).\"],[\"EOF\",\"Correct. The loop does result = fscanf(...); if (result == EOF) break; (L1, Full methods).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Two different programs can each use the logical name <code>infile</code>, each connected to a different physical file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In the slide-20 figure one program reaches b.txt and another reaches aa.txt, both through a logical name called infile (L1, Physical file vs logical file).\"],[\"False\",\"The slide-20 figure shows exactly this: two programs, each with its own logical name infile, for b.txt and aa.txt (L1).\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":2,\"title\":\"Physical File Organization: the Hard Disk\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Secondary storage devices\",\"pts\":[\"Secondary storage is different from main memory, so we have to understand <b>how it works in order to do good file design</b>. Two major types of secondary storage:<br>(1) Direct Access Storage Devices (<b>DASD</b>)<br>(2) Serial Access Storage Devices (<b>SASD</b>)\",\"<b>DASD</b> (Direct Access Storage Devices):<br>• <b>Magnetic disks</b>: hard disk, floppy disk.<br>• <b>Optical disks</b>: CD-ROM (Compact Disk – Read Only Memory), CD-R (Compact Disk – Recordable), CD-RW (Compact Disk – ReWritable), DVD (Digital Video Disk).\",\"<b>SASD</b> (Serial Access Storage Devices): <b>magnetic tape</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-storage-types\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-storage-types\":\"DASD or SASD? Sort the secondary storage devices\"}},{\"h\":\"Disk components\",\"pts\":[\"We can use <b>disks</b> or <b>HDD</b> to refer to hard disks. The important components in disks are:<br>(1) Platters<br>(2) Read/write heads<br>(3) Arm assembly<br>(4) Spindle<br>(5) Arms\",\"Figure (slide 5, inside and outside a hard disk): a drive with its cover off, with labels for the base casting, spindle, slider (and head), actuator arm, actuator axis, actuator, platters, cover mounting holes (cover not shown), case mounting holes, ribbon cable (attaches heads to logic board), SCSI interface connector, jumper pins, jumper, power connector and tape seal.\",\"Figure (slides 6, 16 and 19, an opened drive): arrows point to the <b>platters</b>, <b>spindle</b>, <b>read/write heads</b>, <b>arm</b> and <b>arm assembly</b>.\",\"<b>1. Platter</b>: the <b>circular disk</b> on which magnetic data is stored in a hard disk drive. It can store information on <b>both sides (surfaces)</b> of one or more platters; all surfaces have the <b>same components at the same position</b>, and the platters are structured to facilitate <b>storage and retrieval</b> of data.<br>• Each platter is divided into concentric rings called <b>tracks</b>: there are thousands of tracks on each platter, they look like rings, and the information is stored in successive tracks on the surface of the disk.<br>• Each track is divided into a number of <b>sectors</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Platter layout: tracks, sectors, clusters and cylinders\",\"pts\":[\"Figure (slides 8, 15 and 18, disk drive with 4 platters and 8 surfaces): a side view of the platters on the <b>spindle</b>; there is a <b>read/write head</b> on each of the 8 surfaces, and all heads are held by the <b>boom</b>, which moves in and out (two arrows). On slide 8 a top view is added: a ring labelled <b>Track</b>, with <b>Track 0</b> and <b>Track 1</b>.\",\"Figure (slide 9): stacked platters; one ring on the top platter is marked <b>Track</b>, and a small piece of that ring is marked <b>Sector</b>.\",\"Example (slide 10): a platter has 1000 tracks, each containing 33 sectors. Track capacity = number of sectors per track × bytes per sector = 33 × 512 = <b>16,896 bytes</b>.\",\"A <b>sector</b> is the <b>smallest addressable portion</b> of a disk, usually <b>512 bytes</b>.\",\"Another view of sector organization is the one maintained by the <b>OS file manager</b>: it views the file as a series of <b>clusters</b> of sectors. A <b>cluster</b> is a fixed number of <b>contiguous sectors</b>.\",\"Example (slide 11): sector size = 512 bytes and cluster size = 3 sectors, so the cluster size = 3 × 512 = <b>1536 bytes</b>. The slide has two figures:<br>• a disk whose top surface has one small dark piece of a ring labelled \\\"Sector\\\" and a longer dark run labelled \\\"Cluster of 3 Sectors\\\";<br>• a stack of 4 platters with a small yellow piece on the top platter labelled \\\"Cluster\\\".\",\"Figure (slide 12): a disk surface with concentric tracks. <b>A</b> = track (one ring); <b>B</b> = geometrical sector (a pie-shaped wedge across all tracks); <b>C</b> = track sector (the part of the wedge on one track); <b>D</b> = cluster (a run of neighbouring sectors along one track).\",\"A <b>cylinder</b> is the set of tracks directly above/below each other. All data on one cylinder can be read <b>without moving the arm</b>. Number of cylinders = number of tracks per surface.\",\"Figure (slide 13, cylinder): a stack of 4 platters with the same yellow ring highlighted on every platter, labelled <b>Cylinder</b>.\",\"So a platter has four components: <b>sector</b>, <b>cluster</b>, <b>track</b>, <b>cylinder</b> (figure, slide 14: four stacks of platters, each highlighting one of them).\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-platter-parts\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-platter-parts\":\"Track, geometrical sector, track sector, cluster and cylinder on a platter\"}},{\"h\":\"Read/write heads, arm assembly, spindle and arms\",\"pts\":[\"<b>2. Read/write heads</b>: the small part of the disk that moves above the disk platter. Heads only fly above the platter surface with clearance of as little as <b>3 nanometers</b>. A read/write head <b>moves to the track that contains the data</b> to be transferred. Each surface has its own read/write head.\",\"<b>3. Arm assembly</b>: an internal set of hard disk components containing the arms, which contain the read/write heads. Its role is to <b>read and write information</b> from a set of platters coated with a thin magnetic material. When the arm assembly stops working, the drive fails.<br>On a read of a particular byte, the OS finds the correct platter, track and sector, reads the <b>entire sector into a buffer</b> (a special area in memory), then finds the requested byte within that buffer.\",\"<b>4. Spindle</b> holds the platters in a <b>fixed position</b>, with <b>enough space for the read/write arms</b> to get the data on the disks, and <b>rotates</b> them, bringing the sector under the head.\",\"<b>5. Arms</b> carry, guide and move the heads. Moving the arm is called <b>seeking</b>, and it is usually the <b>slowest</b> part.\",\"Figure (slide 20): three <b>platters</b> on one <b>spindle</b>, with a <b>rotation</b> arrow. Each platter has a ring of small segments: the ring on the top platter is labelled <b>track t</b> and one segment of the ring (middle platter) is labelled <b>sector s</b>; the same track on all platters, joined by dashed lines, is <b>cylinder c</b>. Each platter has a <b>read-write head</b> (a dark square) at the end of an <b>arm</b>, and all arms are fixed to the <b>arm assembly</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-disk-parts\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-disk-parts\":\"The hard disk's components, one by one (slide 20)\"}},{\"h\":\"Capacity formulas\",\"formula\":[\"Sector capacity = bytes per sector (512 B)\",\"Cluster capacity = sectors per cluster × bytes per sector\",\"Track capacity = sectors per track × bytes per sector\",\"Cylinder capacity = tracks per cylinder × track capacity\",\"Drive capacity = number of cylinders × cylinder capacity\",\"Platters = cylinder height / 2   (surfaces = cylinder height)\",\"Number of cylinders = tracks per surface\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-capacity\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-capacity\":\"Capacity calculator: sector, cluster, track, cylinder and drive\"}},{\"h\":\"Worked problem (slides 22–26)\",\"pts\":[\"Given:<br>• 90,000 fixed-length records of 256 B<br>• 512 B/sector<br>• 60 sectors/track<br>• cylinder height 14 tracks<br>• 5000 tracks/surface<br>• 4 sectors/cluster.\",\"• Platters = 14/2 = <b>7</b><br>• Sides = <b>14</b><br>• Track size = 60×512 = <b>30,720 B</b><br>• Cylinders = <b>5000</b>\",\"• Cylinder size = 14 × 30,720 = <b>430,080 B</b><br>• Sectors per cylinder = 430,080/512 = <b>840</b>\",\"• File size = 90,000 × 256 = <b>23,040,000 B</b><br>• Records/sector = 512/256 = <b>2</b><br>• Records/track = 30,720/256 = <b>120</b><br>• Records/cylinder = 430,080/256 = <b>1680</b>\",\"• Sectors for file = 23,040,000/512 = <b>45,000</b><br>• Tracks = 23,040,000/30,720 = <b>750</b><br>• Cylinders = 23,040,000/430,080 = <b>53.57</b> (‡ 54 whole cylinders)\",\"Drive capacity = 5000 × 430,080 = <b>2,150,400,000 B</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-worked-problem\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-worked-problem\":0},\"figH\":{\"fp-worked-problem\":\"The worked problem, step by step: how much disk a file needs\"}},{\"h\":\"Organizing tracks by sectors vs by blocks\",\"pts\":[\"<b>By sectors</b>: used by the <b>OS</b>.<br><b>Slow access</b> or <b>fragmentation</b> when handling a file of records.<br>Fragmentation is loss of space within a sector, due to records not fitting exactly in a sector (e.g. sector size is 512 B and record size is 300 B).<br>Some OSs allow the <b>system administrator to use clusters</b>.\",\"<b>By blocks</b>: used by the <b>DBMS</b>. Disk tracks may be divided into <b>user-defined blocks (pages)</b> rather than sectors. A block is usually organized to contain an <b>integral number of logical records</b>.\",\"<b>Blocking factor</b> = number of records stored in each block. No internal fragmentation and no record spans two blocks.\",\"In the block-addressing scheme each block of data is accompanied by one or more <b>sub-blocks</b> containing extra information about the block. A block typically contains:<br>• <b>Count sub-block</b>: the number of bytes in the block.<br>• <b>Key sub-block</b> (optional): the key of the last record in the data sub-block, so the disk controller can search for a key without loading the block into main memory.<br>• <b>Data sub-block</b>: the data records.\",\"Figure (slide 29, sub-blocks): one block drawn as an arc of a track, made of a <b>key sub-block</b>, a <b>count sub-block</b> and the <b>data sub-block</b>, followed by a <b>gap</b> before the next block.\",\"Figure (slide 30, pages and blocks): a disk surface whose concentric tracks are cut into sectors, with gaps between them. Below it, one track is drawn as a row: sector 1, gap, sector 2, gap, sector 3, gap, sector 4, gap, …, and a brace shows <b>1 page/block = 4 sectors</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-sectors-blocks\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-sectors-blocks\":\"Sectors, blocks and sub-blocks: how a track is organized\"}},{\"h\":\"Non-data overhead\",\"pts\":[\"Space used for things other than data.\",\"Sector-organized: sector address, track address, condition (defective?) at the start of each sector, plus gaps between sectors.\",\"Block-organized: sub-blocks and inter-block gaps, so there is <b>more</b> non-data overhead than with sector addressing.\",\"<b>Exercise</b> (slides 32–34): a <b>block-addressable</b> disk with track size = 20,000 B, non-data overhead per block = 300 B, record size = 100 B. How many records can be stored per track if the blocking factor is (a) 10, (b) 60?<br>BF = 10: data sub-block = 1000; block = 1000+300 = 1300; ⌊20,000/1300⌋ = ⌊15.38⌋ = 15 blocks, so <b>150 records/track</b>.<br>BF = 60: data sub-block = 6000; block = 6000+300 = 6300; ⌊20,000/6300⌋ = ⌊3.17⌋ = 3 blocks, so <b>180 records/track</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-records-track\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-records-track\":\"Records per track with a blocking factor and non-data overhead\"}},{\"h\":\"The cost of a disk access\",\"formula\":[\"Average total time = Seek time + Rotational delay + Transfer time\",\"Revolution time (ms) = 60 × 1000 / RPM\",\"Average latency = ½ × revolution time   (min = 0, max = 1 revolution)\",\"Transfer time = (bytes transferred / bytes per track) × revolution time\",\"              = revolution time / sectors per track   (for one sector)\"],\"pts\":[\"The time to access a sector in a track on a surface is divided into <b>3 components</b>: <b>seek time</b>, <b>rotational delay</b> and <b>transfer time</b>.\",\"Figure (slide 35): a disk with the arm reaching over it. Labels show the <b>seek time</b> (the arm moving to the track), the <b>rotational latency</b> (the disk turning the sector to the head) and the <b>transmission time</b> (the sector passing under the head).\",\"<b>Seek time</b>: time required to move the arm to the correct cylinder. The <b>largest</b> in cost. Typically:<br>• 5 ms to move from one track to the next (track-to-track).<br>• 50 ms maximum (from the inside track to the outside track).<br>• 30 ms average (from one random track to another random track).\",\"<b>Rotational delay (latency)</b>: time needed for the disk to rotate so the sector we want is under the read/write head.<br>Hard disks usually rotate at about 5000 rpm, which is one revolution per 12 ms.<br>• Min latency = 0<br>• Max latency = time for one disk revolution<br>• <b>Average latency (r)</b> = (min + max)/2 = max/2 = time for ½ disk revolution = ½ × (60 × 1000)/RPM ms<br>Typically 6–8 ms average.\",\"<b>Transfer time</b>: the time for the read/write head to pass over a <b>block</b>.<br>Transfer time = (number of bytes transferred / number of bytes on a track) × rotation time, or revolution time / number of sectors per track.<br>E.g. with 63 sectors per track, the time to transfer one sector is 1/63 of a revolution.\"],\"table\":[[\"Time component\",\"Action\"],[\"Seek time\",\"Time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder\"],[\"Rotational delay (or latency)\",\"Time it takes for the disk to rotate so that the desired sector is under the read/write head\"],[\"Transfer time\",\"Once the read/write head is positioned over the data, this is the time it takes for transferring data\"]],\"fig\":[\"fp-access-steps\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-access-steps\":1},\"figH\":{\"fp-access-steps\":\"A disk access in three steps: seek, rotational latency, transfer (slide 35)\"}},{\"h\":\"Examples 1 and 2\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Ex 1</b>: average seek 8 ms, average rotational delay 3 ms, maximum rotational delay 6 ms, spindle speed 10,000 rpm, 170 sectors/track, sector size 512 B. Average time to read one sector?<br>Transfer = revolution time / sectors per track = (1/10,000 min)/170 = (60/10,000 s)/170 = 6/170,000 s = 6/170 ms ≈ <b>0.035 ms</b>.<br>Total = 8 + 3 + 0.035 = <b>11.035 ms</b>.\",\"<b>Ex 2</b> (a current disk, Seagate ST3200822A): capacity 200 GB; transfer rate: maximum internal 683 Mbits/sec, maximum external 100 Mbytes/sec; discs/heads 2/4; 512 bytes per sector; spindle speed 7200 rpm; average seek 8.5 ms; average latency 4.16 ms; 170 sectors per track. Average time to read one sector?<br>Transfer = (1/7200 min)/170 = (60/7200 s)/170 = 6/(720 × 170) = 6/122,400 ≈ <b>0.05 ms</b>.<br>Total = 8.5 + 4.16 + 0.05 = <b>12.71 ms</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-access-time\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-access-time\":\"Average time to read one sector: Examples 1 and 2, editable\"}},{\"h\":\"Disk as a bottleneck\",\"pts\":[\"Processes are often <b>disk-bound</b>: the network and CPU wait a long time for the disk. Techniques:\",\"<b>1. Multiprocessing</b>: the CPU works on other jobs while waiting for the disk.\",\"<b>2. Disk striping</b>: put different blocks of the file on different drives, so independent processes do not interfere (parallelism).\",\"<b>3. RAID</b>: Redundant Array of Independent Disks.\",\"<b>4. RAM disk</b> (memory disk): part of main memory simulates a disk (speed vs volatility).\",\"<b>5. Disk cache</b>: a large block of memory holds pages from the disk. The cache is checked first, and on a miss the disk is accessed.\"]},{\"h\":\"Quiz (2), solved\",\"pts\":[\"Given:<br>• 20 surfaces<br>• 800 tracks/surface<br>• 25 sectors/track<br>• 512 B/sector<br>• 3600 rpm<br>• seek: 7 ms track-to-track, 28 ms average, 50 ms max.\",\"‡ (a) Revolution = 60,000/3600 = 16.67 ms, so <b>average latency = 8.33 ms</b>.\",\"‡ (b) <b>Disk capacity</b> = 20 × 800 × 25 × 512 = <b>204,800,000 B ≈ 195.3 MB</b>.\",\"‡ (c) <b>Read the whole disk, one cylinder at a time</b> (800 cylinders × 20 tracks). Assume each track costs average latency + one full revolution = 8.33 + 16.67 = 25 ms, so one cylinder = 20 × 25 = 500 ms. Moves: one average seek (28 ms) to reach the first cylinder, then 799 track-to-track seeks (7 ms).<br>Total ≈ 28 + 799×7 + 800×500 = 28 + 5,593 + 400,000 = <b>405,621 ms ≈ 405.6 s</b>. The slide gives no answer, so state your assumptions in the exam.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"DASD examples\",\"Magnetic disks (hard, floppy) and optical disks (CD-ROM, CD-R, CD-RW, DVD).\"],[\"SASD example\",\"Magnetic tape.\"],[\"Sector\",\"The smallest addressable portion of a disk, usually 512 bytes.\"],[\"Cluster\",\"A fixed number of contiguous sectors (the OS file manager's view).\"],[\"Cylinder\",\"Tracks directly above/below each other; readable without moving the arm.\"],[\"Number of cylinders =\",\"Number of tracks per surface.\"],[\"Seeking\",\"Moving the arm; usually the slowest part of a disk access.\"],[\"What carries, guides and moves the R/W heads?\",\"The arms.\"],[\"Spindle\",\"Holds the platters in a fixed position and rotates them.\"],[\"Blocking factor\",\"Number of records stored in each block.\"],[\"Sub-blocks\",\"Count (bytes in block), Key (optional, last record's key), Data.\"],[\"Fragmentation\",\"Loss of space within a sector because records do not fit exactly.\"],[\"Average latency formula\",\"½ × (60 × 1000 / RPM) ms.\"],[\"Transfer time (one sector)\",\"Revolution time / sectors per track.\"],[\"Access time\",\"Seek + rotational delay + transfer.\"],[\"5 fixes for the disk bottleneck\",\"Multiprocessing, disk striping, RAID, RAM disk, disk cache.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Mention the differences between seek time, rotational delay and transfer time.\",\"Seek time: time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder (the largest cost). Rotational delay (latency): time for the disk to rotate so the desired sector is under the head (average = half a revolution). Transfer time: once the head is over the data, the time to transfer it (revolution time / sectors per track per sector).\"],[\"\\\"Network and CPU have to wait a long time for the disk.\\\" Give four techniques to solve this.\",\"Multiprocessing (CPU does other jobs while waiting); disk striping (blocks on different drives in parallel); RAID; RAM disk (memory simulates a disk); disk cache (memory holds disk pages, and a miss goes to disk).\"],[\"Compare organizing tracks by sectors and by blocks.\",\"Sectors: used by the OS; fixed 512 B units; causes fragmentation and slow access for record files. Blocks: used by the DBMS; user-defined size holding an integral number of records (blocking factor); no internal fragmentation, no record spans two blocks; sub-blocks add more non-data overhead.\"],[\"A disk rotates at 6000 rpm. Find the average latency.\",\"Revolution = 60,000/6000 = 10 ms, so average latency = 5 ms.\"],[\"Sector = 512 B, cluster = 3 sectors. Cluster size?\",\"3 × 512 = 1536 bytes.\"],[\"A platter has 1000 tracks with 33 sectors each. Track capacity?\",\"33 × 512 = 16,896 bytes (the number of tracks does not matter for track capacity).\"],[\"Consider a block-addressable disk: size of track = 20,000 bytes, non-data overhead per block = 300 bytes, record size = 100 bytes. How many records can be stored per track if the blocking factor is (a) 10, (b) 60?\",\"(a) Size of data sub-block = 10 × 100 = 1000 B, so block = 1000 + 300 = 1300 B; blocks per track = ⌊20,000/1300⌋ = ⌊15.38⌋ = 15, so records per track = 15 × 10 = <b>150</b>.<br>(b) Data sub-block = 6000 B, block = 6300 B; blocks per track = ⌊20,000/6300⌋ = ⌊3.17⌋ = 3, so records per track = 3 × 60 = <b>180</b>.\"],[\"Quiz (2): a disk has 20 surfaces, 800 tracks/surface, 25 sectors/track, 512 bytes/sector, 3600 rpm, 7 ms track-to-track seek, 28 ms average seek and 50 ms maximum seek. Find (a) the average latency and (b) the disk capacity.\",\"‡ (a) Revolution = 60 × 1000 / 3600 = 16.67 ms, so average latency = ½ × 16.67 = <b>8.33 ms</b>.<br>(b) Capacity = surfaces × tracks/surface × sectors/track × bytes/sector = 20 × 800 × 25 × 512 = <b>204,800,000 bytes</b> (≈ 195.3 MB).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"___ are used to carry, guide and move the read/write head.\",\"o\":[[\"Arms\",\"Correct. The arms carry, guide and move the read/write heads; moving them is called seeking, usually the slowest part of an access (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Arm assembly\",\"The arm assembly is the whole internal set that contains the arms.\"],[\"Platters\",\"Platters are the circular disks that STORE the magnetic data on both surfaces; they do not move the heads (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Spindle\",\"The spindle holds the platters in place and ROTATES them to bring a sector under the head; the arms move the heads (L2, Disk components).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm Model A\"},{\"q\":\"___ is the time needed to move the read/write arm to the correct sector (cylinder).\",\"o\":[[\"Transfer time\",\"Transfer time is the time for the head to pass over the data once it is positioned, not the time to move the arm (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"Latency\",\"Latency (rotational delay) is the wait for the disk to rotate the sector under the head, after the arm is already in place (L2).\"],[\"Rotational time\",\"Rotation is done by the spindle and gives rotational delay; moving the arm to the right cylinder is seek time (L2).\"],[\"Seek time\",\"Correct. Seek time is the time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder; it is the largest part of the access cost (typical average 30 ms) (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm Model A\"},{\"q\":\"All the information on a single cylinder can be accessed without:\",\"o\":[[\"Moving the spindle\",\"The spindle never needs to \\\"move\\\": it only rotates the platters all the time. What stays still on one cylinder is the arm assembly (L2, Cylinder).\"],[\"Moving the arm assembly\",\"Correct. A cylinder is all tracks under the heads at one arm position.\"],[\"Rotation\",\"Rotation is still needed to bring sectors under the head.\"],[\"Seeking\",\"Seeking means moving the arm, which is the same idea, but the model answer is (b).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm Model B\"},{\"q\":\"Sector size = 512 B, 4 sectors per cluster, 64 sectors per track. Cluster size =\",\"o\":[[\"32,768 bytes\",\"32,768 = 64 × 512 is the TRACK capacity (sectors per track × bytes per sector); a cluster is only 4 sectors (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"2048 bytes\",\"Correct. 4 × 512 = 2048. Sectors per track is a distractor.\"],[\"128 bytes\",\"128 = 512 / 4 comes from dividing instead of multiplying; cluster capacity = sectors per cluster × bytes per sector (L2).\"],[\"8 bytes\",\"8 bytes matches no formula (it is not 4 × 512 or 64 × 512); a cluster of 4 sectors holds 4 × 512 = 2048 B (L2).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm Model A\"},{\"q\":\"Average latency for a disk spinning at 5000 rpm is:\",\"o\":[[\"12 ms\",\"12 ms = 60,000 / 5000 is one FULL revolution; average latency is half a revolution, 6 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"6 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60 × 1000 / 5000 = 12 ms, and average latency = ½ × 12 = 6 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"3 ms\",\"3 ms is a quarter revolution (12 / 4); the average wait is half a revolution, ½ × 12 = 6 ms (L2).\"],[\"0.012 ms\",\"0.012 is one revolution in SECONDS (60 / 5000), wrongly labelled ms and not halved; the answer is ½ × 12 ms = 6 ms (L2).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm Model A\"},{\"q\":\"Track = 20,000 B, overhead = 300 B/block, record = 100 B, blocking factor = 60. Records per track?\",\"o\":[[\"200\",\"200 = 20,000 / 100 ignores the 300 B overhead per block and the rule that only whole blocks fit; the right count is 3 blocks × 60 = 180 (L2, Non-data overhead).\"],[\"180\",\"Correct. Block = 6300 B, ⌊20,000/6300⌋ = 3 blocks × 60 = 180.\"],[\"190\",\"190 ≈ 3.17 blocks × 60 counts a partial block; only ⌊20,000 / 6300⌋ = 3 whole blocks fit, giving 180 (L2).\"],[\"150\",\"150 is the slide answer for blocking factor 10 (block 1300 B, 15 blocks × 10); with BF = 60 it is 180 (L2, Non-data overhead).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which unit is the smallest addressable portion of a disk?\",\"o\":[[\"Track\",\"A track is a whole ring on the surface, divided into many sectors, so it is not the smallest addressable unit (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Cluster\",\"A cluster is a fixed number of contiguous sectors (the OS view), so it is bigger than a sector (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Sector\",\"Correct. A sector is the smallest addressable portion of a disk, usually 512 bytes; tracks, clusters and cylinders are all made of sectors (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Cylinder\",\"A cylinder is the set of tracks above/below each other on all surfaces, the largest of these units (L2, Disk components).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which is a Serial Access Storage Device?\",\"o\":[[\"Hard disk\",\"A hard disk is a magnetic disk, which the lecture lists under DASD (direct access), not serial access (L2, Secondary storage devices).\"],[\"CD-ROM\",\"A CD-ROM is an optical disk, listed under DASD (direct access); the only SASD example is magnetic tape (L2).\"],[\"Magnetic tape\",\"Correct. The lecture lists magnetic tape as the Serial Access Storage Device (SASD); magnetic and optical disks are DASD (L2, Secondary storage devices).\"],[\"DVD\",\"A DVD is an optical disk and therefore a DASD (direct access); the SASD example is magnetic tape (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The key sub-block contains:\",\"o\":[[\"The number of bytes in the block\",\"The number of bytes in the block is held in the COUNT sub-block; the key sub-block holds the key of the last record (L2, Sub-blocks).\"],[\"The key of the last record in the data sub-block\",\"Correct. It lets the controller search without loading the block into memory.\"],[\"All the records\",\"The records themselves are in the DATA sub-block; the key sub-block holds only the last record's key (L2, Sub-blocks).\"],[\"The sector address\",\"The sector address is non-data overhead of sector-organized tracks; it is not one of the block's sub-blocks (L2, Non-data overhead).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A disk has cylinder height 14. How many platters does it have?\",\"o\":[[\"14\",\"Cylinder height 14 means 14 tracks per cylinder, i.e. 14 surfaces (sides). Each platter has 2 surfaces, so there are 7 platters (L2).\"],[\"7\",\"Correct. Cylinder height = number of surfaces = 14, and each platter has two surfaces, so platters = 14 / 2 = 7 (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"28\",\"28 = 14 × 2 multiplies instead of dividing; platters = cylinder height / 2 = 7 (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"5000\",\"That is the number of cylinders in the slide example.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which technique puts different blocks of a file on different drives?\",\"o\":[[\"RAM disk\",\"A RAM disk is part of main memory simulating a disk; it does not spread a file's blocks over several drives (L2, Disk bottleneck).\"],[\"Disk cache\",\"A disk cache is a large block of memory holding pages from the disk, checked before the disk; it uses one drive (L2, Disk bottleneck).\"],[\"Disk striping\",\"Correct. Disk striping puts different blocks of a file on different drives, so independent processes can work in parallel without interfering (L2, Disk bottleneck).\"],[\"Multiprocessing\",\"Multiprocessing lets the CPU work on other jobs while waiting for the disk; it does not place blocks on drives (L2, Disk bottleneck).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"7200 rpm, 170 sectors/track. Transfer time for one sector ≈\",\"o\":[[\"8.33 ms\",\"8.33 ms = 60,000 / 7200 is one full revolution, i.e. the time to transfer a whole track, not one sector (L2, Example 2).\"],[\"4.16 ms\",\"4.16 ms is half a revolution, the average latency in Example 2, not the transfer time (L2).\"],[\"0.05 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60,000 / 7200 = 8.33 ms; one sector = 8.33 / 170 ≈ 0.049 ≈ 0.05 ms (L2, Example 2).\"],[\"170 ms\",\"170 is the number of sectors per track, not a time; transfer for one sector = revolution / 170 ≈ 0.05 ms (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Network and CPU have to wait a long time for the disk to tansmit data, we have a various teckniques to solve this problem\",\"o\":[[\"Disk striping and distributed processing\",\"Disk striping and distributed processing (multiprocessing) are techniques, but incomplete: RAID is one too, so \\\"Both A and B\\\" is fuller (L2, Disk bottleneck).\"],[\"Redundant Array of indepentent disks\",\"RAID is only one technique; striping and multiprocessing are also on the slide, so \\\"Both A and B\\\" is fuller (L2).\"],[\"Both A and B\",\"Correct. L2 \\\"Disk as a bottleneck\\\": the network and CPU wait for the disk. Techniques: (1) multiprocessing, (2) disk striping, (3) RAID, (4) RAM disk, (5) disk cache. The question asks for the \\\"various techniques\\\", and \\\"Both A and B\\\" covers the most of them: striping + distributed processing (multiprocessing) + RAID.\"],[\"Disk cache\",\"Disk cache is a real technique (number 5 on the slide), but it is only one; \\\"Both A and B\\\" groups more of the slide's techniques (L2).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Used to carry, Gide and move the read/write head called .....\",\"o\":[[\"Spindles\",\"Spindles hold the platters and rotate them under the heads; they do not move the heads (L2).\"],[\"Platters\",\"Platters are the circular disks that store the data on both surfaces (L2).\"],[\"Arm assembly\",\"The arm assembly is the whole internal unit that holds the arms and heads; the specific part that carries and moves the heads is the arms (L2).\"],[\"Arms\",\"Correct. L2 disk components: the ARMS carry, guide and move the read/write heads; moving them to the right track is seeking (same question and answer in Midterm 2022/23 Model A).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The small part of the disk, that move above the disk platter is ----\",\"o\":[[\"Platter\",\"The platter is the circular magnetic disk that stores the data; the head moves above it (L2).\"],[\"Arm\",\"The arm carries, guides and moves the head; it is not the small part that reads and writes above the surface (L2).\"],[\"Spindle\",\"The spindle holds and rotates the platters; it does not move above them (L2).\"],[\"R/W Head\",\"Correct. L2: read/write heads are \\\"the small part of the disk that moves above the disk platter\\\". They fly above the surface (clearance as little as 3 nm) and move to the track that holds the data; each surface has its own head.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The circular disk on which magnetic data is stored in a hard disk drive is .......\",\"o\":[[\"Spindle\",\"The spindle is the shaft that holds and spins the platters; it stores nothing (L2).\"],[\"Arm assembly\",\"The arm assembly holds the arms and heads that read and write the platters; it does not store data (L2).\"],[\"Platter\",\"Correct. L2: the platter is \\\"the circular disk on which magnetic data is stored in a hard disk drive\\\". It is coated with a thin magnetic material and can store data on both surfaces.\"],[\"Arm\",\"The arm carries, guides and moves the read/write head (L2).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The time to access a sector in a track on a surface is divided into .....\",\"o\":[[\"Three components\",\"Correct. L2 cost of a disk access: \\\"the time to access a sector in a track on a surface is divided into 3 components\\\": seek time (move the arm to the track), rotational delay (wait for the sector to come under the head) and transfer time (the head passes over the block).\"],[\"Four components\",\"Four is the number of platter components (sector, cluster, track, cylinder), a different list in the same lecture (L2).\"],[\"one components\",\"The lecture splits the access time into three separate parts, not one (L2).\"],[\"Two components\",\"Two leaves one of the three out (usually transfer time) (L2).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"It's time to take the disk to rotate so that the desired sector is under the read/write head\",\"o\":[[\"Rotational delay (or latency)\",\"Correct. L2: rotational delay (latency) is the time needed for the disk to rotate so the sector we want is under the read/write head. Minimum 0, maximum one revolution, average half a revolution.\"],[\"Transfer time\",\"Transfer time is the head passing over the block, after the sector is already under it (L2).\"],[\"Seek Time\",\"Seek time is moving the ARM to the right track/cylinder: arm movement, not rotation (L2).\"],[\"Revolution time\",\"Revolution time is one FULL turn (e.g. 12 ms at 5000 rpm), which is only the MAXIMUM latency (L2).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The DBMS views the file as a series of .......\",\"o\":[[\"Blocks\",\"Correct. L2 organizing tracks: by SECTORS (used by the OS, whose file manager views the file as a series of clusters) or by BLOCKS (used by the DBMS: tracks divided into user-defined blocks/pages, each usually holding an integral number of logical records). So the DBMS sees blocks.\"],[\"Clusters\",\"Clusters (fixed groups of contiguous sectors) are the OS file manager's view, not the DBMS's (L2).\"],[\"Tracks\",\"Tracks are physical rings on the platter, part of the disk's geometry, not a file manager's view of a file (L2).\"],[\"Bytes\",\"Bytes is the L3 stream view (a file as a stream of bytes), not the DBMS view (L3).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"It's the time for the read/write head to pass over a block.\",\"o\":[[\"Transfer time\",\"Correct. L2: transfer time is the time for the read/write head to pass over a block. Transfer time = (bytes transferred / bytes on a track) × rotation time, or revolution time / sectors per track.\"],[\"Seek Time\",\"Seek time is moving the ARM to the right track; it happens before the head reaches the block (L2).\"],[\"Rotational delay (or latency)\",\"Rotational delay is waiting for the sector to rotate UNDER the head, before passing over it (L2).\"],[\"Revolution time\",\"Revolution time is a full turn; transfer time is only the fraction of a turn the block takes up (L2).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"A hard disk with 20 surfaces will have _____ heads.\",\"o\":[[\"20\",\"Correct. L2: each SURFACE has its own read/write head (the slide's drive with 4 platters has 8 surfaces and 8 heads). So 20 surfaces → 20 heads.\"],[\"5\",\"5 divides by 4 for no reason: it is neither the number of platters (10) nor the number of heads (L2).\"],[\"10\",\"10 is the number of PLATTERS (2 surfaces each); heads are counted per surface, not per platter (L2).\"],[\"1\",\"Every surface needs its own head; they all move together on the arm assembly, but there are still 20 of them (L2).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"All the information on a single cylinder can be accessed without ………..\",\"o\":[[\"Moving the Spindle\",\"The spindle never \\\"moves\\\": it only rotates the platters all the time (L2).\"],[\"Rotation\",\"Rotation is still needed to bring each sector under the head; only arm movement is avoided (L2).\"],[\"moving the arm assembly\",\"Correct. L2: a cylinder is the set of tracks directly above/below each other, all under the heads at one arm position, so its data can be read without moving the arm. Same question in Midterm 2022/23 Model B, model answer \\\"moving the arm assembly\\\".\"],[\"Seeking\",\"Seeking means moving the arm, the same idea, but the model answer (Model B) is \\\"moving the arm assembly\\\", matching the slide's \\\"without moving the arm\\\" (L2).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"An internal set of hard disk component containing arms which contain the read/write head\",\"o\":[[\"Track\",\"A track is a ring on a platter surface where data is stored, part of the layout, not a set of components (L2).\"],[\"Arm assembly\",\"Correct. L2: the arm assembly is \\\"an internal set of hard disk components containing the arms, which contain the read/write heads\\\". Its role is to read and write information from the platters; when it stops working, the drive fails.\"],[\"Spindle\",\"The spindle holds and rotates the platters (L2).\"],[\"Platter\",\"The platter is the circular magnetic disk that stores the data (L2).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"It's time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder\",\"o\":[[\"Revolution time\",\"Revolution time is one full turn of the disk (e.g. 12 ms at 5000 rpm); no arm movement (L2).\"],[\"Transfer time\",\"Transfer time is the head passing over the block, after the arm is in place and the sector has arrived (L2).\"],[\"Seek Time\",\"Correct. L2: seek time is the time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder. It is the first of the three access-time components and usually the slowest.\"],[\"Rotational delay (or latency)\",\"Rotational delay is waiting for the disk to spin the sector under the head, after the seek (L2).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A disk has 512 B/sector, 40 sectors/track, cylinder height 8 and 2000 tracks per surface. What is the drive capacity?\",\"o\":[[\"40,960,000 B\",\"That is 2000 × 40 × 512, the capacity of ONE surface. You forgot to multiply by the 8 tracks per cylinder.\"],[\"327,680,000 B\",\"Correct. Track = 40 × 512 = 20,480 B; cylinder = 8 × 20,480 = 163,840 B; 2000 cylinders × 163,840 = 327,680,000 B.\"],[\"163,840,000 B\",\"This used 4 (the number of platters) instead of 8 surfaces. Cylinder height counts surfaces.\"],[\"163,840 B\",\"That is the size of one cylinder, not the whole drive.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A disk spins at 4000 rpm, has 50 sectors per track and an average seek time of 9 ms. What is the average time to read 5 consecutive sectors?\",\"o\":[[\"25.5 ms\",\"This used a full revolution (15 ms) as the latency instead of half a revolution.\"],[\"16.8 ms\",\"This transferred only one sector (0.3 ms) instead of five.\"],[\"18 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60,000/4000 = 15 ms; latency = 7.5 ms; transfer = 5 × 15/50 = 1.5 ms; total = 9 + 7.5 + 1.5 = 18 ms.\"],[\"10.5 ms\",\"This left out the rotational delay (seek + transfer only).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A track has 24 sectors of 512 B. Records are 150 B, the blocking factor is 16 and the non-data overhead is 400 B per block. How many records fit on one track?\",\"o\":[[\"81\",\"That is 12,288/150, which ignores blocks and overhead.\"],[\"80\",\"This ignored the 400 B overhead (block = 2400 B gives 5 blocks).\"],[\"70\",\"This used 4.39 blocks. A partial block cannot be stored, so take the floor first.\"],[\"64\",\"Correct. Track = 24 × 512 = 12,288 B; block = 16 × 150 + 400 = 2800 B; ⌊12,288/2800⌋ = 4 blocks; 4 × 16 = 64 records.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A file has 30,000 fixed-length records of 128 B. The disk has 512 B/sector, 40 sectors/track and cylinder height 8. How many tracks are needed to store the file?\",\"o\":[[\"188\",\"Correct. File = 30,000 × 128 = 3,840,000 B; track = 40 × 512 = 20,480 B; 3,840,000/20,480 = 187.5, rounded UP to 188.\"],[\"187\",\"Rounded down. A partial track must still be allocated, so round up.\"],[\"7500\",\"That is the number of SECTORS (3,840,000/512).\"],[\"24\",\"That is the number of CYLINDERS (3,840,000/163,840 = 23.4, rounded up).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"When the OS reads a single byte from a disk, what does it actually read into the buffer?\",\"o\":[[\"Only that byte\",\"The sector is the smallest addressable unit, so a single byte cannot be read alone.\"],[\"The entire sector that contains the byte\",\"Correct. The OS finds the platter, track and sector, reads the entire sector into a buffer, then finds the byte.\"],[\"The entire track\",\"A track is larger than the smallest addressable unit; the slides say a sector is read.\"],[\"The entire cylinder\",\"A cylinder is a group of tracks; it is not the read unit.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which technique for the disk bottleneck uses part of main memory to simulate a disk, trading volatility for speed?\",\"o\":[[\"Disk striping\",\"Striping puts blocks of a file on different drives.\"],[\"Disk cache\",\"A disk cache holds pages copied from the disk; it does not act as a disk itself.\"],[\"RAM disk\",\"Correct. A RAM disk (memory disk) simulates a disk in main memory: fast, but volatile.\"],[\"Multiprocessing\",\"Multiprocessing lets the CPU do other jobs while waiting for the disk.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Organizing tracks by blocks has LESS non-data overhead than organizing them by sectors.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Blocks add sub-blocks (count, key) and inter-block gaps, which increases overhead.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The slides say block organization has MORE non-data overhead than sector addressing.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Tracks are organized by 512-byte sectors, and each 300-byte record is stored in its own sector. The unused 212 bytes in each sector are an example of:\",\"o\":[[\"Seeking\",\"Seeking is moving the arm to the correct cylinder; it has nothing to do with unused space inside a sector (L2).\"],[\"Blocking factor\",\"The blocking factor is the number of records per block, used in block organization.\"],[\"Disk striping\",\"Disk striping spreads a file's blocks over several drives for parallelism; it does not describe wasted bytes in a sector (L2).\"],[\"Fragmentation\",\"Correct. Fragmentation is the loss of space within a sector when records do not fit exactly.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"The number of cylinders on a disk equals the number of tracks per surface.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Each cylinder is the set of tracks at one arm position, one per surface, so there is one cylinder per track position.\"],[\"False\",\"The slides state this rule directly: number of cylinders = tracks per surface.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which part of a disk access is usually the slowest (largest cost)?\",\"o\":[[\"Seek time\",\"Correct. Moving the arm to the right cylinder is usually the slowest part.\"],[\"Transfer time\",\"Transfer of one sector is a tiny fraction of a revolution (e.g. 0.05 ms).\"],[\"Rotational delay\",\"Latency is typically 6–8 ms, which is less than a typical 30 ms average seek.\"],[\"Reading the sector into the buffer\",\"This is part of the transfer, not the largest cost.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"……… is the circular disk on which magnetic data is stored in a hard disk drive.\",\"o\":[[\"Spindle\",\"The spindle holds the platters and rotates them; it does not store data (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Track\",\"A track is one concentric ring on a platter, not the whole disk (L2).\"],[\"Platter\",\"Correct. The platter is the circular disk that stores magnetic data, on both of its surfaces (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Cylinder\",\"A cylinder is the set of tracks above/below each other across platters (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"……… holds the platters in a fixed position and rotates them, bringing the sector under the head.\",\"o\":[[\"Arms\",\"Arms carry, guide and move the heads (L2, Disk components).\"],[\"Arm assembly\",\"The arm assembly is the internal set that contains the arms and heads (L2).\"],[\"Read/write head\",\"The head reads and writes data as it passes over the track; it does not rotate the platters (L2).\"],[\"Spindle\",\"Correct. The spindle holds the platters in a fixed position with space for the arms, and rotates them (L2, Disk components).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: A CD-RW is a Serial Access Storage Device (SASD).\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"A CD-RW is an optical disk, which is a DASD; the lecture's SASD example is magnetic tape (L2, Secondary storage devices).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. CD-RW (Compact Disk ReWritable) is an optical disk, a Direct Access Storage Device; SASD is magnetic tape (L2, Secondary storage devices).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A ……… is a fixed number of contiguous sectors; it is how the OS file manager views a file.\",\"o\":[[\"Track\",\"A track is a whole ring divided into sectors (L2).\"],[\"Block\",\"A block (page) is user-defined and used by the DBMS, holding an integral number of records (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"Cylinder\",\"A cylinder is the set of tracks above/below each other (L2).\"],[\"Cluster\",\"Correct. The OS file manager sees the file as a series of clusters, each a fixed number of contiguous sectors (L2, Platter layout).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the slide-12 figure, the pie-shaped wedge that crosses all the tracks (letter B) is the ………\",\"o\":[[\"Track sector\",\"The track sector (C) is only the part of the wedge on one track (L2, Platter layout).\"],[\"Geometrical sector\",\"Correct. B is the geometrical sector, a wedge across all tracks; A is a track, C a track sector, D a cluster (L2, Platter layout).\"],[\"Cluster\",\"The cluster (D) is a run of neighbouring sectors along one track (L2).\"],[\"Track\",\"The track (A) is one ring (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Organizing disk tracks by user-defined blocks (pages) is used by the ………\",\"o\":[[\"OS\",\"The OS organizes tracks by sectors (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"DBMS\",\"Correct. Organizing by blocks is used by the DBMS; organizing by sectors is used by the OS (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"Spindle\",\"The spindle is hardware that rotates the platters; it does not organize tracks (L2).\"],[\"Disk cache\",\"The disk cache is a memory area holding disk pages, a bottleneck technique (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which sub-block holds the number of bytes in the block?\",\"o\":[[\"Count sub-block\",\"Correct. The count sub-block contains the number of bytes in the block (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"Key sub-block\",\"The key sub-block (optional) holds the key of the last record in the data sub-block (L2).\"],[\"Data sub-block\",\"The data sub-block holds the data records themselves (L2).\"],[\"Inter-block gap\",\"A gap is empty space between blocks, part of the non-data overhead (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: When tracks are organized by blocks that hold an integral number of records, a record may span two blocks.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"A block holds a whole number of records, so no record spans two blocks and there is no internal fragmentation (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. With a blocking factor, each block holds an integral number of records: no record spans two blocks (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"……… is the time for the disk to rotate so that the desired sector is under the read/write head.\",\"o\":[[\"Seek time\",\"Seek time is moving the arm to the correct cylinder (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"Transfer time\",\"Transfer time is the time for the head to pass over the data once positioned (L2).\"],[\"Rotational delay\",\"Correct. Rotational delay (latency) is the wait for the platter to bring the sector under the head (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"Access time\",\"Access time is the total: seek + rotational delay + transfer (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: The minimum rotational latency is 0 and the maximum is the time of one full revolution.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. So the average latency = (min + max)/2 = half a revolution (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"False\",\"These are the lecture's values: min = 0, max = one revolution, average = ½ revolution (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the disk cache technique, what happens on a cache miss?\",\"o\":[[\"Part of main memory simulates the whole disk\",\"That is a RAM disk, a different technique (L2, Disk as a bottleneck).\"],[\"The disk is accessed\",\"Correct. The cache is checked first; on a miss the disk is accessed (L2, Disk as a bottleneck).\"],[\"The CPU switches to other jobs\",\"That is multiprocessing (L2).\"],[\"The block is read from several drives in parallel\",\"Putting blocks on different drives is disk striping (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Typically, a seek from the inside track to the outside track (the maximum seek) takes about ………\",\"o\":[[\"5 ms\",\"5 ms is the typical track-to-track seek (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"30 ms\",\"30 ms is the typical average seek, random track to random track (L2).\"],[\"50 ms\",\"Correct. 50 ms is the typical maximum seek, inside track to outside track (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"12 ms\",\"12 ms is one revolution at 5000 rpm, a rotation time, not a seek (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: Each surface of a platter has its own read/write head.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. On the 4-platter, 8-surface drive there is a read/write head on each of the 8 surfaces (L2, Read/write heads).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says each surface has its own head (8 heads for 8 surfaces) (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: RAID, one of the techniques for the disk bottleneck, stands for Redundant Array of Independent Disks.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. RAID = Redundant Array of Independent Disks (L2, Disk as a bottleneck).\"],[\"False\",\"This is the lecture's expansion of RAID (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A disk has 48 sectors per track and 512 bytes per sector. Track capacity = ………\",\"o\":[[\"24,576 B\",\"Correct. Track capacity = sectors per track × bytes per sector = 48 × 512 = 24,576 B (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"49,152 B\",\"49,152 = 96 × 512 doubles the sectors; a track has 48 sectors (L2).\"],[\"12,288 B\",\"12,288 = 24 × 512 uses only half the sectors (L2).\"],[\"560 B\",\"560 = 48 + 512 adds instead of multiplying (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: A disk with 64 sectors per track and 512 bytes per sector has a track capacity of 32,768 B.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. 64 × 512 = 32,768 B (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"False\",\"Track capacity = sectors per track × bytes per sector = 64 × 512 = 32,768 B, so the statement is true (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Sector size = 512 B, 8 sectors per cluster and 80 sectors per track. Cluster size = ………\",\"o\":[[\"40,960 B\",\"40,960 = 80 × 512 is the TRACK capacity; a cluster is only 8 sectors (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"64 B\",\"64 = 512 / 8 divides instead of multiplying (L2).\"],[\"640 B\",\"640 = 8 × 80 multiplies the two sector counts and ignores the 512 B (L2).\"],[\"4096 B\",\"Correct. Cluster = sectors per cluster × bytes per sector = 8 × 512 = 4096 B (L2, Capacity formulas).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Average latency for a disk spinning at 7500 rpm is ………\",\"o\":[[\"8 ms\",\"8 ms = 60,000/7500 is one full revolution, the MAXIMUM latency (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"2 ms\",\"2 ms is a quarter revolution; the average is half (L2).\"],[\"4 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60 × 1000 / 7500 = 8 ms; average latency = ½ × 8 = 4 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"0.008 ms\",\"60/7500 = 0.008 is one revolution in SECONDS, not ms (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: A disk spinning at 4000 rpm has an average latency of 15 ms.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"15 ms = 60,000/4000 is one full revolution (the maximum); the average latency is half: 7.5 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60,000/4000 = 15 ms, so the average latency = ½ × 15 = 7.5 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A drive has 6 platters, and data is stored on both surfaces of every platter. What is its cylinder height?\",\"o\":[[\"12\",\"Correct. Cylinder height = number of surfaces = 2 × platters = 2 × 6 = 12 (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"6\",\"6 is the number of platters; each platter has two surfaces, each with its own track in the cylinder (L2).\"],[\"3\",\"3 = 6/2 divides instead of multiplying (L2).\"],[\"24\",\"24 counts four tracks per platter; there are only two surfaces per platter (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A disk has 10 surfaces and 3000 tracks per surface. How many cylinders does it have?\",\"o\":[[\"3000\",\"Correct. Number of cylinders = number of tracks per surface = 3000 (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"30,000\",\"30,000 = 10 × 3000 is the total number of tracks on the disk (L2).\"],[\"300\",\"300 = 3000/10; the surfaces give the tracks per cylinder, not fewer cylinders (L2).\"],[\"10\",\"10 is the cylinder height (tracks per cylinder), not the number of cylinders (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A disk has 512 B/sector, 50 sectors/track, cylinder height 12 and 4000 tracks/surface. Cylinder capacity = ………\",\"o\":[[\"25,600 B\",\"25,600 = 50 × 512 is one TRACK; a cylinder has 12 tracks (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"307,200 B\",\"Correct. Track = 50 × 512 = 25,600 B; cylinder = 12 × 25,600 = 307,200 B (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"153,600 B\",\"153,600 = 6 × 25,600 uses the number of platters (6); the cylinder height counts surfaces (12) (L2).\"],[\"600 B\",\"600 = 50 × 12 is the number of SECTORS per cylinder, not bytes (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A disk has 512 B/sector, 50 sectors/track, cylinder height 12 and 4000 tracks/surface. Drive capacity = ………\",\"o\":[[\"102,400,000 B\",\"102,400,000 = 4000 × 25,600 forgets the cylinder height of 12 (L2, Capacity formulas).\"],[\"614,400,000 B\",\"614,400,000 uses 6 platters instead of 12 surfaces per cylinder (L2).\"],[\"14,745,600,000 B\",\"This multiplies by the cylinder height twice (L2).\"],[\"1,228,800,000 B\",\"Correct. Cylinder = 12 × 50 × 512 = 307,200 B; drive = 4000 cylinders × 307,200 = 1,228,800,000 B (L2, Capacity formulas).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A disk has 512 B/sector, 50 sectors/track, cylinder height 12 and 4000 tracks/surface. Records are 128 B. How many records fit in one cylinder?\",\"o\":[[\"200\",\"200 = 25,600/128 is the records per TRACK (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"4\",\"4 = 512/128 is the records per SECTOR (L2).\"],[\"2400\",\"Correct. Cylinder = 307,200 B; records per cylinder = 307,200/128 = 2400 (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"1200\",\"1200 = 307,200/256 would be for 256 B records (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A file has 40,000 fixed-length records of 128 B, stored on a disk with 512 B sectors. How many sectors does the file need?\",\"o\":[[\"10,000\",\"Correct. File = 40,000 × 128 = 5,120,000 B; sectors = 5,120,000/512 = 10,000 (4 records per sector) (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"40,000\",\"40,000 gives each record a whole sector, but 4 records of 128 B fit in a 512 B sector (L2).\"],[\"160,000\",\"160,000 = 40,000 × 4 multiplies by the records per sector instead of dividing (L2).\"],[\"5,120,000\",\"5,120,000 is the file size in bytes, not sectors (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A file has 20,000 fixed-length records of 256 B. The disk has 512 B/sector and 36 sectors/track. How many tracks are needed to store the file?\",\"o\":[[\"277\",\"5,120,000/18,432 = 277.78; rounding down leaves part of the file without a track (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"278\",\"Correct. File = 20,000 × 256 = 5,120,000 B; track = 36 × 512 = 18,432 B; 5,120,000/18,432 = 277.78, rounded up to 278 (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"556\",\"556 = 20,000/36 puts one record per sector, but 2 records of 256 B fit in a 512 B sector (L2).\"],[\"10,000\",\"10,000 = 5,120,000/512 is the number of SECTORS (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A 5,120,000 B file is stored on a disk with 18,432 B per track and cylinder height 8. How many whole cylinders are needed?\",\"o\":[[\"34\",\"5,120,000/147,456 = 34.72; 34 cylinders would not hold the whole file, so round up (L2, Worked problem).\"],[\"278\",\"278 is the number of TRACKS (5,120,000/18,432 rounded up) (L2).\"],[\"70\",\"70 uses 4 tracks per cylinder (the platters); a cylinder has 8 tracks (L2).\"],[\"35\",\"Correct. Cylinder = 8 × 18,432 = 147,456 B; 5,120,000/147,456 = 34.72, so 35 whole cylinders (L2, Worked problem).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A block-addressable disk has track size = 15,000 B, non-data overhead = 200 B per block and record size = 50 B. How many records can be stored per track if the blocking factor is 20?\",\"o\":[[\"300\",\"300 = 15,000/50 ignores the 200 B overhead per block (L2, Non-data overhead).\"],[\"250\",\"250 = 12.5 × 20 uses a fractional block; blocks per track must be floored to 12 (L2).\"],[\"240\",\"Correct. Data sub-block = 20 × 50 = 1000 B; block = 1000 + 200 = 1200 B; ⌊15,000/1200⌋ = ⌊12.5⌋ = 12 blocks; 12 × 20 = 240 records (L2, Non-data overhead).\"],[\"12\",\"12 is the number of BLOCKS per track, not records (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A block-addressable disk has track size = 15,000 B, non-data overhead = 200 B per block and record size = 50 B. How many records can be stored per track if the blocking factor is 50?\",\"o\":[[\"250\",\"Correct. Block = 50 × 50 + 200 = 2700 B; ⌊15,000/2700⌋ = ⌊5.56⌋ = 5 blocks; 5 × 50 = 250 records (L2, Non-data overhead).\"],[\"278\",\"278 ≈ 5.56 × 50 keeps the fractional block; a block cannot be split, so take the floor (L2).\"],[\"300\",\"300 = 15,000/50 ignores the overhead (L2).\"],[\"5\",\"5 is the number of blocks per track (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A disk spins at 6000 rpm and has 100 sectors per track. The transfer time for one sector is ………\",\"o\":[[\"10 ms\",\"10 ms = 60,000/6000 is one full revolution (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"5 ms\",\"5 ms is the average latency (half a revolution), not the transfer time (L2).\"],[\"0.05 ms\",\"0.05 ms halves the transfer time; the ½ belongs only to the average latency (L2).\"],[\"0.1 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60,000/6000 = 10 ms; transfer = revolution / sectors per track = 10/100 = 0.1 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Average seek = 10 ms, spindle speed = 6000 rpm, 100 sectors per track. Average time to read one sector = ………\",\"o\":[[\"20.1 ms\",\"20.1 uses a full revolution (10 ms) as the latency; the average latency is half: 5 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"15.1 ms\",\"Correct. Latency = ½ × 10 = 5 ms; transfer = 10/100 = 0.1 ms; total = 10 + 5 + 0.1 = 15.1 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"10.1 ms\",\"10.1 leaves out the rotational delay (L2).\"],[\"15 ms\",\"15 leaves out the transfer time of 0.1 ms (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Average seek = 12 ms, average rotational delay = 4 ms, spindle speed = 7500 rpm, 32 sectors per track. Average time to read 2 sectors = ………\",\"o\":[[\"16.25 ms\",\"16.25 transfers only one sector (0.25 ms); two sectors take 0.5 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"20.5 ms\",\"20.5 uses a full revolution (8 ms) as the delay; the average rotational delay is given as 4 ms (L2).\"],[\"16.5 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60,000/7500 = 8 ms; transfer = 2 × 8/32 = 0.5 ms; total = 12 + 4 + 0.5 = 16.5 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"12.5 ms\",\"12.5 leaves out the rotational delay (L2).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A track holds 24,000 bytes and the disk spins at 3000 rpm. The transfer time for 6000 bytes is ………\",\"o\":[[\"20 ms\",\"20 ms = 60,000/3000 is one full revolution, the time to transfer the whole track (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"10 ms\",\"10 ms is the average latency (half a revolution) (L2).\"],[\"0.25 ms\",\"0.25 is the fraction of the track (6000/24,000), not a time; multiply it by the revolution time (L2).\"],[\"5 ms\",\"Correct. Revolution = 60,000/3000 = 20 ms; transfer = (6000/24,000) × 20 = 5 ms (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A disk has 12 surfaces, 2000 tracks per surface, 30 sectors per track and 512 bytes per sector. Disk capacity = ………\",\"o\":[[\"368,640,000 B\",\"Correct. Capacity = surfaces × tracks/surface × sectors/track × bytes/sector = 12 × 2000 × 30 × 512 = 368,640,000 B (L2, Quiz 2).\"],[\"30,720,000 B\",\"30,720,000 = 2000 × 30 × 512 is only one surface (L2).\"],[\"184,320,000 B\",\"184,320,000 uses 6 (platters) instead of 12 surfaces (L2).\"],[\"737,280,000 B\",\"737,280,000 counts 24 surfaces, doubling the 12 given surfaces (L2).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A disk has an average latency of 5 ms. What is its spindle speed?\",\"o\":[[\"12,000 rpm\",\"12,000 = 60,000/5 treats 5 ms as a full revolution; it is only half (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"6000 rpm\",\"Correct. Average latency = ½ revolution, so revolution = 10 ms and RPM = 60,000/10 = 6000 (L2, Cost of a disk access).\"],[\"3000 rpm\",\"3000 rpm gives a 20 ms revolution and a 10 ms average latency (L2).\"],[\"5000 rpm\",\"5000 rpm gives a 12 ms revolution and a 6 ms average latency (L2).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Tracks are organized by 512 B sectors and records of 200 B cannot be split between sectors. How many bytes are lost to fragmentation in each full sector?\",\"o\":[[\"312 B\",\"312 = 512 − 200 assumes only one record per sector, but two 200 B records fit (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"0 B\",\"512 is not a multiple of 200, so some space is lost: that is fragmentation (L2).\"],[\"112 B\",\"Correct. ⌊512/200⌋ = 2 records = 400 B, so 512 − 400 = 112 B are lost in each sector (L2, Sectors vs blocks).\"],[\"212 B\",\"212 B is the waste for 300 B records (512 − 300), the lecture's example, not for 200 B records (L2).\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":3,\"title\":\"Logical File Organization: Fields and Records\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"File input and output\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Text file</b>: a sequence of characters (e.g. C source code), viewable in a text editor.\",\"<b>Binary file</b>: a sequence of bytes (e.g. movie, music), which needs a specialized program.\",\"Files can be <b>input</b> files (the program reads) or <b>output</b> files (the program writes).\"]},{\"h\":\"File of records\",\"pts\":[\"The logical view: data is a collection of <b>records</b>.\",\"<b>Record</b>: a group of fields forming a logical unit.\",\"<b>Field</b>: a data value, the smallest unit of data with logical meaning. Fields have names, types, lengths and values.\",\"Three file structures: <b>stream</b>, <b>field</b> and <b>record</b> structures.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-file-records\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-file-records\":\"The logical view: a file is records, a record is fields\"}},{\"h\":\"1. Stream files\",\"pts\":[\"Information is written as a stream of bytes containing <b>no added information</b>.\",\"Example: <code>AmesMary123 MapleStillwaterOK74075MasonAlan90 EastgateAdaOK74820</code>.\",\"Problem: there is <b>no way to get the information back</b> in the organized record format.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-stream-file\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-stream-file\":\"A stream file: bytes with no added information\"}},{\"h\":\"2. Field structures (4 ways)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Fixed length</b>: e.g. lengths 5, 7, 25 → <code>87358CARROLLALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>, <code>03818FOLK&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FILE STRUCTURES</code>.\",\"<b>Length indicator</b> at the start of each field → <code>058735907CARROLL19ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>.\",\"<b>keyword = value</b> → <code>ISBN=87359 AU=CARROLL TI=ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>.\",\"<b>Delimiter</b> at the end of each field → <code>87359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND|</code>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-field-structs\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-field-structs\":\"The four field structures, byte by byte: edit the fields and read them back\"}},{\"h\":\"Field structures: advantages and disadvantages\",\"table\":[[\"Type\",\"Advantage\",\"Disadvantage\"],[\"Fixed\",\"Easy to read/store\",\"Wastes space with padding\"],[\"Length indicator\",\"Easy to jump ahead to the end of the field\",\"Long fields need more than 1 byte to store the length (max size > 256)\"],[\"Delimited\",\"May waste less space than length-based\",\"Must check every byte of the field against the delimiter\"],[\"Keyword\",\"Fields are self-describing; allows missing fields\",\"Wastes space with keywords\"]]},{\"h\":\"3. Record structures\",\"pts\":[\"A record is a set of fields that belong together at a higher level of organization. It is a conceptual tool and an important logical notion in the file's structure.\",\"Methods:<br>(1) <b>fixed-length records with fixed-length fields</b><br>(2) <b>fixed-length records with variable-length fields</b> (delimited fields or fields with length indicators, padded to a fixed record size)<br>(3) <b>each record begins with a length indicator</b> (byte count), e.g. <code>3387359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-record-structs\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-record-structs\":\"The three record structures, and jumping to the i-th record\"}},{\"h\":\"Record structures: advantages and disadvantages\",\"table\":[[\"Type\",\"Advantage\",\"Disadvantage\"],[\"Fixed-length record\",\"Easy to jump to the i-th record\",\"Wastes space with padding\"],[\"Variable-length record\",\"Saves space when record sizes are diverse\",\"Cannot jump to the i-th record unless through an index file\"]]},{\"h\":\"Managing files of records\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Primary key</b>: uniquely identifies a record and does not change.<br><b>Secondary key</b>: used for searching but does not typically identify a record uniquely.\",\"<b>Sequential search</b>: read records in order until a match. <b>O(n)</b>. Appropriate for pattern matching or files with few records.\",\"<b>Direct access</b>: seek directly to the start of the record. <b>O(1)</b>. Possible when we know the <b>RRN</b> (Relative Record Number: the first record has RRN 0, the next RRN 1…).\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-access\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-access\":2},\"figH\":{\"fp-access\":\"Sequential search O(n) vs direct access O(1) with the RRN\"}},{\"h\":\"Seeking\",\"pts\":[\"Generic: <code>Seek(Source_File, Offset)</code>; e.g. <code>Seek(infile, 3030)</code> moves to byte 3030.\",\"C: <code>int fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int origin)</code>, where origin:<br>• <b>0</b> = beginning<br>• <b>1</b> = current<br>• <b>2</b> = end<br>• <code>fseek(infile,0L,0)</code> → start<br>• <code>fseek(infile,0L,2)</code> → end<br>• <code>fseek(infile,-10L,1)</code> → back 10 bytes.\",\"C++: an fstream has two pointers. <code>seekg</code> moves the <b>get</b> (read) pointer and <code>seekp</code> moves the <b>put</b> (write) pointer. Origins: <code>ios::beg</code>, <code>ios::cur</code>, <code>ios::end</code>.<br><code>infile.seekg(0, ios::beg); infile.seekg(0, ios::end); infile.seekg(-10, ios::cur);</code>\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-seek\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-seek\":\"Seeking: fseek in C, seekg and seekp in C++\"}},{\"h\":\"Quiz (3) Q1: copy a file with no loops\",\"code\":\"#include <fstream>\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main() {\\n    ifstream infile(\\\"d:\\\\\\\\IFile.txt\\\", ios::binary);\\n    ofstream outfile(\\\"d:\\\\\\\\OFile.txt\\\", ios::binary);\\n    infile.seekg(0, infile.end);      // jump to end\\n    long size = infile.tellg();       // position = file size\\n    infile.seekg(0);                  // back to start\\n    char* buffer = new char[size];\\n    infile.read(buffer, size);        // read everything at once\\n    outfile.write(buffer, size);      // write everything at once\\n    delete[] buffer;\\n    outfile.close(); infile.close();\\n    return 0;\\n}\",\"pts\":[\"The trick: <code>seekg</code> to the end and <code>tellg</code> gives the size, then one <code>read</code> and one <code>write</code> of that size.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-copy-file\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-copy-file\":\"Copying a whole file with one read and one write\"}},{\"h\":\"Quiz (3) Q2: update a record by RRN\",\"code\":\"struct Item { char desc[31]; int qty; float price; };\\n\\nvoid UpdateRecord(fstream &file, Item &newItem, int RRN) {\\n    file.seekp(RRN * sizeof(newItem), ios::beg); // byte offset = RRN × record size\\n    file.write((char*)&newItem, sizeof(newItem)); // overwrite the old record\\n}\",\"pts\":[\"Fixed-length fields and records mean every record has the same size, so the byte offset is exactly <b>RRN × record size</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-update-rrn\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-update-rrn\":\"Updating a record in place: seekp to RRN × record size, then write\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"Text vs binary file\",\"Text: sequence of characters (editable). Binary: sequence of bytes (needs a special program).\"],[\"Field\",\"The smallest unit of data with logical meaning.\"],[\"Record\",\"A group of fields forming a logical unit.\"],[\"Stream file problem\",\"No added info, so records can't be recovered in organized form.\"],[\"4 field structures\",\"Fixed length, length indicator, keyword=value, delimiter.\"],[\"Fixed field: +/−\",\"+ easy to read/store; − wastes space with padding.\"],[\"Delimited field: +/−\",\"+ may waste less space; − must check every byte against the delimiter.\"],[\"Keyword field: +/−\",\"+ self-describing, allows missing fields; − wastes space with keywords.\"],[\"Length-indicator field: +/−\",\"+ easy to jump to field end; − long fields need >1 byte for the length.\"],[\"Fixed vs variable-length record\",\"Fixed: easy to jump to the i-th record but wastes padding. Variable: saves space but needs an index to jump.\"],[\"RRN\",\"Relative Record Number; the first record = 0. Offset = RRN × record size.\"],[\"fseek origins\",\"0 = beginning, 1 = current, 2 = end.\"],[\"seekg vs seekp\",\"seekg moves the get (read) pointer; seekp moves the put (write) pointer.\"],[\"Sequential vs direct access\",\"Sequential O(n); direct O(1).\"]],\"qa\":[[\"\\\"There are many ways of adding structure to files to maintain the identity of fields.\\\" Mention them with advantages and disadvantages.\",\"Fixed length (+ easy to read/store, − padding waste); length indicator (+ easy to jump to the end of the field, − long fields need more than 1 byte for the length); delimited (+ may waste less space, − must check every byte for the delimiter); keyword=value (+ self-describing and allows missing fields, − keywords waste space).\"],[\"Consider a fixed-length field, fixed-length record file. Write UpdateRecord given the RRN.\",\"void UpdateRecord(fstream &file, Item &newItem, int RRN){ file.seekp(RRN*sizeof(newItem), ios::beg); file.write((char*)&newItem, sizeof(newItem)); }\"],[\"When is sequential search useful?\",\"For pattern matching or files with few records. Otherwise it is O(n), so direct access (O(1) with the RRN) is preferred.\"],[\"Differentiate between primary and secondary keys.\",\"A primary key uniquely identifies a record and does not change. A secondary key is used to search but does not typically identify a record uniquely.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"In a stream file structure, information is written as a stream of bytes containing:\",\"o\":[[\"No added information\",\"Correct. That is why records can't be recovered.\"],[\"No space\",\"Spaces may exist inside data; that is not the definition.\"],[\"Added information\",\"Added information (delimiters, lengths) is what field structures add.\"],[\"Space\",\"\\\"Space\\\" is not part of the definition; a stream file is a stream of bytes with NO added information, which is why records cannot be recovered (L3, Stream files).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"Which field structure is \\\"self-describing and allows missing fields\\\"?\",\"o\":[[\"Fixed length\",\"Position defines the field; missing fields leave padding.\"],[\"Length indicator\",\"A length indicator only says how many bytes the field has; it does not name the field, so it is not self-describing (L3, Field structures).\"],[\"Delimited\",\"Delimited fields are separated by a symbol such as |, but nothing names each field and a missing field still needs its delimiter (L3, Field structures).\"],[\"Keyword = value\",\"Correct. Each field names itself (ISBN=…, AU=…).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"The main disadvantage of delimited fields is:\",\"o\":[[\"Waste space with padding\",\"Wasting space with padding is the disadvantage of FIXED-length fields; delimiters cost a byte-by-byte check instead (L3, Field structures table).\"],[\"Have to check every byte of the field against the delimiter\",\"Correct. With a delimiter at the end of each field, the program must compare every byte of the field with the delimiter to find where it ends (L3, Field structures table).\"],[\"Waste space with keywords\",\"Wasting space with keywords is the disadvantage of KEYWORD = VALUE fields, whose advantage is being self-describing (L3, Field structures table).\"],[\"Long fields need more than 1 byte for length\",\"Needing more than one byte for long lengths is the disadvantage of LENGTH-INDICATOR fields (max size > 256), not delimited ones (L3).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A disadvantage of variable-length records is:\",\"o\":[[\"Wasted space with padding\",\"Wasted padding is the disadvantage of FIXED-length records; variable-length records actually save space when sizes are diverse (L3, Record structures table).\"],[\"You cannot jump to the i-th record unless through an index file\",\"Correct. Because records have different sizes, you cannot compute where record i starts, so you cannot jump to it without an index file (L3, Record structures table).\"],[\"They cannot store text\",\"Variable-length records can hold any data including text (e.g. 87359|CARROLL|ALICE…); their weakness is no direct jump to record i (L3).\"],[\"They require fixed-length fields\",\"Variable-length records usually use variable fields (delimiters or length indicators); fixed fields are not a requirement (L3, Record structures).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Direct access has the complexity:\",\"o\":[[\"O(n)\",\"O(n) is SEQUENTIAL search, reading records in order until a match; direct access seeks straight to the record (L3, Managing files of records).\"],[\"O(1)\",\"Correct. Direct access seeks straight to the start of the record (offset known from its RRN), so the cost does not depend on n: O(1) (L3, Managing files of records).\"],[\"Log(n)\",\"log n is the cost of BINARY search on a sorted file; direct access needs one seek, O(1) (L3–L4).\"],[\"All answers correct\",\"The three costs belong to different methods: O(n) sequential, log n binary search, O(1) direct access, so they cannot all be right (L3).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm Model B\"},{\"q\":\"Sequential access has the complexity:\",\"o\":[[\"O(n)\",\"Correct. Sequential search reads records in order until it finds a match, so in the worst case it reads all n records: O(n) (L3, Managing files of records).\"],[\"O(1)\",\"O(1) is DIRECT access by RRN (one seek); sequential access may have to read every record (L3).\"],[\"Log(n)\",\"log n is BINARY search on a file sorted by key; sequential search is O(n) (L3–L4).\"],[\"All answers correct\",\"Only O(n) describes sequential access; O(1) is direct access and log n is binary search (L3).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm Model A\"},{\"q\":\"A binary search has the complexity:\",\"o\":[[\"O(n)\",\"O(n) is linear (sequential) search, which reads records one by one; binary search halves the range each step (L4, Ordered files).\"],[\"O(1)\",\"O(1) is direct access by RRN with a single seek; binary search needs about log₂ n probes (L3).\"],[\"O(log₂ n)\",\"Correct. Binary search compares the key with the middle record and discards half the remaining range each step, so it needs about log₂ n accesses (L4, Ordered files).\"],[\"O(n²)\",\"O(n²) would be slower than even sequential search; binary search on a sorted file is O(log₂ n) (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"fseek(infile, -10L, 1) does what?\",\"o\":[[\"Moves to byte 10 from the beginning\",\"Origin 1 is CURRENT, and the offset is negative.\"],[\"Moves back 10 bytes from the current position\",\"Correct. In fseek(stream, offset, origin), origin 1 = current position, and offset −10L moves 10 bytes backwards from there (L3, Seeking).\"],[\"Moves 10 bytes before the end\",\"10 bytes before the end would be fseek(infile, -10L, 2); origin 2 is the end, but this call uses origin 1 (current) (L3, Seeking).\"],[\"Closes the file\",\"fseek only moves the file pointer; closing the file is done with fclose (L3, Seeking).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In C++, which function moves the PUT (write) pointer?\",\"o\":[[\"seekg\",\"seekg moves the GET (read) pointer; the put (write) pointer is moved with seekp (L3, Seeking).\"],[\"seekp\",\"Correct. An fstream has two pointers: seekg moves the get (read) pointer and seekp moves the put (write) pointer (L3, Seeking).\"],[\"tellg\",\"tellg only REPORTS the current get (read) position, e.g. to find the file size; it moves nothing (L3, Quiz 3).\"],[\"fseek\",\"fseek is the C function for moving the file pointer; the question asks for the C++ put-pointer function, seekp (L3).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Fixed-length records of 40 bytes. Byte offset of the record with RRN 5?\",\"o\":[[\"5\",\"5 is the RRN itself; the byte offset is RRN × record size = 5 × 40 = 200 (L3, Direct access).\"],[\"200\",\"Correct. RRNs start at 0, so byte offset = RRN × record size = 5 × 40 = 200 (L3, Direct access).\"],[\"240\",\"240 = 6 × 40 treats RRN as 1-based; the first record has RRN 0, so the offset is 5 × 40 = 200 (L3).\"],[\"45\",\"45 = 5 + 40 adds instead of multiplying; offset = RRN × record size = 200 (L3).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Easy to jump ahead to the end of the field, it's advantage of ....\",\"o\":[[\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"Correct. L3 field-structures table: with a length indicator each field starts with its length (e.g. 05 87359 07 CARROLL …), so the program reads the length and skips straight to the end of the field. Disadvantage: long fields need more than 1 byte to store the length.\"],[\"Keyword field structure\",\"Keyword = value's advantage is that fields are self-describing and may be missing; it gives no length to jump with (L3).\"],[\"Fixed field structure\",\"Fixed fields' advantage is \\\"easy to read/store\\\"; the jump-ahead advantage is listed for the length indicator (L3).\"],[\"Delimiter field structure\",\"Delimiter is the opposite: you must check every byte to find the delimiter, so you cannot jump ahead (L3).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Waste space with padding, it's disadvantage of ....\",\"o\":[[\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"Length indicator's disadvantage is that long fields need more than 1 byte for the length; there is no padding (L3).\"],[\"Delimiter field structure\",\"Delimiter's disadvantage is checking every byte for the delimiter; it may waste LESS space (L3).\"],[\"Fixed field structure\",\"Correct. L3 field-structures table: in a FIXED-length structure every field has a set size, so a short value (a 3-letter name in a 10-byte field) is padded with blanks, and the padding is wasted space. Advantage: easy to read/store.\"],[\"Keyword field structure\",\"Keyword's disadvantage is the space taken by the keywords themselves; there is no padding (L3).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Fields are self describing, allows for missing fields, it's advantage of ....\",\"o\":[[\"Fixed field structure\",\"Fixed fields are known only by position: not self-describing, and a missing field still takes its padded space (L3).\"],[\"Keyword field structure\",\"Correct. L3 field-structures table: in keyword = value (e.g. last=Ames|first=Mary|city=Stillwater|) each field carries its own name, so fields are self-describing, and a field with no value can simply be left out. Disadvantage: the keywords waste space.\"],[\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"A length indicator says how many bytes a field has, not which field it is (L3).\"],[\"Delimiter field structure\",\"Delimited fields are separated by a symbol and known only by their order, so they are not self-describing (L3).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Have to check every byte of field, it's disadvantage of ....\",\"o\":[[\"Keyword field structure\",\"Keyword's disadvantage is the space taken by the keywords; its advantage is self-describing fields (L3).\"],[\"Fixed field structure\",\"Fixed fields' disadvantage is padding waste; field ends are at known positions, so no byte checking (L3).\"],[\"Delimiter field structure\",\"Correct. L3 field-structures table: in a DELIMITED structure fields are separated by a special character (87359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND|), so to find the end of a field the program must check every byte for the delimiter. Advantage: may waste less space (no padding).\"],[\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"Length indicator's disadvantage is that long fields need more than 1 byte for the length; its advantage is the opposite: jumping straight to the end of the field (L3).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A book record has ISBN = <code>52814</code>, author = <code>KNUTH</code>, title = <code>SORTING</code>. Which is the correct encoding using a 2-digit <b>length indicator</b> at the start of each field?\",\"o\":[[\"<code>52814|KNUTH|SORTING|</code>\",\"That is the delimiter method, not length indicators.\"],[\"<code>ISBN=52814 AU=KNUTH TI=SORTING</code>\",\"ISBN=… AU=… TI=… is the keyword = value method; length indicators put the field length before each field (05 52814…) (L3).\"],[\"<code>055281405KNUTH07SORTING</code>\",\"Correct. Each field is preceded by its length: 05 + 52814, 05 + KNUTH, 07 + SORTING.\"],[\"<code>055281405KNUTH06SORTING</code>\",\"SORTING has 7 characters, not 6. The length was miscounted.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Records use delimited fields and each record begins with a length indicator (byte count), as in the slide example <code>3387359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>. What is the length indicator for the record <code>52814|KNUTH|SORTING</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"17\",\"This counted only the data characters and left out the two | delimiters.\"],[\"20\",\"This added a trailing delimiter that the record does not have.\"],[\"3\",\"That is the number of fields, not the byte count.\"],[\"19\",\"Correct. 5 + 1 + 5 + 1 + 7 = 19 bytes, just as the slide counts 5 + 1 + 7 + 1 + 19 = 33.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Fixed-length records are written from <code>struct Student { char name[24]; int id; float gpa; };</code> (assume int and float are 4 bytes each, no padding). What is the byte offset of the record with RRN 12?\",\"o\":[[\"384\",\"Correct. Record size = 24 + 4 + 4 = 32 B; offset = RRN × record size = 12 × 32 = 384.\"],[\"416\",\"That is 13 × 32, which treats RRNs as starting at 1. The first record has RRN 0.\"],[\"352\",\"That is 11 × 32, an off-by-one in the other direction.\"],[\"12\",\"12 is the RRN, not the byte offset; the offset is RRN × record size = 12 × 32 = 384 (L3, Direct access).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"An <code>fstream file</code> holds fixed-length 50-byte records. Which call positions the file so that the next <code>write</code> overwrites the record with RRN 7?\",\"o\":[[\"<code>file.seekg(350, ios::beg);</code>\",\"seekg moves the GET (read) pointer. Writing uses the put pointer.\"],[\"<code>file.seekp(350, ios::beg);</code>\",\"Correct. seekp moves the put (write) pointer, and 7 × 50 = 350 bytes from the beginning.\"],[\"<code>file.seekp(400, ios::beg);</code>\",\"400 = 8 × 50, which treats RRN as 1-based and lands on the next record.\"],[\"<code>file.seekp(350, ios::end);</code>\",\"The offset must be measured from the beginning (ios::beg), not from the end.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A file holds fixed-length 20-byte records. After <code>fseek(fp, -60L, 2);</code> the program reads one 20-byte record. Which record is read?\",\"o\":[[\"The record with RRN 3\",\"Origin 2 means the END of the file, not the beginning.\"],[\"The last record\",\"The last record starts 20 bytes before the end, not 60.\"],[\"The third record from the end\",\"Correct. Origin 2 = end; moving back 60 bytes = 3 records of 20 bytes, so the read starts at the third-last record.\"],[\"The second record from the end\",\"The second record from the end starts 2 × 20 = 40 bytes before the end, i.e. fseek(fp, -40L, 2); −60L reaches the third-last (L3).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A secondary key uniquely identifies a record and does not change.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That is the definition of a PRIMARY key; a secondary key is used for searching and does not typically identify a record uniquely (L3).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A secondary key is used for searching but does not typically identify a record uniquely.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the main ADVANTAGE of fixed-length records?\",\"o\":[[\"They save space when record sizes are diverse\",\"That is the advantage of variable-length records.\"],[\"Fields are self-describing\",\"That is the advantage of keyword = value fields.\"],[\"They never need padding\",\"Fixed-length records waste space with padding; that is their disadvantage.\"],[\"It is easy to jump to the i-th record\",\"Correct. Every record has the same size, so the offset is RRN × record size.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which field structure makes it easy to jump ahead to the end of a field?\",\"o\":[[\"Length indicator\",\"Correct. The length stored at the start tells you exactly how many bytes to skip.\"],[\"Delimited\",\"With delimiters you must check every byte until you hit the delimiter.\"],[\"Keyword = value\",\"Its advantage is being self-describing and allowing missing fields.\"],[\"Stream\",\"A stream file adds no information at all, so there are no field boundaries to jump to; the length indicator gives that (L3, Stream files).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A music file is best described as:\",\"o\":[[\"A text file, because it is a sequence of characters\",\"Text files (like C source code) can be viewed in a text editor; music cannot.\"],[\"A binary file, a sequence of bytes that needs a specialized program\",\"Correct. The slides give movies and music as binary file examples.\"],[\"A stream file of records\",\"\\\"Stream\\\" is a logical file structure, not the text/binary classification.\"],[\"An input file only\",\"Whether a file is input or output depends on how the program uses it.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the \\\"copy a file with no loops\\\" program, after <code>infile.seekg(0, infile.end);</code> the call <code>infile.tellg()</code> gives the size of the file in bytes.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The get pointer is at the end, so its position equals the number of bytes; one read and one write of that size then copy the file.\"],[\"False\",\"tellg reports the current get position, and at the end of the file that position is the file size.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"……… is the smallest unit of data with logical meaning.\",\"o\":[[\"Field\",\"Correct. A field is a data value, the smallest unit of data with logical meaning (L3, File of records)\"],[\"Record\",\"A record is a group of fields forming a logical unit, so it is bigger than a field.\"],[\"Stream\",\"A stream is a sequence of bytes with no added information, not a logical unit.\"],[\"Primary key\",\"A primary key is a field used to identify a record uniquely, not the definition of the smallest unit.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A ……… is a group of fields forming a logical unit.\",\"o\":[[\"Stream file\",\"A stream file has no added information, so it does not keep fields grouped.\"],[\"Record\",\"Correct. A record is a group of fields forming a logical unit (L3, File of records)\"],[\"Binary file\",\"A binary file is a sequence of bytes such as a movie or music.\"],[\"Delimiter\",\"A delimiter is a mark at the end of each field, not a group of fields.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: A text file is a sequence of bytes, such as a movie, that needs a specialized program.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That is the definition of a binary file, not a text file.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A text file is a sequence of characters (e.g. C source code) viewable in a text editor; the bytes-plus-special-program description is a binary file (L3, File input and output)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The three file structures discussed in the lecture are:\",\"o\":[[\"Text, binary and stream\",\"Text and binary are kinds of files, not the three file structures.\"],[\"Fixed, delimited and keyword\",\"These are field structures (ways to keep fields apart), not the three file structures.\"],[\"Stream, field and record\",\"Correct. The lecture lists stream, field and record structures (L3, File of records)\"],[\"Input, output and index\",\"Input and output describe how a program uses a file, and an index file is used for variable-length records.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The main problem of a stream file such as <code>AmesMary123 MapleStillwaterOK74075...</code> is:\",\"o\":[[\"It wastes space with keywords\",\"That is the disadvantage of keyword = value fields; a stream file adds nothing.\"],[\"It wastes space with padding\",\"Padding waste belongs to fixed-length fields and records.\"],[\"It must check every byte against the delimiter\",\"That is the disadvantage of delimited fields; a stream file has no delimiters.\"],[\"There is no way to get the information back in the organized record format\",\"Correct. The bytes carry no added information, so the fields and records cannot be recovered (L3, Stream files)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In a stream file it is easy to recover each record in its organized form, because each field is followed by a separator.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"A stream file adds no separators or any other information between fields.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A stream file contains no added information, so there is no way to get the records back in organized form (L3, Stream files)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The field encoding <code>ISBN=40211 AU=KNUTH TI=SORTING</code> uses which field structure?\",\"o\":[[\"Keyword = value\",\"Correct. Every field is written as keyword=value, so it is self-describing (L3, Field structures)\"],[\"Length indicator\",\"A length indicator would put the field length (e.g. <code>05</code>) before each value.\"],[\"Delimiter\",\"A delimited encoding ends each field with a mark such as <code>|</code>, with no keywords.\"],[\"Fixed length\",\"Fixed-length fields would be padded to set lengths with no keywords.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The record <code>054021105KNUTH07SORTING</code> stores its fields using:\",\"o\":[[\"Fixed-length fields\",\"Fixed-length fields have no counts in front; they are padded to set lengths.\"],[\"Delimiters at the end of each field\",\"There is no delimiter such as <code>|</code> between the fields.\"],[\"A length indicator at the start of each field\",\"Correct. <code>05</code>, <code>05</code> and <code>07</code> give the length of the field that follows (L3, Field structures)\"],[\"Keyword = value\",\"There are no keywords such as <code>ISBN=</code> in the record.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The main ADVANTAGE of fixed-length fields is:\",\"o\":[[\"Fields are self-describing\",\"Self-describing fields are the advantage of keyword = value.\"],[\"They may waste less space than length-based fields\",\"That is the advantage of delimited fields.\"],[\"It is easy to jump ahead to the end of the field\",\"That is the advantage of the length indicator.\"],[\"They are easy to read and store\",\"Correct. Fixed-length fields are easy to read/store, but waste space with padding (L3, Field structures: advantages and disadvantages)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Wastes space with keywords\\\" is the disadvantage of:\",\"o\":[[\"Delimited fields\",\"Delimited fields must check every byte against the delimiter; they carry no keywords.\"],[\"Keyword = value fields\",\"Correct. Writing a keyword with every field wastes space (L3, Field structures: advantages and disadvantages)\"],[\"Fixed-length fields\",\"Fixed-length fields waste space with padding, not keywords.\"],[\"Length-indicator fields\",\"Their disadvantage is that long fields need more than 1 byte to store the length.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: With length-indicator fields, a field whose size can exceed 256 needs more than 1 byte to store its length.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is the stated disadvantage of the length indicator: long fields need more than 1 byte for the length (L3, Field structures: advantages and disadvantages)\"],[\"False\",\"It is true: one byte is not enough for the length of such long fields.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Delimited fields may waste less space than length-based fields.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. That is the advantage of delimited fields; the cost is checking every byte against the delimiter (L3, Field structures: advantages and disadvantages)\"],[\"False\",\"It is true; the lecture lists this as the advantage of delimited fields.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the slide's fixed-length field example, the field lengths are 5, 7 and 25. How many bytes does each record take?\",\"o\":[[\"35\",\"35 drops 2 bytes; the sum 5 + 7 + 25 is 37.\"],[\"25\",\"25 is only the title field length.\"],[\"37\",\"Correct. 5 + 7 + 25 = 37 bytes per record, whatever the real data length (L3, Field structures)\"],[\"3\",\"3 is the number of fields, not the number of bytes.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"With fixed field lengths 5, 7 and 25, the author <code>FOLK</code> is stored in its 7-byte field followed by how many padding spaces?\",\"o\":[[\"4\",\"4 is the length of FOLK itself, not the padding.\"],[\"7\",\"7 is the whole field size.\"],[\"0\",\"FOLK is shorter than 7 bytes, so it must be padded.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. 7 − 4 = 3 spaces, as in <code>03818FOLK&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FILE STRUCTURES</code> (L3, Field structures)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Using a 2-digit length indicator at the start of each field, the title <code>FILE STRUCTURES</code> is stored as:\",\"o\":[[\"<code>15FILE STRUCTURES</code>\",\"Correct. FILE (4) + space (1) + STRUCTURES (10) = 15 characters (L3, Field structures)\"],[\"<code>14FILE STRUCTURES</code>\",\"14 forgets to count the space, which is part of the field.\"],[\"<code>FILE STRUCTURES|</code>\",\"That is a delimited field, not a length indicator.\"],[\"<code>TI=FILE STRUCTURES</code>\",\"That is keyword = value, not a length indicator.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Each record begins with a length indicator (byte count) and its fields are delimited, as in <code>3387359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>. What is the length indicator for <code>03818|FOLK|FILE STRUCTURES</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"24\",\"24 leaves out the two delimiters <code>|</code>.\"],[\"26\",\"Correct. 5 + 1 + 4 + 1 + 15 = 26 bytes, counted like the slide's 33 (L3, Record structures)\"],[\"37\",\"37 is the fixed-length record size, not this variable-length record.\"],[\"33\",\"33 is the length of the slide's CARROLL record, not this one.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Records are given a fixed size, but the fields inside are delimited and the rest of the record is padded. This record structure is:\",\"o\":[[\"Fixed-length records with fixed-length fields\",\"Here the fields are delimited, so they are variable-length.\"],[\"Each record begins with a length indicator\",\"In that method the record size varies and is given by a byte count at its start.\"],[\"Fixed-length records with variable-length fields\",\"Correct. Delimited or length-indicator fields are padded up to the fixed record size (L3, Record structures)\"],[\"Stream file\",\"A stream file adds no delimiters and no padding.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In the record <code>3387359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code>, the leading <code>33</code> is:\",\"o\":[[\"The ISBN of the book\",\"The ISBN is <code>87359</code>, which follows the 33.\"],[\"The RRN of the record\",\"The RRN is the record's position number, not stored at its start.\"],[\"The number of fields\",\"The record has only 3 fields.\"],[\"The record length (byte count)\",\"Correct. Each record begins with a length indicator giving its byte count (L3, Record structures)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: Variable-length records save space when record sizes are diverse.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. That is the advantage of variable-length records; the drawback is needing an index file to jump to the i-th record (L3, Record structures: advantages and disadvantages)\"],[\"False\",\"It is true; it is the advantage the lecture gives for variable-length records.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: With fixed-length records you cannot jump to the i-th record unless you use an index file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"That is the disadvantage of variable-length records, not fixed-length ones.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Fixed-length records make it easy to jump to the i-th record (offset = RRN × record size); the index file is needed for variable-length records (L3, Record structures: advantages and disadvantages)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A key that uniquely identifies a record and does not change is the:\",\"o\":[[\"Secondary key\",\"A secondary key is used for searching but does not typically identify a record uniquely.\"],[\"Primary key\",\"Correct. A primary key uniquely identifies a record and does not change (L3, Managing files of records)\"],[\"RRN\",\"The RRN is the record's position (0, 1, 2...), not a key field.\"],[\"Length indicator\",\"A length indicator stores a field or record size.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Sequential search is appropriate for:\",\"o\":[[\"Pattern matching or files with few records\",\"Correct. Sequential search is O(n), so it suits pattern matching or small files (L3, Managing files of records)\"],[\"Very large files where we know the RRN\",\"When the RRN is known, direct access (O(1)) is used instead.\"],[\"Jumping to the i-th fixed-length record\",\"Jumping to a record is done by direct access with offset = RRN × record size.\"],[\"Variable-length records that have an index file\",\"With an index file we can jump directly, without reading every record.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"True or False: Direct access is possible when we know the RRN of the record.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Direct access seeks straight to the start of the record, O(1), when the RRN is known (L3, Managing files of records)\"],[\"False\",\"It is true; knowing the RRN is exactly what makes direct access possible.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The RRN of the <b>first</b> record in a file is:\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"Counting starts at 0, so RRN 1 is the second record.\"],[\"The record size\",\"The record size is used to compute the offset, not the RRN.\"],[\"0\",\"Correct. The first record has RRN 0, the next RRN 1, and so on (L3, Managing files of records)\"],[\"The primary key value\",\"The primary key identifies the record's content; the RRN is its position, starting at 0.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Using the Quiz (3) record <code>struct Item { char desc[31]; int qty; float price; };</code> (assume int and float are 4 bytes each, no padding), what is the byte offset of the record with RRN 4?\",\"o\":[[\"124\",\"124 is 31 × 4, the desc array only, not the whole 39-byte record.\"],[\"195\",\"195 is 39 × 5, the offset of RRN 5 (it treats RRN 4 as the fifth step).\"],[\"39\",\"39 is the size of one record (the offset of RRN 1).\"],[\"156\",\"Correct. Record size = 31 + 4 + 4 = 39, offset = 4 × 39 = 156 (L3, Quiz (3) Q2)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A file holds fixed-length 60-byte records. A record starts at byte offset 1200. What is its RRN?\",\"o\":[[\"20\",\"Correct. RRN = offset / record size = 1200 / 60 = 20 (L3, Quiz (3) Q2)\"],[\"21\",\"21 counts records starting from 1; it is the 21st record but its RRN is 20.\"],[\"1140\",\"1140 is the offset of the previous record (RRN 19).\"],[\"72000\",\"72000 multiplies instead of dividing.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In C, the origin value in <code>fseek(infile, offset, origin)</code> that means the <b>end</b> of the file is:\",\"o\":[[\"0\",\"0 means the beginning of the file.\"],[\"1\",\"1 means the current position.\"],[\"2\",\"Correct. Origin 0 = beginning, 1 = current, 2 = end (L3, Seeking)\"],[\"3\",\"There is no origin 3; the only origins are 0, 1 and 2.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which call moves to the <b>start</b> of the file?\",\"o\":[[\"<code>fseek(infile,0L,2)</code>\",\"Origin 2 is the end, so this moves to the end.\"],[\"<code>fseek(infile,0L,0)</code>\",\"Correct. Offset 0 from origin 0 (beginning) is the start of the file (L3, Seeking)\"],[\"<code>fseek(infile,0L,1)</code>\",\"Origin 1 is the current position, so this does not move.\"],[\"<code>fseek(infile,-10L,1)</code>\",\"This moves back 10 bytes from the current position.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The C++ equivalent of <code>fseek(infile, -10L, 1)</code> is:\",\"o\":[[\"<code>infile.seekg(-10, ios::beg);</code>\",\"ios::beg corresponds to origin 0, the beginning.\"],[\"<code>infile.seekg(-10, ios::end);</code>\",\"ios::end corresponds to origin 2, the end.\"],[\"<code>infile.seekp(10, ios::cur);</code>\",\"This moves the put pointer forward 10 bytes, not back.\"],[\"<code>infile.seekg(-10, ios::cur);</code>\",\"Correct. Origin 1 (current) is ios::cur, and −10 moves back 10 bytes (L3, Seeking)\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"True or False: In C++, <code>seekg</code> moves the put (write) pointer of an fstream.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"seekg moves the get pointer; the put pointer is moved by seekp.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. seekg moves the get (read) pointer and seekp moves the put (write) pointer (L3, Seeking)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A file holds fixed-length 25-byte records. After <code>infile.seekg(0, ios::end);</code> the call <code>infile.tellg()</code> returns 500. How many records are in the file?\",\"o\":[[\"25\",\"25 is the record size, not the number of records.\"],[\"19\",\"19 would be the last RRN; the count is 20.\"],[\"20\",\"Correct. tellg at the end gives the file size: 500 / 25 = 20 records (L3, Quiz (3) Q1)\"],[\"12500\",\"12500 multiplies instead of dividing.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"True or False: In the \\\"copy a file with no loops\\\" program, <code>infile.seekg(0)</code> is called after <code>tellg</code> so that the single <code>read</code> starts from the beginning of the file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. After jumping to the end to get the size, the get pointer is moved back to the start, then one read and one write of that size (L3, Quiz (3) Q1)\"],[\"False\",\"It is true; without returning to the start, the read would begin at the end of the file.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In <code>UpdateRecord</code>, which call writes the new record over the old one?\",\"o\":[[\"<code>file.seekg(RRN * sizeof(newItem), ios::beg);</code>\",\"seekg moves the get (read) pointer, and it only positions, it does not write.\"],[\"<code>file.write((char*)&amp;newItem, sizeof(newItem));</code>\",\"Correct. After seekp positions the put pointer at RRN × record size, write overwrites the old record (L3, Quiz (3) Q2)\"],[\"<code>file.seekp(RRN * sizeof(newItem), ios::beg);</code>\",\"seekp only moves the put pointer to the record; it writes nothing.\"],[\"<code>file.tellg();</code>\",\"tellg only reports the current get position.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: A file that a program writes its results into is called an input file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"A file the program writes into is an output file.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Input files are read by the program; output files are written by it (L3, File input and output)\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"True or False: <code>Seek(infile, 3030)</code> moves to byte 3030 of the file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The generic Seek(Source_File, Offset) moves to the given byte offset (L3, Seeking)\"],[\"False\",\"It is true; it is the lecture's own generic example.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":4,\"title\":\"Ordered and Unordered (Heap / Sequential) Files\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Basic file organizations\",\"pts\":[\"A file organization method defines the <b>record placement strategy</b> and the relationship between a record's <b>key value</b> and its <b>relative address</b>.\",\"Goal: efficient <b>retrieve</b> (logical neighbour, records matching a condition, a random record) and <b>update</b> (insert, delete, modify).\",\"Efficiency is measured by the <b>number of disk accesses</b>: fewer is better.\",\"Access strategies:<br>• <b>sequential search</b> is slow and O(n)<br>• <b>direct access</b> gets the record in one read, O(1).\",\"Methods: <b>unordered (heap) file</b>, <b>ordered (sequential) file</b>, <b>indexing</b>, <b>hashing</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-access-strat\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-access-strat\":3},\"figH\":{\"fp-access-strat\":\"Sequential search vs direct access: counting disk accesses\"}},{\"h\":\"Unordered file / heap file\",\"pts\":[\"An <b>unordered</b> set of records stored in <b>order of entry</b>, regardless of key. Also called an unordered sequential file or <b>pile</b> file.\",\"Supports create/destroy, open/close, insert/delete. Records are identified by a <b>record id (rid)</b>.\",\"<b>Retrieve</b>: only <b>linear search</b>.\",\"<b>Insert at the end</b>: simple and efficient, with one access to read the last page and one to write it back.<br>Serious disadvantage: it <b>allows duplicate records</b> (e.g. inserting 4, Ali, CS at offset 80).\",\"<b>Delete</b>: find the record, delete it <b>physically or logically</b>, and write it back.\"]},{\"h\":\"Example heap file (20-byte records)\",\"table\":[[\"Offset\",\"St-no\",\"St-name\",\"St-Dept\"],[\"0\",\"5\",\"Ahmed\",\"CS\"],[\"20\",\"2\",\"Adam\",\"ITB\"],[\"40\",\"1\",\"Mona\",\"ITD\"],[\"60\",\"3\",\"Ali\",\"NW\"],[\"80\",\"4\",\"Ali\",\"CS  ← appended\"]],\"fig\":[\"fp-heap-file\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-heap-file\":\"The heap file: insert at the end, duplicates, and delete\"}},{\"h\":\"The cost model\",\"pts\":[\"• <b>B</b> = number of data pages (page = block)<br>• <b>R</b> = records per page<br>• <b>D</b> = average time to read/write a disk page<br>• <b>C</b> = average time to process a record.\",\"Operations:<br>• <b>Scan</b> (fetch all records)<br>• <b>equality search</b> (\\\"sid = 23\\\")<br>• <b>range search</b> (\\\"name after Smith\\\")<br>• <b>Insert</b><br>• <b>Delete</b> (by rid).\"]},{\"h\":\"Heap file costs\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Scan</b>: B(D + RC). Read B pages, and process R records on each.\",\"<b>Equality search</b>: 0.5B(D + RC). On average half the file is scanned. If nothing matches, the entire file is scanned.\",\"<b>Range search</b>: B(D + RC). Matches may be anywhere, so scan the whole file.\",\"<b>Insert</b>: <b>2D + C</b>. Fetch the last page, add the record, write it back.\",\"<b>Delete</b>: search cost + C + D.\"]},{\"h\":\"Ordered file / sequential file\",\"pts\":[\"An <b>ordered</b> set of records stored in order of the <b>key value</b>. Records are accessed sequentially from beginning to end.\",\"Insert: the new record goes into an exactly determined position, so <b>all records with a greater key move one location towards the end</b>. Delete and update need the opposite procedure.\",\"Example: inserting (4, Ali, CS) into 1, 2, 3, 5 places it at offset 60 and shifts Ahmed (5) to 80.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-sorted-insert\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-sorted-insert\":2},\"figH\":{\"fp-sorted-insert\":\"Inserting into an ordered file: every greater key shifts\"}},{\"h\":\"Sorted file costs\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Scan</b>: B(D + RC).\",\"<b>Equality search</b>: <b>D·log₂B + C·log₂R</b>. Binary search over the pages, then binary search inside the page.\",\"<b>Range search</b>: search cost + cost of retrieving the matching records.\",\"<b>Insert</b>: search cost + 2 × (0.5B(D + RC)) = search + B(D + RC). Read and rewrite half the file on average.\",\"<b>Delete</b>: same as insert: search + B(D + RC).\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-search-compare\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-search-compare\":1},\"figH\":{\"fp-search-compare\":\"Equality search: linear in the heap file vs binary in the sorted file\"}},{\"h\":\"Cost comparison table\",\"table\":[[\"Operation\",\"Heap file\",\"Sorted file\",\"Hashed file\"],[\"Scan\",\"B(D+RC)\",\"B(D+RC)\",\"1.25B(D+RC)\"],[\"Equality search\",\"0.5B(D+RC)\",\"D·log₂B + C·log₂R\",\"H + D + 0.5RC\"],[\"Range search\",\"B(D+RC)\",\"search + retrieve matches\",\"1.25B(D+RC)\"],[\"Insert\",\"2D + C\",\"search + 2·(0.5B(D+RC))\",\"search + C + D\"],[\"Delete\",\"search + C + D\",\"search + 2·(0.5B(D+RC))\",\"search + C + D\"]],\"fig\":[\"fp-cost-calc\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-cost-calc\":\"Cost calculator for heap, sorted and hashed files\"}},{\"h\":\"Reading the exam choices\",\"pts\":[\"Exams expand \\\"search +\\\" into formulas.<br>• Sorted-file delete = <b>Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + B(D+RC)</b>.<br>• Hashed-file delete = <b>1.25B(D+RC) + C + D</b> in the model answer (it uses the hashed scan cost as its \\\"search\\\" term).\",\"Hashed scan is <b>1.25B(D+RC)</b> because hash buckets are typically kept about 80% full, so there are 1.25× as many pages.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"File organization method defines…\",\"Record placement strategy + relationship between key value and relative address.\"],[\"Efficiency is measured by…\",\"The number of disk accesses (fewer is better).\"],[\"Heap file\",\"Unordered records in order of entry; aka pile file.\"],[\"Heap: search method\",\"Linear search only.\"],[\"Heap: main disadvantage\",\"Allows duplicate records.\"],[\"B, R, D, C\",\"Pages, records/page, time per page R/W, time per record processing.\"],[\"Heap scan\",\"B(D + RC)\"],[\"Heap equality search\",\"0.5B(D + RC)\"],[\"Heap insert\",\"2D + C\"],[\"Sorted equality search\",\"D·log₂B + C·log₂R\"],[\"Sorted insert/delete\",\"search + 2·(0.5B(D + RC)) = search + B(D + RC)\"],[\"Hashed scan\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\"],[\"Hashed equality search\",\"H + D + 0.5RC\"],[\"Sorted file insert problem\",\"All records with a greater key must shift one place toward the end.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Why is inserting into a heap file cheap and what is the drawback?\",\"The record is appended to the last page: read the last page (D), add the record (C) and write it back (D), so 2D + C. The drawback is that it allows duplicate records.\"],[\"Why is the sorted-file insert cost search + B(D+RC)?\",\"After finding the position, all later records shift by one. On average half the file (0.5B pages) must be read and written back, so 2 × 0.5B(D+RC).\"],[\"Explain the heap equality-search cost 0.5B(D+RC).\",\"On average the record is found after scanning half the pages. Each page costs D to read plus RC to check its R records. If no record matches, the whole file B(D+RC) is scanned.\"],[\"B = 100 pages, R = 50, D = 15 ms, C = 0.1 ms. Heap scan cost?\",\"B(D+RC) = 100 × (15 + 50×0.1) = 100 × 20 = 2000 ms.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"The average cost for the INSERT process of the heap file is:\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + B(D + RC)\",\"That is the SORTED-file insert/delete: binary search (Dlog₂B + Clog₂R) plus rewriting half the file twice, B(D + RC). A heap insert just appends (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + 2D + C\",\"This glues the sorted-file search cost onto the heap insert 2D + C; no operation in the cost table has this formula. A heap insert needs no search (L4).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (buckets ~80% full); a heap insert only touches the last page: 2D + C (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"2D + C\",\"Correct. Read the last page, add the record, write it back.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm Model A\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the SCAN process of the hashed file is:\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + B(D + RC)\",\"That is the SORTED-file insert/delete cost (binary search + shifting half the file); a hashed scan reads all bucket pages (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + 2D + C\",\"This mixes the sorted search with the heap insert 2D + C; it is not in the cost table, where the hashed scan is 1.25B(D + RC) (L4).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"Correct. Buckets are ~80% full, so 1.25× the pages.\"],[\"B(D + RC)\",\"B(D + RC) is the scan cost of the HEAP and SORTED files; hashed pages are only ~80% full, so the hashed scan is 1.25 × that (L4, Cost table).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm Models A & B\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the DELETE process of the sorted file is:\",\"o\":[[\"2D + C\",\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert (read last page, add, write back); a sorted delete must search and shift records (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"B(D + RC)\",\"The scan cost only; the search part is missing.\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan; the sorted delete is binary search + B(D + RC) (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + B(D + RC)\",\"Correct. Search (binary) + shift half the file (read and write).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm Model B\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for EQUALITY SEARCH in the heap file is:\",\"o\":[[\"2D + C\",\"2D + C is the heap INSERT cost; an equality search must scan pages until the match is found (L4, Heap file costs).\"],[\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"Correct. A heap file has no order, so it is scanned linearly; on average the match is found after half the file: 0.5B(D + RC) (L4, Heap file costs).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC) + C + D\",\"1.25B(D + RC) + C + D is the HASHED-file delete in the model answer, not a heap search (L4, Reading the exam choices).\"],[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R\",\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is binary search, the SORTED-file equality search; a heap file has no order, so it cannot use it (L4, Cost table).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the SCAN process of the sorted file is:\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R\",\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is the sorted-file EQUALITY search; a scan must fetch every record, which reads all B pages (L4, Sorted file costs).\"],[\"B(D + RC)\",\"Correct. A scan fetches every record, so all B pages are read (D each) and R records processed on each (RC): B(D + RC), the same as the heap scan (L4, Sorted file costs).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (pages ~80% full); a sorted file has B full pages, so B(D + RC) (L4).\"],[\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"0.5B(D + RC) is the HEAP-file equality search (half the file on average); a scan reads the whole file (L4).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the DELETE process of the hash file is (model answer):\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R\",\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is the SORTED-file equality search, not a hashed delete (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"B(D + RC)\",\"B(D + RC) is the heap/sorted SCAN cost; the hashed delete in the model answer is 1.25B(D + RC) + C + D (L4, Reading the exam choices).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC) + C + D\",\"Correct per the model answer: search + C + D.\"],[\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"0.5B(D + RC) is the HEAP-file equality search; the hashed delete is search + C + D, written 1.25B(D + RC) + C + D in the model answer (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"In a heap file, searching can be done using:\",\"o\":[[\"Binary search\",\"Binary search needs records sorted by key, but a heap file stores them in order of entry, so it cannot be used (L4, Heap file).\"],[\"Linear search only\",\"Correct. Records are stored in order of entry regardless of key, so the lecture says retrieval in a heap file is by linear search only (L4, Heap file).\"],[\"Hashing\",\"Hashing needs a hashed file whose addresses are computed from the key; a heap file just stores records in entry order (L4).\"],[\"B-tree index\",\"A B-tree index is a separate indexing structure; a plain heap file has none, so only linear search is possible (L4).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Main disadvantage of inserting at the end of a heap file:\",\"o\":[[\"It is slow\",\"Appending is fast: one read of the last page and one write back, 2D + C. Its serious disadvantage is allowing duplicates (L4, Heap file).\"],[\"It allows duplicate records\",\"Correct. Appending never checks existing keys, so the same key can be inserted twice (e.g. a second record 4, Ali, CS at offset 80) (L4, Heap file).\"],[\"It requires shifting records\",\"Shifting records with greater keys towards the end is needed in the SORTED (sequential) file, not the heap file (L4, Ordered file).\"],[\"It needs a hash function\",\"A hash function belongs to hashed files; a heap file simply appends at the end without computing any address (L4).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Inserting into a sequential (sorted) file requires:\",\"o\":[[\"Appending at the end\",\"Appending at the end is the heap-file insert; in a sorted file it would break key order, so records must be shifted (L4, Ordered file).\"],[\"Moving all records with a greater key one location towards the end\",\"Correct. The new record goes to its exact key position, so all records with a greater key move one location towards the end (e.g. 4 goes to offset 60 and 5 shifts to 80) (L4, Ordered file).\"],[\"Rebuilding the hash table\",\"A hash table belongs to hashed files; a sequential file keeps records in key order and shifts them on insert (L4).\"],[\"Nothing special\",\"Key order must be kept, so the record goes into an exact position and all greater keys shift towards the end (L4, Ordered file).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"B = 200 pages, R = 10, D = 10 ms, C = 1 ms. Heap equality search cost?\",\"o\":[[\"4000 ms\",\"4000 ms = 200 × (10 + 10) is the full SCAN B(D + RC); equality search scans half the file on average (L4, Heap file costs).\"],[\"2000 ms\",\"Correct. Equality search = 0.5B(D + RC) = 0.5 × 200 × (10 + 10 × 1) = 100 × 20 = 2000 ms (L4, Heap file costs).\"],[\"21 ms\",\"21 ms = 2 × 10 + 1 is the heap INSERT cost 2D + C, not the equality search (L4).\"],[\"1000 ms\",\"1000 ms = 0.5 × 200 × 10 drops the RC term (10 records × 1 ms per page); the right value is 0.5 × 200 × 20 = 2000 ms (L4).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the delete process of the sorted file is .................\",\"o\":[[\"2D +C\",\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert: read the last page, add the record, write it back (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (buckets ~80% full) (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2*( 0.5B(D + RC) )\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: sorted-file delete = search + 2·(0.5B(D+RC)). The search is a binary search, D·log₂B + C·log₂R; then every later record shifts one place, so on average half the file (0.5B pages) is read and written back (×2).\"],[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"This glues the sorted search onto the heap insert 2D + C; no operation in the cost table has it, and it leaves out shifting half the file (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the Search with equality selection process of the sorted file is .....\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: equality search on a sorted file = D·log₂B + C·log₂R: a binary search over the B pages (D per page read), then a binary search over the R records inside the page (C per comparison).\"],[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2*( 0.5B(D + RC) )\",\"That is the sorted-file INSERT/DELETE: search plus shifting half the file twice; a search does not shift anything (L4).\"],[\"2D +C.\",\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the insert process of the heap file is ..................\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\",\"That is the SORTED-file insert/delete: binary search plus shifting half the file, 2·0.5B(D+RC) = B(D+RC) (L4).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"This puts the sorted search in front of the heap insert; a heap insert needs no search, and no operation in the table has this cost (L4).\"],[\"2D +C.\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: a heap file has no order, so a new record always goes at the END: D to read the last page + C to add the record + D to write the page back = 2D + C, with no search.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the sorted file is ..................\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"This glues the sorted search onto the heap insert; no operation in the cost table has this formula (L4).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (buckets ~80% full, so 1.25× the pages); a sorted file is fully packed (L4).\"],[\"B( D+RC)\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: scanning (fetching all records) costs B(D + RC) for a sorted file, the same as a heap file: read all B pages (D each) and process the R records on each page (C each).\"],[\"2D + C\",\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert, not a scan (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the Search with equality selection process of the heap file is ....\",\"o\":[[\"B(D + RC) + C +D\",\"That is a heap DELETE using a full scan as its search (find it, then C + D to remove it and write the page back); a search reads only half the file on average and writes nothing (L4).\"],[\"2D +C.\",\"2D + C is the heap INSERT: read the last page, add, write back (L4).\"],[\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: equality search on a heap file = 0.5B(D + RC). The file has no order, so pages are read one by one; on average the record is found halfway, after 0.5B pages (if nothing matches, the whole file is scanned).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"Files of unordered records are called\",\"o\":[[\"None of these\",\"\\\"None of these\\\" is wrong because heap files is the lecture's term (L4).\"],[\"Stored files\",\"\\\"Stored files\\\" is not a file organization in the lecture; every file is stored somewhere (L4).\"],[\"Heap files\",\"Correct. L4 \\\"Ordered and Unordered (Heap / Sequential) Files\\\": files of UNORDERED records are heap (pile) files. Records are kept in insertion order: a new record goes at the end (2D + C), and a search scans the file (0.5B(D + RC) on average).\"],[\"Hash files\",\"Hash files are organized by a hash function on a key that picks each record's bucket: a third organization, not unordered (L4–L5).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the heap file is ..................\",\"o\":[[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED scan (buckets ~80% full, so 1.25× the pages); a heap file is fully packed (L4).\"],[\"2D +C.\",\"2D + C is the heap INSERT, not a scan (L4).\"],[\"B( D+RC)\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: heap-file scan = B(D + RC): read all B pages (D each) and process the R records on each page (C each), the same as a sorted-file scan.\"],[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\",\"That is the SORTED-file insert/delete: binary search plus shifting half the file (L4).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"},{\"q\":\"The average cost for the insert process of the sorted file is ..................\",\"o\":[[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2*( 0.5B(D + RC) )\",\"Correct. L4 cost table: sorted-file insert = delete = search + 2·(0.5B(D+RC)). Binary search for the place (D·log₂B + C·log₂R), then every record with a greater key moves one place towards the end: half the file read and written back on average.\"],[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"This puts the sorted search in front of the heap insert; it leaves out shifting half the file, and no operation in the table has it (L4).\"],[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"2D +C.\",\"2D + C is the HEAP insert: a heap just appends at the end, but a sorted file must keep its order (L4).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm 2026/27\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A heap file has B = 400 pages, R = 20 records/page, D = 12 ms, C = 0.5 ms. What is the cost of inserting one record?\",\"o\":[[\"8800 ms\",\"8800 ms = 400 × (12 + 20 × 0.5) is the SCAN cost B(D + RC); an insert only reads and rewrites the last page (L4).\"],[\"4400 ms\",\"That is the equality-search cost 0.5B(D + RC).\"],[\"12.5 ms\",\"This counted only one D. The last page must be read AND written back.\"],[\"24.5 ms\",\"Correct. Insert = 2D + C = 2 × 12 + 0.5 = 24.5 ms.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A sorted file has B = 256 pages, R = 16 records/page, D = 10 ms, C = 1 ms. What is the equality-search cost?\",\"o\":[[\"84 ms\",\"Correct. D·log₂B + C·log₂R = 10 × 8 + 1 × 4 = 84 ms.\"],[\"48 ms\",\"The logs were swapped: D·log₂R + C·log₂B = 40 + 8.\"],[\"88 ms\",\"This used log₂B for both terms (80 + 8). The record term uses log₂R.\"],[\"3328 ms\",\"That is the HEAP equality search 0.5B(D + RC).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Same sorted file (B = 256, R = 16, D = 10 ms, C = 1 ms). What is the cost of inserting one record?\",\"o\":[[\"6656 ms\",\"That is only B(D + RC). You forgot to add the search cost.\"],[\"6740 ms\",\"Correct. Search + 2 × 0.5B(D + RC) = 84 + 256 × (10 + 16) = 84 + 6656 = 6740 ms.\"],[\"3412 ms\",\"This shifted only half the file once (84 + 3328). Half the file must be both read AND written: 2 × 0.5B(D + RC).\"],[\"84 ms\",\"That is only the search cost; it ignores shifting the later records.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A heap file has B = 256, R = 16, D = 10 ms, C = 1 ms. What is the average cost of deleting a record (search + C + D)?\",\"o\":[[\"3328 ms\",\"This is only the search cost. You forgot + C + D.\"],[\"6667 ms\",\"This used the full scan (6656) as the search. On average only half the file is scanned.\"],[\"3339 ms\",\"Correct. Search = 0.5 × 256 × (10 + 16) = 3328; delete = 3328 + 1 + 10 = 3339 ms.\"],[\"21 ms\",\"21 ms = 2 × 10 + 1 is the heap INSERT cost 2D + C; a delete must first search half the file, then add C + D (L4).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A sorted file holds keys 1, 2, 4, 6, 7 in 20-byte records starting at offset 0. A record with key 3 is inserted. How many existing records must be moved?\",\"o\":[[\"0\",\"That is a heap file, where the record is appended at the end and order is ignored.\"],[\"2\",\"Records 4, 6 AND 7 all have greater keys, so all three move.\"],[\"5\",\"Records 1 and 2 have smaller keys, so they stay in place.\"],[\"3\",\"Correct. All records with a greater key (4, 6, 7) move one location toward the end, and key 3 goes in at offset 40.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"A file organization method defines:\",\"o\":[[\"The record placement strategy and the relationship between a record's key value and its relative address\",\"Correct. This is the definition from the slides.\"],[\"How to prepare and format the storage media\",\"Preparing and formatting the storage media is a file MANAGEMENT task (L1); an organization method defines record placement and key-to-address relation (L4).\"],[\"The number of sectors per cluster\",\"Sectors per cluster is a physical disk setting from L2; a file organization method defines record placement and the key–address relationship (L4).\"],[\"Which system software (OS or DBMS) manages the file\",\"That is about file managers, not organization methods.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"The efficiency of a file organization is measured by:\",\"o\":[[\"The size of the records\",\"Record size affects space, but the slides measure efficiency differently.\"],[\"The number of disk accesses\",\"Correct. Fewer disk accesses means a more efficient organization.\"],[\"The number of fields per record\",\"This is a logical design detail, not the efficiency measure.\"],[\"The CPU speed\",\"CPU time is small compared with disk access; the slides count disk accesses.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In a heap file, a range search costs the same as a full scan, B(D + RC).\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Matching records may be anywhere in an unordered file, so every page must be read.\"],[\"False\",\"The heap file has no order to exploit, so range search = B(D + RC), the same as the scan.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Another name for an unordered (heap) file is:\",\"o\":[[\"Indexed file\",\"Indexing is a separate organization method (with heap, sequential and hashing); the heap file's other name is pile file (L4).\"],[\"Sorted file\",\"A sorted file is the ORDERED (sequential) file.\"],[\"Pile file\",\"Correct. The heap file is also called an unordered sequential file or pile file.\"],[\"Hashed file\",\"A hashed file computes each record's address with a hash function.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In a heap file equality search, if no record matches the condition, only half the file is scanned.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Half the file is the AVERAGE when the record exists. With no match you cannot stop early.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. If no record matches, the entire file must be scanned, B(D + RC).\"]],\"a\":1}]},{\"n\":5,\"title\":\"Hashing (from summary and exams)\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Why hashing?\",\"pts\":[\"Hashing is a useful searching technique for implementing indexes. Its main motivation is to <b>improve search time</b>.\",\"Search time:<br>• simple indexes with binary search <b>O(log N)</b><br>• B-trees <b>O(log<sub>k</sub> N)</b><br>• hashing <b>O(1)</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-search-time\"],\"figAt\":{\"fp-search-time\":1},\"figH\":{\"fp-search-time\":\"Search time: binary search vs B-tree vs hashing\"}},{\"h\":\"What is hashing?\",\"pts\":[\"Discover the location of a key by simply examining the key. For that we design a <b>hash function</b>.\",\"A hash function <b>h(k)</b> transforms a <b>key</b> into an <b>address</b>. The address space is chosen beforehand, e.g. 1000 available addresses, so h maps all possible keys U to {0, 1, …, 999}.\",\"With hashing there is <b>no obvious connection between the key and the location</b>.\",\"The address of each record is determined by a mathematical algorithm. This is <b>hashed</b> file organization.\",\"A <b>bucket</b> is a unit of storage that can hold one or more records in a hash file.\"]},{\"h\":\"Collisions\",\"pts\":[\"Two different keys may be transformed into the <b>same address</b>. That is a <b>collision</b>.\",\"Keys that collide are called <b>synonyms</b>.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-collisions\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-collisions\":\"Collisions and synonyms: hashing keys into 10 addresses\"}},{\"h\":\"A simple hash function (Midterm Model A)\",\"code\":\"int Hash(char key[12], int maxAddress) {\\n    int sum = 0;\\n    for (int j = 0; j < 12; j = j + 2)\\n        sum = sum + key[j] * key[j + 1];  // fold pairs of characters\\n    sum = sum % 19937;                   // keep the sum bounded (prime)\\n    return sum % maxAddress;             // map into 0 .. maxAddress-1\\n}\",\"pts\":[\"Step 1: represent the key numerically, combining pairs of characters (ASCII codes).\",\"Step 2: fold and add, dividing by a prime (19937) to prevent overflow.\",\"Step 3: divide by the address-space size and use the remainder as the home address.\"],\"fig\":[\"fp-hash-fn\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-hash-fn\":\"The hash function step by step: from a key to its home address\"}},{\"h\":\"Hashed file costs\",\"table\":[[\"Operation\",\"Hashed file cost\"],[\"Scan\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\"],[\"Equality search\",\"H + D + 0.5RC  (H = time to compute the hash)\"],[\"Range search\",\"1.25B(D + RC)  (hashing does not keep order, so scan everything)\"],[\"Insert\",\"search + C + D\"],[\"Delete\",\"search + C + D\"]],\"fig\":[\"fp-hash-costs\"],\"figH\":{\"fp-hash-costs\":\"Hashed-file costs: scan at 80% full buckets and equality search\"}}],\"cards\":[[\"Hash function h(k)\",\"Transforms a key into an address.\"],[\"Collision\",\"Two different keys hash to the same address.\"],[\"Synonyms\",\"Keys that collide (same address).\"],[\"Bucket\",\"A storage unit holding one or more records in a hash file.\"],[\"Search time: index / B-tree / hashing\",\"O(log N) / O(log_k N) / O(1).\"],[\"Hashed equality search cost\",\"H + D + 0.5RC\"],[\"Why hashed range search is slow\",\"Hashing destroys key order, so the whole file (1.25B pages) is scanned.\"],[\"Hashed organization\",\"The address of each record is determined by a mathematical algorithm.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Write a C++ function to implement a simple hashing algorithm.\",\"int Hash(char key[12], int maxAddress){ int sum=0; for(int j=0;j<12;j+=2) sum += key[j]*key[j+1]; sum %= 19937; return sum % maxAddress; }\"],[\"Define collision and synonyms.\",\"A collision happens when two different keys produce the same address. The keys involved are called synonyms.\"],[\"Why is hashing faster than indexing?\",\"A hash function computes the address directly from the key, so O(1). A simple index needs binary search O(log N) and a B-tree needs O(log_k N).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"The address of each record of a file is determined by a mathematical algorithm known as:\",\"o\":[[\"Sequential\",\"A sequential (ordered) file places records in order of key value; no algorithm computes each record's address (L4, Ordered file).\"],[\"Indexed\",\"An indexed file finds records through a separate index (lookup) structure, not by computing the address with a formula (L5, Why hashing?).\"],[\"Hashed\",\"Correct. In hashed file organization a hash function h(k), a mathematical algorithm, computes each record's address from its key (L5, What is hashing?).\"],[\"None\",\"\\\"None\\\" is wrong because the lecture names this exact organization: hashed, where h(k) computes the address (L5).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Midterm Model B\"},{\"q\":\"A unit of storage that can store one or more records in a hash file organization is:\",\"o\":[[\"Buckets\",\"Correct. The lecture defines a bucket as a unit of storage that can hold one or more records in a hash file (L5, What is hashing?).\"],[\"Disk pages\",\"A general storage term, not the hashing term.\"],[\"Blocks\",\"Blocks are how the DBMS physically organizes tracks (L2); in a hash file the storage unit is called a bucket (L5).\"],[\"Nodes\",\"Nodes belong to trees and linked lists (e.g. B-trees); the hash-file storage unit is the bucket (L5).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Midterm Model C\"},{\"q\":\"When two different keys produce the same address, this is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Overflow\",\"Related, but the event itself is a collision.\"],[\"A collision\",\"Correct. When two different keys are transformed by h(k) into the same address, that event is a collision; the keys themselves are called synonyms (L5, Collisions).\"],[\"A synonym\",\"Synonyms are the KEYS that collide (hash to the same address); the event itself is called a collision (L5, Collisions).\"],[\"Fragmentation\",\"Fragmentation is wasted space inside a sector when records do not fit exactly (L2); it is unrelated to two keys sharing an address (L5).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Search time with hashing is typically:\",\"o\":[[\"O(n)\",\"O(n) is sequential search, reading records one by one; hashing computes the address directly (L3, L5).\"],[\"O(log N)\",\"O(log N) is a simple index searched with binary search; hashing is faster at O(1) (L5, Why hashing?).\"],[\"O(log_k N)\",\"O(log_k N) is the search time of B-trees; hashing goes straight to the address, O(1) (L5, Why hashing?).\"],[\"O(1)\",\"Correct. h(k) computes the record's address directly from the key, so search time does not grow with N: O(1), versus O(log N) for an index and O(log_k N) for a B-tree (L5, Why hashing?).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"In the model hash function, why is sum % 19937 applied before % maxAddress?\",\"o\":[[\"To sort the keys\",\"Hashing does not sort anything; in fact it destroys key order (that is why hashed range search scans the whole file) (L5).\"],[\"To keep the sum bounded (a prime divisor) and avoid overflow\",\"Correct. Dividing by the prime 19937 keeps the folded sum bounded so it does not overflow; the final % maxAddress then maps it to the home address (L5, Simple hash function).\"],[\"To find synonyms\",\"The function only computes an address; synonyms are just keys that happen to get the same address, and % 19937 does not find them (L5).\"],[\"To encrypt the key\",\"Hashing here is for addressing, not security.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Hashed file equality-search cost is:\",\"o\":[[\"H + D + 0.5RC\",\"Correct. Compute the hash (H), read one page (D), scan half the page on average.\"],[\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"0.5B(D + RC) is the HEAP-file equality search (scan half the file); hashing reads only one page (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"D·log₂B + C·log₂R\",\"D·log₂B + C·log₂R is binary search, the SORTED-file equality search; hashing computes the page directly (L4, Cost table).\"],[\"2D + C\",\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert (read last page, write it back), not a search (L4, Cost table).\"]],\"a\":0}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"A short version of the course hash for 4-character keys is:<pre>sum = key[0]*key[1] + key[2]*key[3];\\nsum = sum % 19937;\\nreturn sum % maxAddress;</pre>With ASCII A = 65, B = 66, C = 67, D = 68, what is the home address of key <code>\\\"ABCD\\\"</code> when maxAddress = 100?\",\"o\":[[\"46\",\"Correct. 65 × 66 = 4290; 67 × 68 = 4556; sum = 8846; 8846 % 19937 = 8846; 8846 % 100 = 46.\"],[\"8846\",\"This skipped the final % maxAddress. The address must be in 0..99.\"],[\"66\",\"This added the four codes (266) instead of multiplying pairs.\"],[\"90\",\"90 = 4290 % 100 uses only the first pair (65 × 66); the second pair 67 × 68 = 4556 must be added: 8846 % 100 = 46 (L5).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"With h(k) = k mod 13, which pair of keys are synonyms?\",\"o\":[[\"30 and 44\",\"30 mod 13 = 4 and 44 mod 13 = 5: different addresses.\"],[\"27 and 53\",\"Correct. 27 mod 13 = 1 and 53 mod 13 = 1. Both keys hash to the same address, so they collide and are synonyms.\"],[\"19 and 33\",\"19 mod 13 = 6 and 33 mod 13 = 7: different addresses.\"],[\"40 and 64\",\"40 mod 13 = 1 and 64 mod 13 = 12: different addresses.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Using the course's last step (divide by the address-space size and keep the remainder), what is the home address of the numeric key 1234 in an address space of 101 addresses?\",\"o\":[[\"12\",\"That is the quotient (1234 / 101 ≈ 12), not the remainder.\"],[\"34\",\"This used the last two digits, not the remainder of division by 101.\"],[\"22\",\"Correct. 101 × 12 = 1212, and 1234 - 1212 = 22.\"],[\"101\",\"The addresses run from 0 to 100, so 101 is outside the address space.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"A hashed file has H = 1 ms, D = 10 ms, R = 20 records/page, C = 0.5 ms. What is the equality-search cost H + D + 0.5RC?\",\"o\":[[\"21 ms\",\"This scanned the whole page (RC = 10) instead of half of it on average.\"],[\"15 ms\",\"This left out H, the time to compute the hash.\"],[\"11 ms\",\"This left out the 0.5RC term for checking records in the page.\"],[\"16 ms\",\"Correct. 1 + 10 + 0.5 × 20 × 0.5 = 1 + 10 + 5 = 16 ms.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"With hashing, there is an obvious connection between a record's key and its location in the file.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says the opposite: with hashing there is NO obvious connection between the key and the location; h(k) computes it (L5, What is hashing?).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The hash function computes the address, and there is no obvious connection between the key and the location.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Why does a range search on a hashed file cost 1.25B(D + RC)?\",\"o\":[[\"Hashing does not keep the records in key order, so the whole file must be scanned\",\"Correct. Records in a range can be in any bucket, so every page is read.\"],[\"Only a quarter of the buckets need to be read\",\"No pages can be skipped; all 1.25B pages are read.\"],[\"The hash function must be computed 1.25 times per record\",\"The 1.25 factor is about pages, not hash computations.\"],[\"Records are found by binary search over the buckets\",\"Binary search needs sorted records, which a hashed file does not have.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the hashed-file equality-search cost H + D + 0.5RC, what does H represent?\",\"o\":[[\"The number of buckets\",\"The number of data pages is B in the cost model; H is the time to compute the hash function (L4–L5, Cost model).\"],[\"The time to compute the hash function\",\"Correct. Compute h(k) (H), read the one page (D), and check half its records on average (0.5RC).\"],[\"The height of the cylinder\",\"Cylinder height is a disk-geometry term from Lecture 2.\"],[\"The number of collisions\",\"Collisions do not appear in the formula; H is the time to compute h(k), D reads one page and 0.5RC checks half its records (L5).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the course hash function, what is the purpose of the final line <code>return sum % maxAddress;</code>?\",\"o\":[[\"To prevent integer overflow while folding\",\"That is the job of sum % 19937 (the prime divisor).\"],[\"To combine pairs of characters into a number\",\"Combining pairs of characters is the folding line sum += key[j] * key[j+1]; the final line maps the sum into the address space (L5).\"],[\"To map the sum into the address space 0..maxAddress-1, giving the home address\",\"Correct. The remainder of dividing by the address-space size is the home address.\"],[\"To detect synonyms\",\"The function only computes an address; it does not detect collisions.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"If the address space has 1000 available addresses, the hash function maps every possible key to an address in {0, 1, …, 999}.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The address space is chosen beforehand, and h maps all possible keys into it.\"],[\"False\",\"The slides use this exact example: 1000 addresses, so h maps keys to 0..999.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Why does scanning a hashed file cost 1.25B(D + RC) instead of B(D + RC)?\",\"o\":[[\"Each record is read 1.25 times because of collisions\",\"Each page is read once; the extra cost comes from having more pages.\"],[\"The hash must be computed for every page\",\"A scan reads every page in turn and never computes h(k); the extra 0.25 comes from pages being only ~80% full (L5).\"],[\"The file is sorted after hashing\",\"Hashing does not sort the file; it scatters records by h(k). The 1.25 factor comes from buckets being ~80% full (L5).\"],[\"Buckets are kept about 80% full, so the same data occupies 1.25 times as many pages\",\"Correct. 1 / 0.8 = 1.25, so there are 1.25B pages to read.\"]],\"a\":3}]}],\"exams\":[{\"title\":\"Midterm 2022/23 · Model A\",\"meta\":\"CS308 File Organization · 1 hour · 20 marks · Dr. M. AbdelFattah\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Q1-a · Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"7 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"……… are used to carry, guide and move the read/write head.\",\"o\":[\"Arms\",\"Arm Assembly\",\"Platters\",\"Spindle\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Arms carry, guide and move the heads (moving them = seeking). The arm assembly is the whole set; platters store data; the spindle rotates.\",\"ow\":[null,\"The arm assembly is the whole internal set that holds the arms and heads; the part that carries and moves the heads is the arms (L2, Disk components).\",\"Platters are the circular disks that store data on both surfaces; they do not carry the heads (L2).\",\"The spindle holds the platters and rotates them under the heads; it does not move the heads (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"……… is the time needed to move the read/write arm to the correct sector.\",\"o\":[\"Transfer time\",\"Latency\",\"Rotational time\",\"Seek time\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Seek = arm movement. Latency/rotation = waiting for the platter to spin. Transfer = reading the bytes.\",\"ow\":[\"Transfer time is the time for the head to pass over the data once positioned, not the arm movement (L2, Cost of a disk access).\",\"Latency (rotational delay) is the wait for the sector to rotate under the head, after the arm is already in place (L2).\",\"Rotation is done by the spindle and causes rotational delay; moving the arm is seeking (L2).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Sequential access has the complexity ………\",\"o\":[\"O(n)\",\"O(1)\",\"Log(n)\",\"All answer correct\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Sequential search reads records in order until it finds a match, so in the worst case it reads all n records: O(n) (L3, Managing files of records).\",\"ow\":[null,\"O(1) is direct access by RRN (one seek), not sequential access (L3).\",\"log n is binary search on a file sorted by key, not sequential access (L3–L4).\",\"The three belong to different methods (sequential O(n), binary log n, direct O(1)), so not all are correct (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"If sector size = 512 B, sectors per cluster = 4 and sectors per track = 64, the cluster size = ………\",\"o\":[\"32768 byte\",\"2048 byte\",\"128 byte\",\"8 byte\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Cluster = 4 × 512 = 2048 B. 32768 = 64 × 512 is the track size (a distractor).\",\"ow\":[\"32768 = 64 × 512 is the TRACK capacity; a cluster is only 4 sectors: 4 × 512 (L2, Capacity formulas).\",null,\"128 = 512 / 4 divides instead of multiplying; cluster = sectors per cluster × bytes per sector (L2).\",\"8 bytes fits no formula; a 4-sector cluster holds 4 × 512 = 2048 B (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the insert process of the heap file is ………\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"2D + C\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Read the last page (D), add the record (C), write it back (D).\",\"ow\":[\"That is the sorted-file insert/delete: binary search plus shifting half the file, B(D + RC) (L4, Cost table).\",\"Sorted search added to 2D + C matches no entry in the cost table; a heap insert needs no search (L4).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the hashed-file scan (pages ~80% full), not a heap insert (L4).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the hashed file is ………\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"B(D+RC)\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Hashed pages are ~80% full, so 1.25B pages to scan.\",\"ow\":[\"That is the sorted-file insert/delete cost (binary search + shifting half the file), not a scan (L4).\",\"Sorted search plus the heap insert 2D + C is not a table formula; the hashed scan is 1.25B(D + RC) (L4).\",null,\"B(D + RC) is the heap/sorted scan; hashed pages are ~80% full, so 1.25 times as many pages are read (L4).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Q1-b\",\"marks\":\"2 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write a C++ function to implement a simple hashing algorithm.\",\"ans\":\"<pre>int Hash(char key[12], int maxAddress)\\n{\\n    int sum = 0;\\n    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 12; j = j + 2)\\n        sum = sum + key[j] * key[j+1];\\n    sum = sum % 19937;\\n    return sum % maxAddress;\\n}</pre>\",\"why\":\"Fold pairs of characters, bound the sum with a prime modulus, then take mod of the address-space size to get the home address.\"}]},{\"title\":\"Q2\",\"marks\":\"11 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"a) \\\"There are many ways of adding structure to files to maintain the identity of fields.\\\" Mention these ways and show the advantages and disadvantages for each way. [2 marks]\",\"ans\":\"<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr><tr><td>Fixed</td><td>Easy to read/store</td><td>Wastes space with padding</td></tr><tr><td>With length indicator</td><td>Easy to jump ahead to the end of the field</td><td>Long fields need more than 1 byte to store the length (max size &gt; 256)</td></tr><tr><td>Delimited fields</td><td>May waste less space than length-based</td><td>Must check every byte of the field against the delimiter</td></tr><tr><td>Keyword</td><td>Fields are self-describing; allows missing fields</td><td>Wastes space with keywords</td></tr></table>\",\"why\":\"Lecture 3 table.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"b) Fixed-length records; 10,000 records; record = 128 B. Disk: 512 B/sector; 20 sectors/track; cylinder height = 12 tracks; 500 tracks/surface; non-data overhead = 500 B/block; spindle = 5000 rpm; average seek = 7 ms.<br>(i) Average latency.\",\"ans\":\"Average latency = time for ½ revolution = (0.5 × 1000 × 60) / 5000 = <b>6 ms</b>.\",\"why\":\"One revolution = 60,000/5000 = 12 ms, and half of that is 6 ms.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(ii) Number of records/track if blocking factor is 20.\",\"ans\":\"Track capacity = 20 × 512 = 10,240 B<br>Block size = 128 × 20 + 500 = 3060 B<br>Blocks/track = ⌊10,240 / 3060⌋ = ⌊3.35⌋ = 3 blocks<br>Records/track = 3 × 20 = <b>60 records</b>\",\"why\":\"Always add the overhead to each block and take the floor, because a block cannot be split across tracks.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(iii) Disk capacity.\",\"ans\":\"Disk capacity = track capacity × cylinder height × tracks/surface = 10,240 × 12 × 500 = <b>61,440,000 B ≈ 58.6 MB</b>.\",\"why\":\"Cylinder = 12 tracks and number of cylinders = 500. 61,440,000 / 1,048,576 ≈ 58.6 MB.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(iv) Average access time to read three sectors.\",\"ans\":\"Revolution = 60,000/5000 = 12 ms<br>Transfer (3 sectors) = 3 × 12 / 20 = <b>1.8 ms</b><br>Average total = seek 7 + latency 6 + transfer 1.8 = <b>14.8 ms</b>\",\"why\":\"Each sector takes 1/20 of a revolution. The model answer writes this as [(3×60)/5000]/20, which is the same thing.\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Midterm 2022/23 · Model B\",\"meta\":\"CS308 File Organization · 1 hour · 20 marks · Dr. M. AbdelFattah\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Q1-a · Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"6 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"All the information on a single cylinder can be accessed without …………\",\"o\":[\"moving the Spindle\",\"moving the arm assembly\",\"Rotation\",\"Seeking\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A cylinder is all tracks under the heads at one arm position, so no arm movement is needed. Rotation is still required.\",\"ow\":[\"The spindle never stops rotating the platters; what stays still while reading one cylinder is the arm assembly (L2).\",null,\"Rotation is still needed to bring each sector under the head; only arm movement is avoided (L2).\",\"Seeking (moving the arm) is the same idea, but the model answer is (b), the arm assembly, matching the slide wording \\\"without moving the arm\\\" (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Direct access has the complexity ………\",\"o\":[\"O(n)\",\"O(1)\",\"Log(n)\",\"All answer correct\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Direct access seeks straight to the record's byte offset (RRN × record size) in one access, so the cost does not depend on n: O(1) (L3, Managing files of records).\",\"ow\":[\"O(n) is sequential search, which reads records in order until a match (L3).\",null,\"log n is binary search on a sorted file; direct access needs a single seek (L3–L4).\",\"Only O(1) describes direct access; O(n) is sequential and log n is binary search (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"File Organization methods must provide ………\",\"o\":[\"Fast Access Time and good space Utilization\",\"Preparing/formatting storage media to store data\",\"Allocating storage space and addressing\",\"Managing free spaces\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The other three are File MANAGEMENT techniques.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Preparing/formatting storage media is a file MANAGEMENT task, not what organization methods must provide (L1).\",\"Allocating storage space and addressing is a file MANAGEMENT task (L1).\",\"Managing free space is a file MANAGEMENT task; organization must give fast access and good space use (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The address of each record of a file is determined by a mathematical algorithm known as ………\",\"o\":[\"Sequential\",\"Indexed\",\"Hashed\",\"None\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"In hashed file organization a hash function h(k), a mathematical algorithm, transforms each record's key into its address (L5, What is hashing?).\",\"ow\":[\"A sequential file stores records in key order; no algorithm computes each address (L4).\",\"An indexed file locates records through a separate index structure, not a formula (L5).\",null,\"Wrong because the lecture names this organization: hashed, where h(k) computes the address (L5).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the delete process of the sorted file is ………\",\"o\":[\"2D + C\",\"B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Binary search to find it (Dlog₂B + Clog₂R), then shift half the file: 2 × 0.5B(D+RC) = B(D+RC).\",\"ow\":[\"2D + C is the heap-file insert (read last page, add, write back), not a sorted delete (L4).\",\"B(D + RC) is only the shifting part (or a scan); the search term Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is missing (L4).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the hashed-file scan, not a sorted delete (L4).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the hashed file is ………\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + D + C\",\"B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D+RC) + D + C\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A hashed scan reads every bucket page; buckets are kept about 80% full, so the data occupies 1/0.8 = 1.25 times as many pages: 1.25B(D + RC) (L4, Cost table).\",\"ow\":[\"This is a binary-search term plus D + C, matching no scan formula; a scan must read all pages (L4).\",\"B(D + RC) is the heap/sorted scan; hashed pages are only ~80% full, so it is 1.25 times larger (L4).\",null,\"1.25B(D + RC) + D + C is the hashed DELETE in the model answer (search + C + D), not the scan (L4).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Q1-b\",\"marks\":\"3 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Write a C++ program, with no loops, to copy the contents of d:\\\\src.txt to d:\\\\dest.txt (destination is empty).\",\"ans\":\"<pre>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\\n#include &lt;fstream&gt;\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main() {\\n    ifstream infile(\\\"d:\\\\\\\\src.txt\\\", ios::binary);\\n    ofstream outfile(\\\"d:\\\\\\\\dest.txt\\\", ios::binary);\\n    infile.seekg(0, infile.end);\\n    long size = infile.tellg();\\n    infile.seekg(0);\\n    char* buffer = new char[size];\\n    infile.read(buffer, size);\\n    outfile.write(buffer, size);\\n    delete[] buffer;\\n    outfile.close(); infile.close();\\n    return 0;\\n}</pre>\",\"why\":\"seekg to the end, tellg gives the size, then one read and one write replace the loop.\"}]},{\"title\":\"Q2\",\"marks\":\"11 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"a) \\\"Network and CPU have to wait a long time for the disk to transmit data.\\\" Give four techniques to solve this problem. [2 marks]\",\"ans\":\"1. <b>Multiprocessing</b>: the CPU works on other jobs while waiting for the disk.<br>2. <b>Disk striping</b>: different blocks of the file on different drives, so independent processes don't interfere (parallelism).<br>3. <b>RAID</b>: Redundant Array of Independent Disks.<br>4. <b>RAM disk</b>: part of main memory simulates a disk (speed vs volatility).<br>5. <b>Disk cache</b>: memory holds disk pages; check the cache first and go to disk on a miss.\",\"why\":\"Lecture 2, \\\"Disk as a bottleneck\\\". Any four earn the marks.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"b) Fixed-length records; 10,000 records; record = 128 B. Disk: 512 B/sector; 30 sectors/track; cylinder height = 10; 1000 tracks/surface; 4 sectors/cluster; 1000 rpm; average seek = 10 ms; average rotational delay = 5 ms.<br>(i) Cylinder size.\",\"ans\":\"Cylinder size = 30 × 512 × 10 = <b>153,600 B = 150 KB</b><br>Records per cylinder = 153,600 / 128 = 1200 records\",\"why\":\"Track = 15,360 B, and ×10 tracks per cylinder.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(ii) Number of tracks to save the file.\",\"ans\":\"File = 10,000 × 128 = 1,280,000 B = 1250 KB<br>Track = 30 × 512 = 15,360 B = 15 KB<br>Tracks = 1250 / 15 = 83.3, so <b>84 tracks</b>\",\"why\":\"Round UP: a partial track still has to be allocated.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(iii) Total disk size.\",\"ans\":\"Cylinder size × tracks/surface = 150 KB × 1000 = 150,000 KB ≈ <b>146.5 MB</b>\",\"why\":\"Number of cylinders = tracks per surface = 1000.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(iv) Average time to read two sectors.\",\"ans\":\"Revolution = 60,000/1000 = 60 ms<br>Transfer (2 sectors) = 2 × 60 / 30 = <b>4 ms</b><br>Total = seek 10 + rotational delay 5 + transfer 4 = <b>19 ms</b>\",\"why\":\"The model answer computes \\\"(2/1000)×60 = 120 ms\\\" and divides by 30. That gives the same 4 ms, but the clearer way is one revolution (60 ms) × 2/30. The average rotational delay (5 ms) is given, so don't recompute it from the rpm.\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Midterm 2022/23 · Model C\",\"meta\":\"CS308 File Organization · 1 hour · 20 marks · Dr. M. AbdelFattah\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Q1-a · Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"7 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"In stream file structure the information is written as a stream of bytes containing …………\",\"o\":[\"no added information\",\"no space\",\"added information\",\"space\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"That is why records can't be recovered from a stream file.\",\"ow\":[null,\"\\\"No space\\\" is not the definition; the point is that no added information (delimiters, lengths) is written (L3, Stream files).\",\"Added information (delimiters, length indicators, keywords) is what field structures add; a stream has none (L3).\",\"\\\"Space\\\" is not the definition; a stream file contains no added information (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"A binary search has the complexity ………\",\"o\":[\"O(n)\",\"O(1)\",\"O(log₂ n)\",\"O(n²)\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Binary search on a sorted file compares the key with the middle record and discards half of the remaining range each step, so it needs about log₂ n accesses (L4, Sorted file).\",\"ow\":[\"O(n) is linear (sequential) search, which reads records one by one (L3).\",\"O(1) is direct access by RRN with one seek (L3).\",null,\"O(n²) would be slower than even sequential search; binary search is O(log₂ n) (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Files are managed by system software: ………\",\"o\":[\"OS\",\"DBMS\",\"MIS\",\"Both a and b\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"The lecture names two file managers (system software): the operating system and the DBMS, so both a and b are correct (L1, File managers).\",\"ow\":[\"The OS is a file manager, but so is the DBMS, so this is only half the answer (L1).\",\"The DBMS is a file manager, but so is the OS, so this is only half the answer (L1).\",\"MIS is a management information system, not one of the file managers (OS, DBMS) named in the lecture (L1).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"A unit of storage that can store one or more records in a hash file organization is ………\",\"o\":[\"Buckets\",\"Disk pages\",\"Blocks\",\"Nodes\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The lecture defines a bucket as the unit of storage that can hold one or more records in a hash file organization (L5, What is hashing?).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Disk page is a general storage term (page = block in the cost model); the hashing term is bucket (L5).\",\"Blocks are how the DBMS physically organizes tracks (L2); in hashing the unit is the bucket (L5).\",\"Nodes belong to trees and linked lists, not to hash file organization (L5).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the search with equality selection of the heap file is ………\",\"o\":[\"2D + C\",\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) + C + D\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A heap file is unordered, so equality search is linear; on average the match is found after scanning half the file: 0.5B(D + RC) (L4, Heap file costs).\",\"ow\":[\"2D + C is the heap INSERT cost (read last page, write it back), not a search (L4).\",null,\"1.25B(D + RC) + C + D is the hashed-file delete in the model answer (L4).\",\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is binary search, the SORTED-file equality search; a heap file is unordered (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the delete process of the hash file is ………\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R\",\"B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC) + C + D\",\"0.5B(D + RC)\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Model answer: search + C + D, with 1.25B(D+RC) used as the search term.\",\"ow\":[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is the sorted-file equality search, not a hashed delete (L4).\",\"B(D + RC) is the heap/sorted scan cost (L4).\",null,\"0.5B(D + RC) is the heap-file equality search (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the sorted file is ………\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R\",\"B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"0.5B(D + RC)\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A scan fetches every record, so all B pages are read (D each) and the R records on each processed (RC): B(D + RC), the same as the heap scan (L4, Sorted file costs).\",\"ow\":[\"Dlog₂B + Clog₂R is the sorted-file equality search (binary search), not a full scan (L4).\",null,\"1.25B(D + RC) is the hashed-file scan, where pages are only ~80% full (L4).\",\"0.5B(D + RC) is the heap-file equality search (half the file on average) (L4).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Q1-b\",\"marks\":\"3 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"Consider a (fixed-length field, fixed-length record) file. Write the full body of UpdateRecord given the Relative Record Number (RRN).\",\"ans\":\"<pre>struct Item {\\n    char desc[31];\\n    int qty;\\n    float price;\\n};\\n\\nvoid UpdateRecord(fstream &amp;file, Item &amp;newItem, int RRN)\\n{\\n    // newItem contains the new record to overwrite the old one\\n    file.seekp(RRN * sizeof(newItem), ios::beg);\\n    file.write((char *) &amp;newItem, sizeof(newItem));\\n}</pre>\",\"why\":\"Fixed sizes mean offset = RRN × record size. seekp positions the put pointer, and write overwrites the record.\"}]},{\"title\":\"Q2\",\"marks\":\"10 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"a) Mention the main differences between: (i) Physical file and logical file; (ii) Seek time, rotational delay and transfer time. [4 marks]\",\"ans\":\"<b>(i)</b> Physical file: a collection of bytes on disk; has a physical name (A.txt); used once when opening. Logical file: a channel connecting the program to the physical file; its logical name is a program variable (infile, fp); used as many times as needed (open, read, write, append, close).<br><b>(ii)</b> Seek time: time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder. Rotational delay (latency): time for the disk to rotate the desired sector under the head. Transfer time: once positioned over the data, the time to transfer it.\",\"why\":\"Lecture 1 and Lecture 2 tables.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"b) Same disk as Model B (512 B/sector, 30 sectors/track, cylinder height 10, 1000 tracks/surface, 1000 rpm, seek 10 ms, rotational delay 5 ms; 10,000 records × 128 B).<br>(i) Number of records per cylinder.\",\"ans\":\"Cylinder size = 30 × 512 × 10 = 153,600 B<br>Records/cylinder = 153,600 / 128 = <b>1200 records</b>\",\"why\":\"The model answer shows only the cylinder size. Finish with the division by record size.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(ii) Number of sectors to save the file.\",\"ans\":\"File = 10,000 × 128 = 1,280,000 B<br>Sectors = 1,280,000 / 512 = <b>2500 sectors</b>\",\"why\":\"The model answer mislabels this line as \\\"tracks\\\", but the value is sectors.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(iii) Total disk size.\",\"ans\":\"150 KB × 1000 = 150,000 KB ≈ <b>146.5 MB</b>\",\"why\":\"Cylinder size × number of cylinders.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"(iv) Average time to read four sectors.\",\"ans\":\"Revolution = 60 ms<br>Transfer = 4 × 60 / 30 = <b>8 ms</b><br>Total = 10 + 5 + 8 = <b>23 ms</b>\",\"why\":\"Seek + given rotational delay + transfer.\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Midterm 2026/2027\",\"meta\":\"CS308 File Organization and Processing · Midterm, 1st term 2026/2027 · online quiz · 30 minutes · 20 marks · 30 questions · Lectures 1–4 · Dr. M. AbdelFattah\",\"note\":\"No official answer key was published. The answers are worked out from Lectures 1–4 and the 2022/23 midterm model answers. This midterm also asked the Lecture 4 heap and sorted file costs. Two questions are judgement calls: the disk-bottleneck question (all of a, b and d are techniques on the slide; \\\"Both A and B\\\" covers the most of them), and \\\"not used as a file opening mode\\\", where all four flags are in the lecture and ios::trunc is the answer of the MCQ bank it comes from. The questions are kept as they appeared, typos included (\\\"Lenght\\\", \\\"Gide\\\", \\\"tansmit\\\", \\\"teckniques\\\", \\\"indepentent\\\").\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Choose the correct answer\",\"marks\":\"30 questions\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the delete process of the sorted file is .................\",\"o\":[\"2D +C\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2*( 0.5B(D + RC) )\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: sorted-file delete = search + 2·(0.5B(D+RC)). The search is a binary search, D·log₂B + C·log₂R; then every later record shifts one place, so on average half the file (0.5B pages) is read and written back (×2).\",\"ow\":[\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert: read the last page, add the record, write it back (L4, Cost table).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (buckets ~80% full) (L4, Cost table).\",null,\"This glues the sorted search onto the heap insert 2D + C; no operation in the cost table has it, and it leaves out shifting half the file (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"EOF is an integer type defined in stdio. hand has a value ____________\",\"o\":[\"NULL\",\"0\",\"– 1\",\"1\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"EOF is an integer constant defined in stdio.h with the value −1. The L1 readASCII() loop does result = fscanf(...) and stops when result == EOF; fgetc and fscanf return EOF (−1) at end of file or on error. (The lectures use EOF in code but do not print its value; −1 is the C standard value.)\",\"ow\":[\"NULL is a null POINTER: what fopen returns when the file cannot be opened (if (my_file == NULL)), not the integer EOF (L1).\",\"0 is what fread returns at end of file (zero elements read); it is a count, not the EOF constant (L1).\",null,\"1 is a normal successful count (e.g. fread read one record); it does not mean end of file (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Network and CPU have to wait a long time for the disk to tansmit data, we have a various teckniques to solve this problem\",\"o\":[\"Disk striping and distributed processing\",\"Redundant Array of indepentent disks\",\"Both A and B\",\"Disk cache\"],\"a\":2,\"key\":\"No official key. All of a, b and d are techniques from the slide; \\\"Both A and B\\\" is chosen because it covers the most of them (striping, multiprocessing, RAID).\",\"why\":\"L2 \\\"Disk as a bottleneck\\\": the network and CPU wait for the disk. Techniques: (1) multiprocessing, (2) disk striping, (3) RAID, (4) RAM disk, (5) disk cache. The question asks for the \\\"various techniques\\\", and \\\"Both A and B\\\" covers the most of them: striping + distributed processing (multiprocessing) + RAID.\",\"ow\":[\"Disk striping and distributed processing (multiprocessing) are techniques, but incomplete: RAID is one too, so \\\"Both A and B\\\" is fuller (L2, Disk bottleneck).\",\"RAID is only one technique; striping and multiprocessing are also on the slide, so \\\"Both A and B\\\" is fuller (L2).\",null,\"Disk cache is a real technique (number 5 on the slide), but it is only one; \\\"Both A and B\\\" groups more of the slide's techniques (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Easy to jump ahead to the end of the field, it's advantage of ....\",\"o\":[\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"Keyword field structure\",\"Fixed field structure\",\"Delimiter field structure\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L3 field-structures table: with a length indicator each field starts with its length (e.g. 05 87359 07 CARROLL …), so the program reads the length and skips straight to the end of the field. Disadvantage: long fields need more than 1 byte to store the length.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Keyword = value's advantage is that fields are self-describing and may be missing; it gives no length to jump with (L3).\",\"Fixed fields' advantage is \\\"easy to read/store\\\"; the jump-ahead advantage is listed for the length indicator (L3).\",\"Delimiter is the opposite: you must check every byte to find the delimiter, so you cannot jump ahead (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Used to carry, Gide and move the read/write head called .....\",\"o\":[\"Spindles\",\"Platters\",\"Arm assembly\",\"Arms\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"L2 disk components: the ARMS carry, guide and move the read/write heads; moving them to the right track is seeking (same question and answer in Midterm 2022/23 Model A).\",\"ow\":[\"Spindles hold the platters and rotate them under the heads; they do not move the heads (L2).\",\"Platters are the circular disks that store the data on both surfaces (L2).\",\"The arm assembly is the whole internal unit that holds the arms and heads; the specific part that carries and moves the heads is the arms (L2).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Waste space with padding, it's disadvantage of ....\",\"o\":[\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"Delimiter field structure\",\"Fixed field structure\",\"Keyword field structure\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L3 field-structures table: in a FIXED-length structure every field has a set size, so a short value (a 3-letter name in a 10-byte field) is padded with blanks, and the padding is wasted space. Advantage: easy to read/store.\",\"ow\":[\"Length indicator's disadvantage is that long fields need more than 1 byte for the length; there is no padding (L3).\",\"Delimiter's disadvantage is checking every byte for the delimiter; it may waste LESS space (L3).\",null,\"Keyword's disadvantage is the space taken by the keywords themselves; there is no padding (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Fields are self describing, allows for missing fields, it's advantage of ....\",\"o\":[\"Fixed field structure\",\"Keyword field structure\",\"Lenght indicator field structure\",\"Delimiter field structure\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"L3 field-structures table: in keyword = value (e.g. last=Ames|first=Mary|city=Stillwater|) each field carries its own name, so fields are self-describing, and a field with no value can simply be left out. Disadvantage: the keywords waste space.\",\"ow\":[\"Fixed fields are known only by position: not self-describing, and a missing field still takes its padded space (L3).\",null,\"A length indicator says how many bytes a field has, not which field it is (L3).\",\"Delimited fields are separated by a symbol and known only by their order, so they are not self-describing (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the Search with equality selection process of the sorted file is .....\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2*( 0.5B(D + RC) )\",\"2D +C.\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: equality search on a sorted file = D·log₂B + C·log₂R: a binary search over the B pages (D per page read), then a binary search over the R records inside the page (C per comparison).\",\"ow\":[null,\"That is the sorted-file INSERT/DELETE: search plus shifting half the file twice; a search does not shift anything (L4).\",\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert (L4, Cost table).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The small part of the disk, that move above the disk platter is ----\",\"o\":[\"Platter\",\"Arm\",\"Spindle\",\"R/W Head\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"L2: read/write heads are \\\"the small part of the disk that moves above the disk platter\\\". They fly above the surface (clearance as little as 3 nm) and move to the track that holds the data; each surface has its own head.\",\"ow\":[\"The platter is the circular magnetic disk that stores the data; the head moves above it (L2).\",\"The arm carries, guides and moves the head; it is not the small part that reads and writes above the surface (L2).\",\"The spindle holds and rotates the platters; it does not move above them (L2).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The Structures of file are .....\",\"o\":[\"Physical structure\",\"Both A and B\",\"Stream structure\",\"Logical structures\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"L1 \\\"(3) File structures\\\": a file has a LOGICAL structure (how programmers see it: text, image, data file…) and a PHYSICAL structure (how it is stored on secondary storage). \\\"Both A and B\\\" is mislabelled on the quiz: it means physical (a) and logical (d).\",\"ow\":[\"Physical structure alone is only half: the logical structure is the other one (L1, File structures).\",null,\"Stream is one of the L3 ways of organizing data inside a file (stream, field, record), not one of the file's two structures (L3).\",\"Logical structure alone is only half: the physical structure is the other one (L1, File structures).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The circular disk on which magnetic data is stored in a hard disk drive is .......\",\"o\":[\"Spindle\",\"Arm assembly\",\"Platter\",\"Arm\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L2: the platter is \\\"the circular disk on which magnetic data is stored in a hard disk drive\\\". It is coated with a thin magnetic material and can store data on both surfaces.\",\"ow\":[\"The spindle is the shaft that holds and spins the platters; it stores nothing (L2).\",\"The arm assembly holds the arms and heads that read and write the platters; it does not store data (L2).\",null,\"The arm carries, guides and moves the read/write head (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the insert process of the heap file is ..................\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"2D +C.\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: a heap file has no order, so a new record always goes at the END: D to read the last page + C to add the record + D to write the page back = 2D + C, with no search.\",\"ow\":[\"That is the SORTED-file insert/delete: binary search plus shifting half the file, 2·0.5B(D+RC) = B(D+RC) (L4).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\",\"This puts the sorted search in front of the heap insert; a heap insert needs no search, and no operation in the table has this cost (L4).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What is the function of fputs()?\",\"o\":[\"write a line to a file\",\"read a character from a file\",\"read a line from a file\",\"write a character to a file\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L1 C file functions: fputs(str, fp) writes a string (a line) to a file: f = file, put = write, s = string.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Reading one character is fgetc(fp) (L1).\",\"Reading a line is fgets(str, n, fp), the reading partner of fputs (L1).\",\"Writing one character is fputc(ch, fp) (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the sorted file is ..................\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"B( D+RC)\",\"2D + C\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: scanning (fetching all records) costs B(D + RC) for a sorted file, the same as a heap file: read all B pages (D each) and process the R records on each page (C each).\",\"ow\":[\"This glues the sorted search onto the heap insert; no operation in the cost table has this formula (L4).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (buckets ~80% full, so 1.25× the pages); a sorted file is fully packed (L4).\",null,\"2D + C is the HEAP-file insert, not a scan (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The time to access a sector in a track on a surface is divided into .....\",\"o\":[\"Three components\",\"Four components\",\"one components\",\"Two components\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L2 cost of a disk access: \\\"the time to access a sector in a track on a surface is divided into 3 components\\\": seek time (move the arm to the track), rotational delay (wait for the sector to come under the head) and transfer time (the head passes over the block).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Four is the number of platter components (sector, cluster, track, cylinder), a different list in the same lecture (L2).\",\"The lecture splits the access time into three separate parts, not one (L2).\",\"Two leaves one of the three out (usually transfer time) (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the Search with equality selection process of the heap file is ....\",\"o\":[\"B(D + RC) + C +D\",\"2D +C.\",\"0.5B(D + RC)\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: equality search on a heap file = 0.5B(D + RC). The file has no order, so pages are read one by one; on average the record is found halfway, after 0.5B pages (if nothing matches, the whole file is scanned).\",\"ow\":[\"That is a heap DELETE using a full scan as its search (find it, then C + D to remove it and write the page back); a search reads only half the file on average and writes nothing (L4).\",\"2D + C is the heap INSERT: read the last page, add, write back (L4).\",null,\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"It's time to take the disk to rotate so that the desired sector is under the read/write head\",\"o\":[\"Rotational delay (or latency)\",\"Transfer time\",\"Seek Time\",\"Revolution time\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L2: rotational delay (latency) is the time needed for the disk to rotate so the sector we want is under the read/write head. Minimum 0, maximum one revolution, average half a revolution.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Transfer time is the head passing over the block, after the sector is already under it (L2).\",\"Seek time is moving the ARM to the right track/cylinder: arm movement, not rotation (L2).\",\"Revolution time is one FULL turn (e.g. 12 ms at 5000 rpm), which is only the MAXIMUM latency (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The DBMS views the file as a series of .......\",\"o\":[\"Blocks\",\"Clusters\",\"Tracks\",\"Bytes\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L2 organizing tracks: by SECTORS (used by the OS, whose file manager views the file as a series of clusters) or by BLOCKS (used by the DBMS: tracks divided into user-defined blocks/pages, each usually holding an integral number of logical records). So the DBMS sees blocks.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Clusters (fixed groups of contiguous sectors) are the OS file manager's view, not the DBMS's (L2).\",\"Tracks are physical rings on the platter, part of the disk's geometry, not a file manager's view of a file (L2).\",\"Bytes is the L3 stream view (a file as a stream of bytes), not the DBMS view (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Have to check every byte of field, it's disadvantage of ....\",\"o\":[\"Keyword field structure\",\"Fixed field structure\",\"Delimiter field structure\",\"Lenght indicator field structure\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L3 field-structures table: in a DELIMITED structure fields are separated by a special character (87359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND|), so to find the end of a field the program must check every byte for the delimiter. Advantage: may waste less space (no padding).\",\"ow\":[\"Keyword's disadvantage is the space taken by the keywords; its advantage is self-describing fields (L3).\",\"Fixed fields' disadvantage is padding waste; field ends are at known positions, so no byte checking (L3).\",null,\"Length indicator's disadvantage is that long fields need more than 1 byte for the length; its advantage is the opposite: jumping straight to the end of the field (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which function is used to reposition the file pointer?\",\"o\":[\"changep()\",\"moveg()\",\"go_p()\",\"seekg()\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"L1 C++ file I/O: seekg() (\\\"seek get\\\") moves the read file pointer, e.g. infile.seekg(0, infile.end) jumps to the end and tellg() then gives the size. Its partner for the write pointer is seekp(); the C equivalent is fseek().\",\"ow\":[\"changep() is not a C or C++ file function (L1).\",\"moveg() does not exist; it only imitates the \\\"g\\\" of seekg (L1).\",\"go_p() does not exist (L1).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Files of unordered records are called\",\"o\":[\"None of these\",\"Stored files\",\"Heap files\",\"Hash files\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L4 \\\"Ordered and Unordered (Heap / Sequential) Files\\\": files of UNORDERED records are heap (pile) files. Records are kept in insertion order: a new record goes at the end (2D + C), and a search scans the file (0.5B(D + RC) on average).\",\"ow\":[\"\\\"None of these\\\" is wrong because heap files is the lecture's term (L4).\",\"\\\"Stored files\\\" is not a file organization in the lecture; every file is stored somewhere (L4).\",null,\"Hash files are organized by a hash function on a key that picks each record's bucket: a third organization, not unordered (L4–L5).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the scan process of the heap file is ..................\",\"o\":[\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"2D +C.\",\"B( D+RC)\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + B(D + RC)\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: heap-file scan = B(D + RC): read all B pages (D each) and process the R records on each page (C each), the same as a sorted-file scan.\",\"ow\":[\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED scan (buckets ~80% full, so 1.25× the pages); a heap file is fully packed (L4).\",\"2D + C is the heap INSERT, not a scan (L4).\",null,\"That is the SORTED-file insert/delete: binary search plus shifting half the file (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"It's the time for the read/write head to pass over a block.\",\"o\":[\"Transfer time\",\"Seek Time\",\"Rotational delay (or latency)\",\"Revolution time\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L2: transfer time is the time for the read/write head to pass over a block. Transfer time = (bytes transferred / bytes on a track) × rotation time, or revolution time / sectors per track.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Seek time is moving the ARM to the right track; it happens before the head reaches the block (L2).\",\"Rotational delay is waiting for the sector to rotate UNDER the head, before passing over it (L2).\",\"Revolution time is a full turn; transfer time is only the fraction of a turn the block takes up (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"A hard disk with 20 surfaces will have _____ heads.\",\"o\":[\"20\",\"5\",\"10\",\"1\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L2: each SURFACE has its own read/write head (the slide's drive with 4 platters has 8 surfaces and 8 heads). So 20 surfaces → 20 heads.\",\"ow\":[null,\"5 divides by 4 for no reason: it is neither the number of platters (10) nor the number of heads (L2).\",\"10 is the number of PLATTERS (2 surfaces each); heads are counted per surface, not per platter (L2).\",\"Every surface needs its own head; they all move together on the arm assembly, but there are still 20 of them (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"All the information on a single cylinder can be accessed without ………..\",\"o\":[\"Moving the Spindle\",\"Rotation\",\"moving the arm assembly\",\"Seeking\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L2: a cylinder is the set of tracks directly above/below each other, all under the heads at one arm position, so its data can be read without moving the arm. Same question in Midterm 2022/23 Model B, model answer \\\"moving the arm assembly\\\".\",\"ow\":[\"The spindle never \\\"moves\\\": it only rotates the platters all the time (L2).\",\"Rotation is still needed to bring each sector under the head; only arm movement is avoided (L2).\",null,\"Seeking means moving the arm, the same idea, but the model answer (Model B) is \\\"moving the arm assembly\\\", matching the slide's \\\"without moving the arm\\\" (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which one of the following is correct syntax for opening a file.\",\"o\":[\"FILE *fopen(const *filename, const char *mode)\",\"FILE *fopen(const *filename)\",\"FILE *open(const *filename, const char *mode)\",\"FILE open(const*filename)\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L1: in C a file is opened with fopen, which takes the file name and the mode (\\\"r\\\", \\\"w\\\", \\\"a\\\", \\\"rb\\\", \\\"wb\\\"…) and returns a FILE * pointer (NULL on failure), as in my_file = fopen(\\\"ascii.txt\\\",\\\"r\\\"). The real prototype is FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *mode); the quiz drops \\\"char\\\" from the first argument, but (a) is the only option with the right name, both arguments and the right return type.\",\"ow\":[null,\"No MODE: fopen always needs one so it knows whether to read, write or append (L1).\",\"Wrong name: the C library function is fopen; open is the C++ stream method (infile.open(...)) or a low-level system call, neither returning FILE * (L1).\",\"Wrong name, no mode, and it returns FILE by value instead of a FILE * pointer (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"An internal set of hard disk component containing arms which contain the read/write head\",\"o\":[\"Track\",\"Arm assembly\",\"Spindle\",\"Platter\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"L2: the arm assembly is \\\"an internal set of hard disk components containing the arms, which contain the read/write heads\\\". Its role is to read and write information from the platters; when it stops working, the drive fails.\",\"ow\":[\"A track is a ring on a platter surface where data is stored, part of the layout, not a set of components (L2).\",null,\"The spindle holds and rotates the platters (L2).\",\"The platter is the circular magnetic disk that stores the data (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"It's time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder\",\"o\":[\"Revolution time\",\"Transfer time\",\"Seek Time\",\"Rotational delay (or latency)\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"L2: seek time is the time to move the read/write arm to the correct cylinder. It is the first of the three access-time components and usually the slowest.\",\"ow\":[\"Revolution time is one full turn of the disk (e.g. 12 ms at 5000 rpm); no arm movement (L2).\",\"Transfer time is the head passing over the block, after the arm is in place and the sector has arrived (L2).\",null,\"Rotational delay is waiting for the disk to spin the sector under the head, after the seek (L2).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not used as a file opening mode?\",\"o\":[\"ios::in\",\"ios::trunc\",\"ios::binary\",\"ios::ate\"],\"a\":1,\"key\":\"No official key, and the question is flawed: all four are in the L1 flag list. ios::trunc is the standard answer of the MCQ bank this question comes from.\",\"why\":\"Strictly all four are flags you can pass when opening a C++ stream (L1 lists ios::in, out, app, ate, trunc, binary). The question comes from a well-known C++ MCQ bank whose answer is ios::trunc: it does not decide HOW you access the file (read, write, binary, position); it only truncates an existing file to zero length on opening (\\\"always create new\\\").\",\"ow\":[\"ios::in opens the file for input (reading): an opening mode (L1).\",null,\"ios::binary opens the file in binary instead of text mode: an opening mode (L1).\",\"ios::ate opens the file positioned at the end: an opening mode (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"The average cost for the insert process of the sorted file is ..................\",\"o\":[\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2*( 0.5B(D + RC) )\",\"Dlog2B + Clog2R + 2D + C\",\"1.25B(D + RC)\",\"2D +C.\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L4 cost table: sorted-file insert = delete = search + 2·(0.5B(D+RC)). Binary search for the place (D·log₂B + C·log₂R), then every record with a greater key moves one place towards the end: half the file read and written back on average.\",\"ow\":[null,\"This puts the sorted search in front of the heap insert; it leaves out shifting half the file, and no operation in the table has it (L4).\",\"1.25B(D + RC) is the HASHED-file scan (L4, Cost table).\",\"2D + C is the HEAP insert: a heap just appends at the end, but a sorted file must keep its order (L4).\"]}]}]}],\"midterm\":{\"lecs\":[1,2,3,4],\"scope\":\"Up to heap and sorted files (L1–L4)\",\"sections\":[{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"Files, storage and organization\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Data structures</b> deal with data in <b>main memory</b>; <b>file structures</b> deal with data in <b>secondary storage</b> (files). Both = representation of data + operations for accessing it.\",\"<b>File</b> = a collection of data placed on <b>secondary, non-volatile</b> storage. Main memory (RAM): fast, small, expensive, volatile. Secondary storage: slow, large, cheap, stable. File storage is slow because it has <b>many moving parts</b>.\",\"Data processing = storage, organization, access (<b>sequential</b> or <b>direct</b>) and processing of data. <b>Logical structure</b>: how programmers see the file; <b>physical structure</b>: how it is stored on secondary storage.\",\"<b>Logical organization methods</b>: heap, indexing, hashing. <b>Physical organization methods</b>: organizing tracks by <b>sectors</b> or by <b>blocks</b>. File organization must provide <b>fast access time and good space utilization</b>.\",\"<b>File management</b>: preparing/formatting storage media, allocating storage space and addressing, managing free space. <b>File managers</b> (system software): the <b>OS</b> and the <b>DBMS</b>. Trap: \\\"fast access and good space utilization\\\" is what <i>organization</i> must provide, not a management task.\"]},{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"File I/O in C/C++\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Physical file</b>: bytes on disk with a physical name (A.txt), used <b>once</b>, when opening. <b>Logical file</b>: a channel from the program to the physical file; its logical name is a program variable (infile, fp), used <b>many times</b>. They are linked at the time of <b>opening</b>.\",\"Declaring and opening: C <code>FILE *fp; fp = fopen(\\\"A.txt\\\", \\\"w\\\");</code> (fp = logical name, fopen = method, \\\"A.txt\\\" = physical file, \\\"w\\\" = mode). C++: <code>fstream f; f.open(\\\"A.txt\\\", ios::out);</code>, the 2nd argument is an <b>integer</b> built with bitwise OR <code>|</code> of <code>ios</code> constants.\",\"C modes: <b>r</b> existing, read; <b>w</b> create or <b>truncate</b>, output; <b>a</b> create or <b>append</b>, output; <b>r+ w+ a+</b> the same for input and output; adding <b>b</b> = binary. C++ flags: in, out, <b>app</b> (seek to end before <i>each</i> write), <b>ate</b> (start at the end), trunc, binary.\",\"C mode ↔ C++ flags: r = in; w = out | trunc; a = out | app; r+ = out | in; w+ = out | in | trunc; a+ = out | in | app; …b = … | binary.\",\"<b>Closing</b> frees the logical name for another physical file (\\\"like hanging up the telephone\\\"): C <code>fclose(fp);</code>, C++ <code>f.close();</code>. Bytes go to a <b>buffer</b> and are sent as a <b>block</b>; if the program terminates abnormally without closing, <b>data may be lost</b>.\",\"Generic <code>Read(Source_file, Destination_addr, Size)</code> and <code>Write(Destination_file, Source_addr, Size)</code>. <code>fread/fwrite</code> arguments: (1) address of the variable, (2) element size in bytes, (3) number of elements, (4) logical file name. E.g. <code>fread(a,1,10,infile)</code> reads 10 characters (a char is 1 byte).\",\"Read functions (slide 30): <b>fgetc</b> File Get Character, <b>fgets</b> File Get String (at most n-1 chars), <b>fscanf</b> File Scan Format, <b>fread</b> File Read (a record). End of file: C, <code>fread</code> returns <b>0</b>; C++, <code>infile.fail()</code> or <code>infile.eof()</code> is true. Standard names need no open/close: stdout/cout, stdin/cin, stderr/cerr.\",\"Full methods: writeC uses <code>fputc</code>, readC <code>fgetc</code> + <code>putchar</code>, writeS <code>fputs</code> (stops on an empty line), readS <code>fgets</code> + <code>puts</code>, writeASCII <code>fprintf</code> (chars 32 to 127), readASCII <code>fscanf</code> until it returns <code>EOF</code>. The sample tests <code>if (my_file == NULL)</code> to see whether the file opened.\"]},{\"lec\":1,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks (Lecture 1)\",\"pts\":[\"MCQ: \\\"Files are managed by system software: ………\\\" → <b>Both OS and DBMS</b> (MIS is the distractor).\",\"MCQ: \\\"File organization methods must provide ………\\\" → <b>fast access time and good space utilization</b>; the distractors are the three file <i>management</i> tasks.\",\"Written: \\\"Mention the main differences between physical file and logical file\\\" (bytes on disk with a physical name, used once vs channel with a logical name, used many times).\",\"Code: write C/C++ file code with open, read, write and close (e.g. Model B: copy <code>src.txt</code> to <code>dest.txt</code> with one <code>read</code> and one <code>write</code>, no loops).\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"Storage devices and the disk\",\"pts\":[\"<b>DASD</b> (direct access): magnetic disks (hard, floppy) and optical disks (CD-ROM, CD-R, CD-RW, DVD). <b>SASD</b> (serial access): <b>magnetic tape</b>.\",\"Five disk components: <b>platters</b> (circular disks, data on both surfaces), <b>read/write heads</b> (one per surface, fly as close as 3 nm), <b>arm assembly</b> (holds the arms; if it fails the drive fails), <b>spindle</b> (holds the platters fixed and <b>rotates</b> them), <b>arms</b> (carry, guide and move the heads; moving them = <b>seeking</b>, the slowest part).\",\"Platter parts: <b>track</b> (a ring), <b>sector</b> (smallest addressable portion, usually <b>512 B</b>), <b>cluster</b> (fixed number of contiguous sectors, the OS file manager's view), <b>cylinder</b> (tracks above/below each other, read <b>without moving the arm</b>). Number of cylinders = tracks per surface; cylinder height = surfaces = 2 × platters.\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"Capacity and blocking calculations\",\"pts\":[\"Formulas: cluster = sectors/cluster × bytes/sector; track = sectors/track × bytes/sector; cylinder = cylinder height × track; drive = cylinders × cylinder. Example: 60 sectors/track, 512 B, height 14, 5000 tracks/surface → track 30,720 B, cylinder 430,080 B, drive 2,150,400,000 B, platters 7.\",\"File storage: file size = records × record size; then divide by sector, track or cylinder size. Records per track = track / record. A part-used track or cylinder still counts, so <b>round up</b> (90,000 × 256 B = 23,040,000 B → 750 tracks, 53.57 → 54 cylinders).\",\"<b>By sectors</b> (OS): slow access or <b>fragmentation</b> (space lost inside a sector, e.g. 300 B records in 512 B sectors). <b>By blocks</b> (DBMS): user-defined blocks (pages) holding an integral number of records; <b>blocking factor</b> = records per block; no record spans two blocks. Sub-blocks: <b>count</b> (bytes in block), <b>key</b> (optional, last record's key), <b>data</b>. Blocks have <b>more</b> non-data overhead (sub-blocks + inter-block gaps).\",\"Records per track with blocks: block = BF × record + overhead; blocks/track = <b>⌊track / block⌋</b> (floor: a block cannot be split); records/track = blocks × BF. Track 20,000 B, overhead 300 B, record 100 B: BF 10 → ⌊20,000/1300⌋ = 15 → <b>150</b>; BF 60 → ⌊20,000/6300⌋ = 3 → <b>180</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"Access time and the disk bottleneck\",\"pts\":[\"Access time = <b>seek time</b> (move the arm to the correct cylinder, the <b>largest</b> cost: 5 ms track-to-track, 30 ms average, 50 ms max) + <b>rotational delay</b> (sector rotates under the head) + <b>transfer time</b> (head passes over the data).\",\"Revolution = 60 × 1000 / RPM ms; latency min 0, max 1 revolution, <b>average = ½ revolution</b> (5000 rpm → 12 ms → 6 ms). Transfer = (bytes / bytes per track) × revolution = n sectors × revolution / sectors per track. If the average rotational delay is <b>given</b>, use it.\",\"Worked: 10,000 rpm, 170 sectors/track, seek 8 ms, latency 3 ms → transfer = 6/170 ≈ 0.035 ms, total = <b>11.035 ms</b>. Model A: 5000 rpm, 20 sectors/track, seek 7 ms, 3 sectors → 7 + 6 + 3 × 12/20 = <b>14.8 ms</b>.\",\"Disk bottleneck (processes are <b>disk-bound</b>: network and CPU wait for the disk): <b>multiprocessing</b>, <b>disk striping</b> (blocks on different drives, parallelism), <b>RAID</b> (Redundant Array of Independent Disks), <b>RAM disk</b> (memory simulates a disk, speed vs volatility), <b>disk cache</b> (check the cache first, a miss goes to the disk).\"]},{\"lec\":2,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks (Lecture 2)\",\"pts\":[\"Fill the blank: \\\"……… are used to carry, guide and move the read/write head\\\" → <b>Arms</b>; \\\"……… is the time to move the arm to the correct sector\\\" → <b>Seek time</b>; \\\"a cylinder can be accessed without ………\\\" → <b>moving the arm assembly</b>.\",\"Short calculations as MCQs: cluster size (4 × 512 = 2048 B, the track size 64 × 512 is a distractor) and average latency (5000 rpm → 6 ms).\",\"Q2 disk problem (every model): given records, sector, track, cylinder height, tracks/surface, overhead, rpm and seek, find average latency, cylinder size, records per cylinder, sectors or tracks to save the file (round up), records/track for a blocking factor, total disk size, and the average time to read 2, 3 or 4 sectors.\",\"Written: differences between seek time, rotational delay and transfer time; four techniques for \\\"network and CPU wait a long time for the disk\\\".\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"Files, records and stream files\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Text file</b>: a sequence of characters (e.g. C source code), viewable in a text editor. <b>Binary file</b>: a sequence of bytes (e.g. movie, music), needs a specialized program.\",\"<b>Input</b> files: the program reads from them. <b>Output</b> files: the program writes to them.\",\"<b>Field</b>: a data value, the <b>smallest unit of data with logical meaning</b> (has a name, type, length and value). <b>Record</b>: a <b>group of fields forming a logical unit</b>. Logical view: a file is a collection of records.\",\"Three file structures: <b>stream</b>, <b>field</b> and <b>record</b> structures.\",\"<b>Stream file</b>: a stream of bytes with <b>no added information</b>, e.g. <code>AmesMary123 Maple...</code>. Problem: <b>no way to get the information back</b> in the organized record format.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"Field and record structures (advantages and disadvantages)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Fixed length</b> (lengths 5, 7, 25 → <code>03818FOLK&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;FILE STRUCTURES</code>): + easy to read/store; − wastes space with padding.\",\"<b>Length indicator</b> at the start of each field (<code>0587359</code>...): + easy to jump ahead to the end of the field; − long fields (max size &gt; 256) need more than 1 byte for the length.\",\"<b>Delimiter</b> at the end of each field (<code>87359|CARROLL|</code>): + may waste less space than length-based; − must check every byte of the field against the delimiter.\",\"<b>Keyword = value</b> (<code>ISBN=87359 AU=CARROLL</code>): + fields are self-describing, allows missing fields; − wastes space with keywords.\",\"Record methods: (1) <b>fixed-length records with fixed-length fields</b>; (2) <b>fixed-length records with variable-length fields</b> (delimited or length-indicator fields, padded to the fixed record size); (3) <b>each record begins with a length indicator</b> (byte count): <code>3387359|CARROLL|ALICE IN WONDERLAND</code> → 5+1+7+1+19 = 33.\",\"<b>Fixed-length record</b>: + easy to jump to the i-th record; − wastes space with padding. <b>Variable-length record</b>: + saves space when record sizes are diverse; − cannot jump to the i-th record unless through an <b>index file</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"Keys, access and seeking\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Primary key</b>: uniquely identifies a record and does not change. <b>Secondary key</b>: used for searching, does not typically identify a record uniquely.\",\"<b>Sequential search</b>: read in order until a match, <b>O(n)</b>; good for pattern matching or files with few records. <b>Direct access</b>: seek straight to the record, <b>O(1)</b>, possible when we know the <b>RRN</b> (first record = RRN 0).\",\"Fixed-length records: <b>byte offset = RRN × record size</b>. E.g. 40-byte records, RRN 5 → 200.\",\"C: <code>fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int origin)</code>, origin <b>0 = beginning, 1 = current, 2 = end</b>. <code>fseek(infile,0L,0)</code> start, <code>fseek(infile,0L,2)</code> end, <code>fseek(infile,-10L,1)</code> back 10 bytes. Generic: <code>Seek(infile, 3030)</code> moves to byte 3030.\",\"C++: an fstream has two pointers: <code>seekg</code> moves the <b>get</b> (read) pointer, <code>seekp</code> the <b>put</b> (write) pointer; origins <code>ios::beg</code>, <code>ios::cur</code>, <code>ios::end</code>. <code>tellg</code> after <code>seekg(0, ios::end)</code> = file size (copy with no loops: one read + one write). Update by RRN: <code>file.seekp(RRN*sizeof(newItem), ios::beg)</code> then <code>write</code>.\"]},{\"lec\":3,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"Fill the blank: in a stream file structure, information is written as a stream of bytes containing ……… → <b>No added information</b> (Midterm Model C).\",\"Complexities: direct access → <b>O(1)</b> (Model B); sequential access → <b>O(n)</b> (Model A); binary search → <b>O(log₂ n)</b> (Model C).\",\"Essay: \\\"many ways of adding structure to files to maintain the identity of fields\\\": list the 4 field structures with advantages and disadvantages.\",\"Code (Quiz 3): copy a file with no loops (<code>seekg</code> end + <code>tellg</code>), and <code>UpdateRecord</code> by RRN in a fixed-length field, fixed-length record file.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"Heap and sorted files\",\"pts\":[\"A file organization defines the <b>record placement strategy</b> and how a record's <b>key value</b> relates to its <b>relative address</b>. Efficiency is measured by the <b>number of disk accesses</b>. <b>Sequential search</b> is O(n); <b>direct access</b> is O(1).\",\"<b>Unordered (heap, pile) file</b>: records in <b>order of entry</b>, regardless of key. Retrieve only by <b>linear search</b>. Insert <b>at the end</b> (one access to read the last page, one to write it back). Serious disadvantage: it <b>allows duplicates</b>. Delete: find the record, delete it <b>physically or logically</b>, write it back.\",\"<b>Ordered (sequential) file</b>: records in order of the <b>key value</b>. Insert goes into an exact position, so <b>all records with a greater key move one location towards the end</b>; delete and update need the opposite. Example: inserting (4, Ali, CS) into 1, 2, 3, 5 puts it at offset 60 and shifts Ahmed (5) to 80.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"The cost table (memorize it)\",\"pts\":[\"Symbols: <b>B</b> = data pages, <b>R</b> = records per page, <b>D</b> = time to read/write a page, <b>C</b> = time to process a record.\",\"<b>Heap file</b><br>• Scan: <b>B(D + RC)</b><br>• Equality search: <b>0.5B(D + RC)</b><br>• Range search: <b>B(D + RC)</b><br>• Insert: <b>2D + C</b><br>• Delete: <b>search + C + D</b>\",\"<b>Sorted file</b><br>• Scan: <b>B(D + RC)</b><br>• Equality search: <b>D·log₂B + C·log₂R</b><br>• Range search: <b>search + retrieve matches</b><br>• Insert: <b>search + 2 × (0.5B(D + RC))</b><br>• Delete: <b>search + 2 × (0.5B(D + RC))</b>\",\"<b>Hashed file</b> (from the same table)<br>• Scan: <b>1.25B(D + RC)</b>, because buckets are kept about 80% full<br>• Equality search: <b>H + D + 0.5RC</b><br>• Range search: <b>1.25B(D + RC)</b><br>• Insert and delete: <b>search + C + D</b>\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"Why each cost looks that way\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Heap equality search</b>: no order, so pages are read one by one; on average the record is halfway, so <b>0.5B</b> pages. If nothing matches, the whole file is scanned.\",\"<b>Heap insert 2D + C</b>: D to read the last page, C to add the record, D to write it back. No search.\",\"<b>Sorted equality search</b>: binary search over the pages (D·log₂B), then binary search inside the page (C·log₂R).\",\"<b>Sorted insert/delete</b>: after the search, later records shift, so half the file (<b>0.5B</b> pages) is read <b>and</b> written back, hence × 2. Exams expand it: <b>Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + B(D + RC)</b>.\"]},{\"lec\":4,\"h\":\"What the doctor asks\",\"pts\":[\"The 2026/27 midterm asked this table directly: heap scan, equality search and insert; sorted scan, equality search, insert and delete; and \\\"files of unordered records are called\\\" → <b>heap files</b>.\",\"The wrong choices are other rows of the table: <b>2D + C</b> is the heap insert, <b>1.25B(D + RC)</b> is the hashed scan, and <b>Dlog₂B + Clog₂R + 2D + C</b> matches no operation at all.\"]}]}}");
